hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"601","543 - And Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah and Umm Salamah: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be overtaken by dawn while in a state of major impurity, then he would fast."
"602","544 - And Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"603","545 - And Fahd narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"604","546 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdalah informed us, from Abu Salih, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"605","547 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Amir ibn Abi Umayyah, from Umm Salamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that also. He said: So Abu Hurayrah retracted his ruling. Thus this Abu Hurayrah also held that what Aishah and Umm Salamah narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter is preferable to what al-Fadl narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contradicting it, and Allah we ask for success."
"606","Chapter 81: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: When I forbid you something, refrain from it, and when I command you with a matter, do of it what you can. 548 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah informed me, they said: Abu Hurayrah used to narrate that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whatever I forbid you, avoid it, and whatever I command you, do of it what you can, for those before you perished only by their many questions and their differing over their prophets."
"607","549 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik informed us, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"608","550 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad and Malik narrated to us, from Abi al-Zinad, from Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"609","551 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abi Bakr, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"610","552 - Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab. Abu Ja'far said: And Abd al-Wahhab did not mention: from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the same as it."
"611","553 - Fahd narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, Abu Salih narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"612","554 - And Fahd narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith to determine the meaning by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between what he forbids, commanding its avoidance absolutely, and what he commands, making it according to what the commanded can do, not making it an absolute command as he made what he forbids absolute. We found that the things he forbids, those forbidden were able to do them, so he forbade them to do them henceforth. And we found that the things commanded to be done may be within their ability and may be beyond it, and they are charged therein only with what they can, as Allah, exalted is He, said: {Allah charges no soul except its capacity}, meaning its ability, and as He, exalted is He, said: {Allah charges no soul except what He has given it}, and as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in what follows."
"613","555 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: When we pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to hearing and obeying, he would say to us: As much as you can. And we shall mention regarding this meaning, later in this book, about the pledge of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people, how it was, with more than this, God willing. Since what they are commanded may be within their ability or beyond it, he said to them therein what is mentioned of his saying to them in these hadiths, for they know their own selves' strength for that or inability better. This in our view is the meaning in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between his command and his prohibition in these hadiths we mentioned, and Allah, exalted is He, knows best His intent therein, and we ask Him for success therein and in other than it."
"614","Chapter 82: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the man who instructed his sons that when he dies they burn him, then grind him, then scatter him in the wind on land and sea, and about Allah's forgiveness of him despite that. 556 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abu Na'amah al-Adawi informed us, Abu Hunaydah al-Bara ibn Nawfal narrated to us, from Walan al-Adawi, from Hudhayfah, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke one morning... then he mentioned a long hadith of the Day of Resurrection, then mentioned therein the intercession of the martyrs. He said: Then Allah says: I am the Most Merciful of the merciful; look into the Fire whether there is anyone who ever did any good. They find in the Fire a man, and it is said to him: Did you ever do any good? He says: No, except that I commanded my children: When I die, burn me with fire, then grind me until I am like kohl, then take me to the sea and scatter me in the wind, for by Allah, the Lord of the worlds will never overpower me to punish me, since I punished myself in this world for it. Allah, exalted is He, says to him: Why did you do this? He says: From fear of You. He says: Look at a kingdom of the greatest kingdom, for yours is its like and ten times it. We examined what is in this hadith of this testator's instruction to his sons to burn him, grind him till like kohl, and scatter him in the sea in the wind, and of his saying thereafter: By Allah, the Lord of the worlds will never overpower me. We held that possibly permissible under the law of that generation to which the testator belonged, as devotion to their Lord, exalted and mighty is He, out of fear of His punishment. And his saying: By Allah, the Lord of the worlds will never overpower me, is in our view - and Allah knows best - upon straitening, meaning: Allah will never straiten me to punish me by His straitening for what I did to myself in this world which I instructed you with, hoping His mercy and seeking His forgiveness, trusting Him and knowing His mercy, pardon and overlooking for even less than that deed. The proof is Allah's saying: {As for man, when his Lord tries him} up to {then He straitens his provision}, meaning narrows it, and His saying about His prophet Dhu al-Nun, Yunus (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {And Dhu al-Nun, when he went off angry and thought We would not straiten him}, meaning not narrow upon him, and His saying: {He extends provision for whom He wills and straitens}. So his saying: By Allah, the Lord of the worlds will never overpower me, means: He will never straiten me, for what I did to myself hoping His mercy and seeking forgiveness."
"615","557 - As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrating, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, he said: Abu Mas'ud al-Badri and Hudhayfah came to me while we three were walking with no one with us, and Abu Mas'ud said to Hudhayfah: O Abu Abdullah, did you hear him - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - narrate the hadith of the man who used to rob graves? He said: Yes, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There was a man among those before you who used to rob graves. When death came to him, he called his sons and said: O my sons, what kind of father was I to you? They said: A good father. He said: I ask you a question. They said: What is it? He said: When I die, burn me, then grind me with the hardest grinding you ever ground anything, then look for a windy day and scatter me in it, for if Allah overpowers me He will punish me. So Allah raised him and said: What made you do what you did? He said: Fear of You. So He forgave him by that. Abu Mas'ud said: And I heard it too."
"616","558 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from Hudhayfah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There was a man among those before you with an evil opinion of his deeds. When death came to him, he said to his family: When I die, burn me, then grind me, then scatter me in the sea, for if Allah overpowers me He will not forgive me. He said: Allah commanded the angels and they received his soul. He said: He said to him: What made you do what you did? He said: O Lord, I did it only from fear of You, O Allah. So Allah forgave him. What is in these two hadiths is: If Allah overpowers me, He will not forgive me. Its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is: If Allah straitens upon me, He will not forgive me."
"617","559 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us. And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father say: Qatadah narrated to us, from Uqbah ibn Abd al-Ghafir, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he mentioned a man among the predecessors - or he said among those who were - he mentioned a phrase meaning this: Allah gave him wealth and children, and when death came to him, he said to his sons: What kind of father was I to you? They said: A good father. He said: He never stored up any good with Allah. Qatadah explained it: He never saved up any good with Allah, and if He overpowers him He will punish him. He said: When I die, burn me until I become charcoal, then crush me - or he said: grind me fine - then when there is a stormy wind, scatter me in it. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He took their covenants upon that, and they did that. Allah said to him: Be, and he was, and he was a standing man. Allah said: O My servant, what made you do what you did? He said: O Lord, fear of You, or fright of You. He said: Nothing met him but that He had mercy on him. He said: And he said another time: Nothing met him but that He had mercy on him. He said: I narrated it to Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, and he said: I heard this from Salman, except that he added therein: He said: Then scatter me in the sea, or as he narrated. The meaning of these two hadiths is also like the meaning of the hadiths whose mention preceded in this chapter."
"618","560 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hijabi narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sadaqah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, raised: Cast half of me on the land and half of me in the sea. The land was summoned with what is in it, and the sea with what is in it. He said: What made you do what you did? He said: O Lord, fear of You. He said: Nothing met him but it. Ibn Abi Dawud told us: This hadith was with none but al-Hijabi."
"619","561 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, he informed him that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A servant transgressed against himself until death came to him, then he said to his family: When I die, burn me, then crush me, then scatter me in the wind in the sea. By Allah, if Allah overpowers me He will punish me with a punishment He punishes none of His creation with. He said: His family did that with him, so Allah, exalted is He, said to everything that took anything of him: Render what you took of him, and he was standing. Allah said: What made you do what you did? He said: Fear of You. He said: So He forgave him."
"620","562 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Kathir ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same as it."
"621","563 - And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad and Malik informed me, from Abi al-Zinad, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man who never did any good said to his family, when he dies: Burn him, then scatter half of him on the land and half of him in the sea. By Allah, if Allah overpowers him He will punish him with a punishment He punishes none of the worlds with. When he died they did so. Allah commanded the sea and it gathered what is in it, and commanded the land and it gathered what is in it. Then He said: Why did you do this? He said: From fear of You, O Lord, and You know best. So He forgave him."
"622","564 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"623","565 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Ajlan, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: A man who never did any good, when death came to him, said to his family: When I die, burn me with fire until I become ashes, then scatter me in the wind, half of me on the land and half of me in the sea. That was done with him. Allah commanded him and he was gathered, then He said: Why did you do this? He said: From fright of You, O Lord, and You know best. Allah said: I have forgiven you. The meanings of these hadiths were like the meanings of those we mentioned before them in this chapter, and this hadith was narrated with wordings other than those we narrated it with in this chapter."
"624","566 - As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There was a servant among Allah's servants whom Allah gave wealth and children, and he did not uphold Allah's religion at all. He remained until when part of his life had gone and part remained, he remembered and knew he had stored up no good with Allah. He called his sons and said: What kind of father do you know? They said: The best, O our father. He said: By Allah, I shall not leave with any of you wealth that is from me unless you take it or you do what I command you. He said: He took a covenant upon them, by my Lord. He said: Either not; when I die, take me and cast me in the fire until I am charred, then crush me, then scatter me in the wind, perhaps I shall elude Allah. He said: They did with him, by the Lord of Muhammad, when he died. He was brought in the best form he had been, and came before Allah, exalted is He, and He said: What made you choose the fire? He said: Fear of You, O Lord. He said: I hear you fearful, so he was turned to in repentance. What is in this hadith stands in place of what is in the first hadiths we mentioned of that testator's saying: If Allah overpowers me, perhaps I shall elude Allah. We found this in nothing narrated in this chapter except this hadith, and this hadith was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by only one man, Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah, grandfather of Bahz. Contradicting him therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Hudhayfah, Abu Mas'ud, Abu Sa'id, Salman and Abu Hurayrah. We counted what Hudhayfah narrated therein as other than what Abu Bakr narrated, though Hudhayfah's hadith narrated from him by Walan is from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because Hudhayfah in his hadith added to what is in the hadith of Abu Bakr the mention of robbing graves, and everyone who heard this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and narrated it - the six first ones and Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah - heard the same wording the six mentioned, for they narrated it from him at different times with agreeing wordings, which could only be by their preserving it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in those wordings. Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah heard it likewise, but it occurred to his heart that the meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by His overpowering is power, whose opposite in his view is to elude Him, whereas the Prophet's intent by overpowering was straitening. What Mu'awiyah brought was this meaning, and what the six first ones narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is preferable, especially as among them is al-Siddiq, one of the two after him whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded to be followed, and Allah grants success."
"625","Chapter 83: Explanation of the difficulty of the possible cause in which {You have nothing of the matter} was revealed. 567 - Bakkar narrated to us, Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the dawn prayer, when he raised his head from bowing, say: Our Lord, and to You is praise, in the last rak'ah, then say: O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so, supplicating against some of the hypocrites. He said: Then Allah revealed: {You have nothing of the matter}, the verse."
"626","568 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, my grandfather Sa'id narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate against men among the polytheists, naming them by name, until Allah revealed to him: {You have nothing of the matter}, the verse."
"627","569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Salamah ibn Raja narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his head from the last rak'ah, he would say: O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid, Salamah ibn Hisham, Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, and the weak among the believers. O Allah, tighten Your grip upon Mudar and make it years upon them like the years of Yusuf. O Allah, curse Lihyan, Ri'l, Dhakwan and Usayyah, who disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. Then Allah revealed: {You have nothing of the matter}. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never supplicated against anyone thereafter."
"628","570 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. And Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri Abu al-Hasan narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then they both concurred, saying: from Thabit, from Anas: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had his front tooth broken on the day of Uhud and was wounded, and he began to wipe the blood from his face saying: How shall a people prosper who wounded the face of their Prophet and broke his front tooth while he calls them? Then Allah revealed: {You have nothing of the matter}."
"629","571 - Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: On the day of Uhud his front tooth was broken and he was wounded in his face. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while wiping the blood from his face: How shall a people prosper who stained the face of their Prophet with blood while he calls them to their Lord? Then Allah revealed: {You have nothing of the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers}. We examined these reports to determine which of the two mentioned meanings this verse was revealed about. It was possible it was revealed at one time intending both causes mentioned in these reports, but we found that far-fetched, for the battle of Uhud was in year three, and the conquest of Makkah was in year eight, and the Prophet's supplication for those he supplicated for in his prayer was before the conquest of Makkah, so it is far-fetched that both causes said to occasion this verse occurred together at its revelation. It was possible it was revealed twice, once for the cause mentioned by Abdullah ibn Umar and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and once for the cause mentioned by Anas, but that is refuted, for had it been so it would exist twice in the Quran, as is found: {O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and hypocrites}, in two places, one in Surah Bara'ah (73) and the other in Surah al-Tahrim (9). Since that is not the case for the verse recited in these reports, this possibility is void too. It was possible it was revealed as Quran for one of the two causes mentioned in these reports, Allah knows which, then revealed afterwards for the other cause, not as Quran appended to what was revealed, but as Allah informing His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby that he has nothing of the matter, and matters belong to Allah alone, Who turns to whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. We found no possibility for these reports better than this, so it is the most deserving in our view of what was said about the possible revelation of the recited verse therein, and Allah we ask for success."
"630","Chapter 84: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Twelve thousand shall never be overcome from fewness. 572 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard Yunus ibn Yazid narrating, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of companions are four, the best of detachments are four hundred, the best of armies are four thousand, and twelve thousand shall never be overcome from fewness. In our view this hadith is among what Jarir ibn Hazim alone narrated from Yunus ibn Yazid with this chain; we know none who shared it with him, nor know any of al-Zuhri's companions who narrated it from al-Zuhri besides Yunus ibn Yazid."
"631","Except that Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb differed with us therein, mentioning that Uqayl ibn Khalid shared with Yunus ibn Yazid in this hadith with this chain, narrating it from al-Zuhri with this chain as Yunus ibn Yazid narrated it from him. 573 - And he mentioned to us regarding that what he mentioned that Muhammad ibn Sulayman, meaning Luwayn, informed him of, from Hibban ibn Ali, from Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of companions are four, the best of detachments are four hundred, the best of armies are four thousand. And he mentioned a phrase meaning: That twelve thousand shall not be defeated from fewness when they are patient and truthful. Then Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb said to us at that: And Hibban ibn Ali is not strong. Part of our proof against him therein, by Allah's guidance, was that Hibban ibn Ali only took this hadith from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Uqayl, as mentioned."
"632","574 - As Fahd narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Mindal and Hibban narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of companions are four, the best of detachments are four hundred, the best of armies are four thousand, and twelve thousand shall never be afflicted from fewness. So this hadith returned, from Hibban, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Uqayl, with its chain and text, and Hibban is not strong in his narration as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb mentioned, and likewise say the scholars of chains besides him, and Mindal his brother is in their view below him therein. Since that is so, the hadith returns to Yunus upon what Jarir ibn Hazim narrated from him, with no firm partner with him in narration therein. If an objector says: Did any besides Mindal and Hibban narrate this hadith from Uqayl? It is said to him: Yes, others besides them narrated it from Uqayl: al-Layth ibn Sa'd, whose trustworthiness regarding Uqayl and firmness and precision from him is beyond obscurity among the scholars of chains and their narrators."
"633","575 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Ibn Marzuq, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from his father, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, in its text only without its chain. So this hadith returns to Yunus ibn Yazid from the narration of Jarir, connected, and to Uqayl from the narration of al-Layth from him, severed. Then we examined what is in this hadith of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Twelve thousand shall never be afflicted from fewness. We found Allah's obligation upon His servants had been that twenty patient ones not flee from two hundred, by His saying: {O Prophet, urge the believers}, the verse, so the obligation therein was that a people not flee from ten times their number. Then Allah lightened that upon them as mercy, revealing: {Now Allah has lightened for you, knowing there is weakness in you}, the verse, so the obligation therein became that they not flee from twice their number, and that was absolute in small and large numbers. Then Allah specified, upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), twelve thousand, as He specified them, that they not flee from numbers above them, and informed upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they shall never be afflicted from fewness. Thus went Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in his large book of campaigns, stating it therein without narrating any disagreement between him and any of his companions, and thus more than one of the scholars took commanding right and forbidding wrong upon this very meaning, among them Ibn Shubrumah Abdullah al-Dabbi."
"634","As Ishaq ibn Isma'il ibn Abd al-A'la al-Ayli Abu Ya'qub wrote to me, narrating to me, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, that he narrated to him, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: If a man flees from two men he has fled, and if he flees from three he has not fled. Sufyan said: I narrated it to Ibn Shubrumah, and he said: So is commanding right and forbidding wrong. This too was absolute with Ibn Shubrumah in all numbers, and there was narrated from Malik therein what shows his doctrine was like what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas we narrated, of distinguishing between twelve thousand and numbers below it."
"635","As I heard Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Fulayh ibn Sulayman al-Khuza'i Abu Abdullah mention that al-Umari the worshipper - Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab - came to Malik and said to him: O Abu Abdullah, we see these rulings that have been altered; does refraining from fighting those who altered them avail us? Malik said to him: If you have with you twelve thousand like you, refraining is not allowed you; and if you do not have this number of your likes with you, you are excused from refraining. This answer of Malik was the finest answer, and we hold - and Allah knows best - he took it from the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ibn Abbas we narrated: Twelve thousand shall never be afflicted from fewness, and Allah grants success."
"636","Chapter 85: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the mosques to which alone journeys are undertaken, and of the excellence of prayer therein over other mosques, and whether they are equal therein or some excel others. 576 - al-Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz al-Uwaysi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best to which mounts are ridden are the mosque of Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the mosque of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He did not mention in his hadith other than this."
"637","577 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Qaza'ah, from Abu Sa'id, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Journeys are not undertaken except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the Furthest Mosque, and this mosque of mine."
"638","578 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Qaza'ah al-Uqayli, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Burdens are not bound except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis. Abu Ja'far said: The mention of the third mosque dropped from the hadith."
"639","579 - Muhammad ibn Sinan ibn Sarj al-Shayzari Abu Ja'far narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from Qaza'ah, from Abdullah ibn Amr and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Journeys are not undertaken except to three mosques: the Furthest Mosque, this mosque of mine, and the Sacred Mosque."
"640","580 - Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Basrah ibn Abi Basrah al-Ghifari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Mounts are not worked except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, my mosque, and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis."
"641","581 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had... then he mentioned with its chain the same as it, except that he said: And the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis, or the mosque of Iliya, he doubted."
"642","582 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that he went out to al-Tur and prayed therein, then came back and met Humayl ibn Basrah al-Ghifari, who said to him: Where have you come from? He said: From al-Tur. He said: Had I met you before you went to it, you would not have gone to it. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The livers of mounts are not struck except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of mine, and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis."
"643","583 - al-Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ibn al-Had and Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us, that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to them both, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Basrah ibn Abi Basrah al-Ghifari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Mounts are not worked except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, my mosque, and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis."
"644","584 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Kathir al-Ansari informed us, Zayd ibn Aslam informed us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: I came to al-Tur and prayed therein, then I met Humayl ibn Basrah al-Ghifari, who said: Where have you come from? I told him, and he said: Had I met you before you went to it, you would not have gone to it. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Mounts are not struck except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of mine, and the mosque of Iliya."
"645","585 - Yahya narrated to us, Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Mutarrif informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, then he mentioned with its chain the same as it. Yahya told us: Sa'id ibn Ufayr said: He is Humayl ibn Basrah ibn Waqqas ibn Hajib ibn Ghifar."
"646","586 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, Abu Salamah narrated to me, Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: I met Abu Basrah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to me: Where have you come from? I said: From al-Tur, where Allah spoke to Musa. He said to him: Had I met you before you went, I would have restrained you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Journeys are not undertaken except to three mosques: the mosque of the Haram, the Furthest Mosque, and my mosque in Madinah."
"647","587 - Fahd narrated to us, Ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Journeying is only to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of yours, and the mosque of Iliya."
"648","588 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the same as it."
"649","589 - Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Basrah ibn Abi Basrah al-Ghifari, from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Mounts are not worked except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, my mosque, and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis."
"650","590 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had, the same as it."
"651","591 - al-Jizi narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ibn al-Had and Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Basrah ibn Abi Basrah al-Ghifari, similar to it."
"652","592 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Abdullah al-Kurayzi narrated to us, Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the same as it."
"653","593 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah and Ibn al-Musayyib, that Abu Hurayrah used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Journeying is only to three mosques... then he mentioned the same as it."
"654","594 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Amr al-Ash'athi narrated to us, Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan al-Hadrami, from Abi al-Ja'd al-Damri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the same as it."
"655","595 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the same as it. We thus understood that journeys are undertaken only to these three mosques, to the exclusion of other mosques. We needed to know the excellence of prayers therein over prayers in other mosques, and whether these three mosques are equal therein or excel one another. So we examined that:"
"656","596 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri. And we found Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques, except the Sacred Mosque."
"657","And al-Saqati told us, and al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan said: And Ziyad ibn Sa'd Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Atiq narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: A prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques. Sufyan said: So we hold that prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques, except the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for its excellence over it is only a hundred prayers."
"658","597 - And we found Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Habib al-Mu'allim, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques, except the Sacred Mosque, and a prayer in that is better than a hundred prayers in this."
"659","598 - And we found Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Makhlad al-Isbahani Abu al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab and Abu Kamil narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Habib al-Mu'allim... then he mentioned with its chain the same as it."
"660","599 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it, except the Sacred Mosque, and a prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in other than it. Abu Ja'far said: As though he means his mosque (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"661","600 - And we found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Husayn, from Muhammad ibn Talhah, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it, except the Sacred Mosque."
"662","601 - And we found al-Rabi' al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Abu Hurayrah, from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it, except the Sacred Mosque."
"663","602 - Musa said: And Abu Abdullah narrated to me this hadith, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the same as it."
"664","603 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth informed me, from Nafi', he narrated to him, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Ma'bad ibn Abbas, that he said: A woman complained of an illness and said: If Allah heals me I shall go out and pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. She recovered, then prepared to go out, and came to Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to greet her, and told her that. She said: Sit and eat what you prepared, and pray in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for I heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A prayer therein is better than a thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques, except the mosque of the Ka'bah."
"665","604 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz - or from Abdullah ibn Ibrahim ibn Qariz, Yahya doubted - from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the same as it."
"666","605 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Salman al-Agharr narrated to us that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the same as it."
"667","606 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Rabah and Ubaydullah ibn Abi Abdullah, from Abi Abdullah al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the same as it."
"668","607 - And we found al-Rabi' al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn al-Arqam, that he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said to me: Where do you intend? I said: To Bayt al-Maqdis. He said: For trade? I said: No, but I intended to pray therein. He said: A prayer here - meaning Madinah - is better than a thousand prayers there - meaning Iliya. We thus understood that the best of them for prayer therein is the Sacred Mosque, and that prayer therein is like a hundred thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques besides these three mentioned in these reports. Then we sought to determine the excellence of prayer in the Furthest Mosque over what is besides these three mosques, and we found the apparent import of what we narrated about the excellence of prayer in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicates no excellence for prayer therein over other than the three mosques mentioned in these reports. Then we looked into the rest of the reports whether we find therein anything of that."
"669","608 - And we found al-Layth ibn Abdah ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi Abu al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Asad al-Khushshi narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Is prayer in your mosque better or prayer in Bayt al-Maqdis? He said: Prayer in my mosque is like four prayers in the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis, and an excellent place of prayer it is; it is the land of the gathering and the resurrection. Then we sought to determine the standing of Sa'id ibn Bashir in narration, and we found Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: I heard Baqiyyah say: I asked Shu'bah about Sa'id ibn Bashir, and he said: He is truthful. Abu Zur'ah told us: And I asked Ahmad ibn Hanbal about him and he said: Trustworthy; our shaykhs Waki' and Ibn Mahdi narrated from him. So what is in this hadith indicates that prayer in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is like two hundred and fifty prayers in the Furthest Mosque."
"670","609 - And we found Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Bashir Abu al-Hasan al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Adami Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Salim al-Qaddah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Bashir, from Isma'il ibn Ubaydullah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abi al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The excellence of prayer in the Sacred Mosque over other than it is a hundred thousand prayers, and in my mosque a thousand prayers, and in the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis five hundred prayers. So in this is that prayer in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is like two prayers, meaning in Bayt al-Maqdis."
"671","610 - And we found Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Isa - who is Ibn Yunus - narrated to us, from Thawr - who is Ibn Yazid - from Ziyad - who is Ibn Abi Sawdah - from his brother, from Maymunah, freedwoman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she asked him: Give us a ruling about Bayt al-Maqdis? He said: The land of the gathering and the resurrection; go to it and pray therein, for a prayer therein is like a thousand prayers in other than it. She said: What if I cannot bear the journey to it? He said: Then let her gift oil to be lit therein, and whoever does that is like one who came to it."
"672","611 - And we found Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih, secretary of al-Layth, narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from Maymunah... the same as it, and he did not mention his brother."
"673","612 - And we found Fahd and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Ibn Salih narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Ziyad, from Maymunah... and she is not Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then they mentioned the same as it, except that they said: Prayer therein is like a thousand prayers, and they did not say: in other than it. So what is in this hadith is that the excellence of prayer in the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis is like its excellence in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We thus established that some of these reports we mentioned in the last section of this chapter abrogated others. Then we sought to verify them, and what is the abrogating therein and the abrogated? Our doctrine in abrogation in such is that it is from Allah, exalted is He, as mercy to His servants, and increase for them in His bounty and mercy. It thus follows that the first rulings therein were upon what is in the reports narrated about the excellence of prayer in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over other mosques besides the Sacred Mosque, and that it is like prayer in any mosque besides the three mosques mentioned in the first reports of this chapter. Then Allah, exalted is He, increased for one who comes to it and prays therein what Abu Dharr narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein. Then Allah increased therein, making it like five hundred prayers in what is besides these three mosques. Then Allah increased therein, making prayer therein like a thousand prayers in other than it of the mosques, besides these three mosques, and making it like prayer in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah knows best His intent therein."
"674","Chapter 86: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the prayer that has this excellence we mentioned in the first chapter: is it of the obligatory or the voluntary prayers? 613 - Ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Affan narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: I heard Aba al-Nadr narrating from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) partitioned a chamber in the mosque of mats, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed therein nights until people gathered to him. Then they missed his voice and thought he had slept, so some began to clear their throats for him to come out to them. He said: What I saw of your doing continued with you until I feared the night prayer would be prescribed upon you. Pray, O people, in your houses, for the best prayer of a man is in his house, except the prescribed prayer."
"675","614 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Abi al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Thabit al-Ansari, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) partitioned a chamber in the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come out at night to pray therein, and men behind him would hear him praying, so they prayed with his prayer. They would come to him every night, until one night the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not come out to them. They cleared their throats, raised their voices and pelted his door. He came out to them angry and said: Your doing continued with you until I thought it would be prescribed. Pray in your houses, for the best prayer of a man is in his house, except this prescribed prayer."
"676","Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abi al-Nadr, from Busr, that Zayd ibn Thabit said: The best prayer is your prayer in your houses, except the congregational prayer. Malik did not raise it. In this hadith of Zayd is the preference of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for voluntary prayers in houses over them in mosques. His address thereby to those he addressed was that their prayers in their dwellings are better than their prayers in his mosque, besides the prescribed prayers. We thus understood they are likewise in the Sacred Mosque and in the Furthest Mosque. In this hadith is jurisprudence deciding among the jurists in what they differed about a man who obligates upon himself to Allah to pray a voluntary prayer in one of the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or the Furthest Mosque, then prays it in his house: does it suffice him or not? Among those who said it suffices: Abu Hanifah and Muhammad. Many of the scholars opposed them, saying: It does not suffice him. Both views were narrated from Abu Yusuf. The correct therein in our view - and Allah knows best - is that it suffices him, for he prayed it in a place where praying it is better than praying it in the place he obligated upon himself to pray it therein to Allah, exalted is He. Only what is devotion to Allah, exalted is He, is obligatory of vows and self-impositions, and Allah we ask for success."
"677","Chapter 87: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever breaks a bone or lames has exited ihram, and another Hajj is upon him. 615 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from al-Hajjaj ibn Amr al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever lames or breaks a bone has exited ihram, and another Hajj is upon him."
"678","616 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, al-Sawwaf informed me, Yahya informed me, from Ikrimah, from al-Hajjaj, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say.... then he mentioned the same as it and added, he said: I narrated that to Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, and they said: He spoke the truth."
"679","617 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Yahya al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya, from Ikrimah, he said: Abdullah ibn Rafi', freedman of Umm Salamah, said: I asked al-Hajjaj ibn Amr about one who is detained while in ihram? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lames or breaks a bone has exited ihram, and another Hajj is upon him. He said: I narrated that to Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayrah and they said: He spoke the truth. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when one who breaks or lames is in one of two cases: either detained thereby or not detained by it? If detained, the ruling of the detained is as Allah, exalted is He, said: {But if you are detained, then whatever is feasible of the offering}, up to {or a rite}. And if thereby not detained, he remains upon his sanctity and exits nothing of it. So this hadith is opposed by all the scholars. Our answer was that this hadith is not opposed by all the scholars as mentioned, since the scholars are of two doctrines about the detention that has the ruling of the detention mentioned in the Book of Allah, exalted is He. One is that such detention is by every preventer preventing proceeding to the House. Among those who held that: Ibn Mas'ud, Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr."
"680","As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: A man of Nakha' in ihram for Umrah called Umayr ibn Sa'id was stung. While he lay fallen on the road, a party came upon them containing Ibn Mas'ud, and they asked him. He said: Send the offering and set between you and it a marked day. When that comes, let him exit ihram. al-Hakam said: And Umarah ibn Umayr said: And sufficient for you from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid that Ibn Mas'ud said: And Umrah is upon him next year. Shu'bah said: And I heard Sulayman, meaning al-A'mash, narrate it the same as al-Hakam narrated it exactly."
"681","And as Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya Abu Shurayh and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: {But if you are detained}, he said: By confinement or illness. Ibrahim said: I narrated it to Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and he said: Thus said Ibn Abbas."
"682","And as Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami and Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr preaching, saying: O people, by Allah, enjoyment of Umrah with Hajj is not as you do; rather enjoyment of Umrah with Hajj is that a man sets out for Hajj, then an enemy, illness or an excusable matter detains him until the days of Hajj pass - or he said: the days of Hajj elapse, Ishaq doubted - then he comes to the House, circumambulates it, runs between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and enjoys his release until the next year, then performs Hajj and offers a sacrifice. This is one of the two doctrines. The other doctrine is that such detention is only by the enemy specifically. Then the scholars thereafter: a group upon the first doctrine, among them Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri and the rest of the Kufan jurists; and a group upon the second doctrine, among them Malik, al-Shafi'i and the rest of the Hijazi jurists. So in what we mentioned is that the hadith we narrated at the beginning of this chapter is not, as this objector mentioned, opposed by all the scholars. This objector said: What then is the meaning of the phrase therein: he has exited ihram, when they all do not say he exits except for a meaning in language after that, of what I mentioned in this chapter? Our answer was: That phrase is sound Arabic speech. Its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is: It has become lawful for him to exit by what he exits with from the ihram he is in, as is said of a woman when divorced after consummation and her waiting period ends: She has become lawful for husbands - not meaning she has become lawful to them like their wives under their marriage contracts, but she has become lawful to them by marriage with a new contract upon her until she returns thereafter lawful to them like their wives under their marriage contracts - until that reaches Allah's saying, exalted is His praise: {But if he divorces her, she is not lawful to him thereafter until she marries another husband}. It is not that when she marries another husband she returns lawful to him, but she returns to a state in which it is lawful for him to newly contract marriage upon her until she is lawful to him. Likewise his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever breaks or lames has exited ihram, is not that he has exited with an exit leaving his sanctity, but it is a cause of exit for him to do a deed by which he leaves his sanctity. So by what we mentioned returns what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), finding therein no impossibility nor departure from the views of all the scholars."
"683","Chapter 88: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prohibition of the earnings of slavewomen. 618 - Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us. And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Abi Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the earnings of slavewomen."
"684","619 - And Ibn Khuzaymah, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah... then they mentioned with its chain the same as it. Then an objector said: How may you accept this from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Book of Allah, exalted is He, and the Sunnah of His Messenger refute it? Allah, exalted is He, said: {And those who seek writing, of those your right hands possess, write for them if you know any good in them}. There is no disagreement among all the scholars that what is sought from contracted slaves by the contracts concluded upon them is their earning, and that slavewomen among them are like males. Barirah was contracted in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the wealth she was contracted upon, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon that and did not denounce it. In that is refutation of what you claimed of the hadith you narrated. Part of our proof against him therein, by Allah's guidance, was: That which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in the hadith we narrated is other than what Allah, exalted is He, permitted in His Book and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his Sunnah, of contracting slavewomen. For Allah, exalted is He, only permitted contracting one in whom his contractor knows good, by His saying: {If you know any good in them}. Some said: The good is earning wealth; and some said: It is righteousness. Each interpretation confirms the other. This shows He only permitted contracting one whose earning is praised, not one whose earning is blamed. And that which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in the hadith we narrated, we understood by his forbidding it to us that it is of the denounced things, for his attribute by which Allah described him is commanding right and forbidding wrong, and among that is Allah's saying: {Those who follow the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet}, up to {and he forbids them the wrong}. We thus understood by his forbidding the earning of those forbidden in the hadith we narrated that it is the blamed earning, not the praised earning. He said: May prohibition be annexed to all earnings when only some of them is intended? Our answer was: When things are many and numerous, it is allowed to annex to all of them what is intended of some to the exclusion of the rest. Among that is Allah's saying to His Prophet in His Book: {And your people denied it}, intending not all his people, but the deniers among them to the exclusion of the believers therein; and His saying to him: {And it is a reminder for you and your people}, intending not his denying people thereby, but his believing people. Similar is what was from him in his Qunut in the dawn prayer, of his saying therein: O Allah, tighten Your grip upon Mudar, and make it years upon them like the years of Yusuf."
"685","620 - al-Muzani narrated it to us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah."
"686","621 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id and Abi Salamah, that they heard Abu Hurayrah say that also. He did not intend by his saying: O Allah, tighten Your grip upon Mudar, all of Mudar - how could that be when he is of Mudar, and the best of those behind him in that prayer are of Mudar, who have no equals - but his saying upon Mudar intended the Mudar opposing him, for whose opposition his Qunut was, to the exclusion of other Mudar. Likewise his prohibition (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the earnings of slavewomen: they are the slavewomen whose earnings are blamed, not the slavewomen whose earnings are praised. He clarified that in a hadith narrated from him by Abu Hurayrah."
"687","622 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to me, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the earning of the slavewoman, except that she have an established craft or a known earning. This shows that the earning included in his prohibition in the first hadith is the prohibition he prohibited in this hadith. Likewise was from Uthman ibn Affan in his sermon to the people."
"688","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him. And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from his uncle Abi Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father, that he heard Uthman preaching, saying: Do not impose earning upon the slavewoman without a craft, for when you impose that upon her she earns by her private part; and do not impose earning upon the minor, for if he finds nothing he steals; and be chaste when Allah, mighty and majestic is He, has made you chaste; and take of foods what is good."
"689","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Abi Suhayl, from his father, he said: I heard Uthman preaching... then he mentioned the same as it. This sermon of his was before the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who had heard from him his prohibition of the earnings of slavewomen, yet they did not reject it upon him nor oppose him therein. This shows their agreement with him thereupon, and that what they heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prohibiting the earnings of slavewomen is only the blamed thereof, not the praised thereof."
"690","Chapter 89: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the people's rows behind him for prayer, and his standing among them in the place of one leading them, and his mentioning thereafter that he was in a state of major impurity, and his gesturing to them: remain as you are, until he came to them bathed with his head dripping water: was that from him after he had said the takbir for prayer or before his takbir for it? 623 - Bakkar narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal and Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us - and the wording is Abu Umar's - from Ziyad al-A'lam, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered into the dawn prayer, then gestured to them: remain in your place. Then he came with his head dripping water and led them in prayer."
"691","624 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Sa'id - meaning Ibn Abi Arubah - from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered into a prayer, and said the takbir and we said the takbir with him. Then he gestured to the people: remain as you are. We remained standing until he came to us bathed, with his head dripping water. Then an objector said: This hadith is outside the views of all the scholars, for there is no disagreement among them that one who says the takbir for prayer while in major impurity, forgetful of that, is not by his takbir for it entering into it. Our answer was: These two hadiths were narrated as we mentioned, from the two Companions from whom they were narrated. And there was narrated from other than them of the Companions that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at that time was his standing the standing of one praying, not an entry from him into prayer by his takbir."
"692","625 - As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to me, al-Zuhri narrated to me, Abu Salamah narrated to me, Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The prayer was established and the people arranged their rows. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out until he stood in his place, then remembered he had not bathed, and said: In your place. He left to his dwelling and bathed, then came out until he stood in his place with his head dripping water."
"693","626 - And as Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid and Abu al-Mughirah Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, al-Zuhri narrated to me, Abu Salamah narrated to me, Abu Hurayrah narrated to me... then he mentioned the same as it."
"694","627 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The prayer was established and the people arranged. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and when he was in his place of prayer he remembered he had not bathed, and said: In your places. Then he returned, bathed, came out with his head dripping."
"695","628 - And as Ibrahim also narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris ibn Laqit narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah... then he mentioned the same as it. So in this was what showed he had not entered into prayer, or upon his knowledge that he had not entered into prayer, by his saying to them: In your place. Although this - even if a discrepancy - is not from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from the narrations of his Companions of his deeds; the discrepancy is from their narrations, not from him. We answer for them with what equalizes their narrations and returns to what excuses them therein, namely we say: The meaning of the saying of Anas and Abu Bakrah in their hadith: then he entered into prayer, is upon the meaning of nearness of his entering into it, not the reality of his entering into it. This is allowed in language, even the Book of Allah, exalted is He, came with the like, Allah, exalted is He, said: {When you divorce women and they reach their term, retain them}, yet when they reach their term the ties between them and their divorcers are cut, so retaining them thereafter is impossible. Allah, exalted is He, clarified that in the other verse: {When you divorce women and they reach their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands}. This shows that after their terms end they are lawful to whoever wishes to marry them, proving His intent in the other verse by mentioning reaching the term is nearness of reaching the term, not its reality. Likewise the Muslims named the son of Ibrahim whom Allah commanded him to slaughter - either Isma'il or Ishaq (peace and blessings of Allah be upon them) - the sacrificed, though he was not slaughtered, but for his nearness to being slaughtered. Likewise what is in the two hadiths of Anas and Abu Bakrah of entering into prayer is upon this meaning too: nearness of entering into it, not reality of entering into it."
"696","Chapter 90: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: A judge does not judge between two while angry. 629 - Bakkar narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us. And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, he said: My father wrote to his son while he was in Sijistan: Do not judge between two while angry, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you judges between two while angry."
"697","630 - And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, that he wrote to his son that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A judge does not judge between two while angry."
"698","631 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zubayd al-Makki narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Qawwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Ibn Jurayj, from Sufyan, that Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the same as it. Then an objector said: How may you narrate this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when you narrate from him, in what was from him at the time of his judging between al-Zubayr and his Ansari opponent, of anger, when the Ansari provoked him by his saying to him that day before that: That he is the son of your aunt."
"699","632 - And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus and al-Layth informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, he narrated to him that Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, that he disputed with a man of the Ansar who had witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about channels of the Harrah with which they both watered palms. He said to the Ansari: Let the water pass. He refused him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Irrigate, O Zubayr, then send to your neighbor. The Ansari became angry and said: O Messenger of Allah, that he is the son of your aunt? The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color, then he said: O Zubayr, irrigate, then withhold the water until it reaches the wall. Ibn Wahb said, and it is the origin: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exhausted for al-Zubayr his right. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had before that pointed to al-Zubayr a view intending ease for him and the Ansari. When the Ansari provoked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he exhausted for al-Zubayr his right in plain judgment. Al-Zubayr said: I think this verse was revealed only about that: {But no, by your Lord, they do not believe until they make you judge in what disputes between them}, the verse, one of them adding over his companion in the story of the hadith."
"700","633 - And as Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Urwah, that he narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he narrated to him that a man of the Ansar disputed with al-Zubayr before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the channels of the Harrah with which they water the palms. He said to the Ansari: Let the water pass. He refused him, and they disputed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Irrigate, O Zubayr, then send to your neighbor. The Ansari became angry and said: O Messenger of Allah, that he is the son of your aunt?! The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color, then he said: O Zubayr, irrigate and withhold the water, then return to the wall. al-Zubayr said: By Allah, I think this verse was revealed only about that: {But no, by your Lord, they do not believe}."
"701","634 - And as Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri... then he mentioned with its chain the same as it. Our answer to him therein was: That which we narrated from Abu Bakrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is upon others than him of the judges, from fear for them of what anger transfers them to, from justice in judgment to its opposite. And that which is in the hadith of al-Zubayr is different from that, for it is about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in Allah's undertaking him, His protecting him and preserving his affairs, is unlike people in such. That was allowed for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he applied it, and was not allowed for others, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him it, as Abu Bakrah narrated it from him."
"702","Chapter 91: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him with the woman seeking refuge from him among his wives when she was brought to him. 635 - Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the woman who sought refuge from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Urwah narrated to me, from Aishah, that the daughter of al-Jawn, when she was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have sought refuge in a Refuge; join your family. al-Awza'i said: We hold that a man's saying to his wife: join your family, is a divorce."
"703","636 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Asad al-Khushshi narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: I asked al-Zuhri: Which of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought refuge from him? He said: Urwah informed me, from Aishah, that the daughter of al-Jawn al-Kilabiyyah, when she was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he drew near her, said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have sought refuge in a Refuge; join your family."
"704","637 - Ja'far ibn Sulayman ibn Muhammad al-Hashimi, then al-Nawfali, narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, Umar al-Mawsili narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Isa narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married al-Kilabiyyah. When she entered upon him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew near her, and she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have sought refuge in a Refuge; join your family. al-Zuhri said: She is Fatimah daughter of al-Dahhak ibn Sufyan. So in what we narrated is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the woman seeking refuge from him, when she disliked his place and sought separation: Join your family. That fell in the position of divorce, by his intending it (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), divorce occurred by it. And there was narrated in the hadith of Ka'b ibn Malik mentioning Allah's turning to him, that when the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him in the days the people avoided speaking to them, with his command to seclude himself from his wife, and that he said to him: Shall I divorce her? He said: No, but seclude yourself from her. He said: So I said to her: Join your family."
"705","638 - Yunus narrated it to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b informed me that Abdullah ibn Ka'b said: I heard Ka'b narrating the hadith of his repentance, and he mentioned therein this speech."
"706","639 - And Abdullah ibn Raja narrated it to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father... then he mentioned the same as it."
"707","640 - And Fahd narrated it to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul al-Kufi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Ka'b, from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b... then he mentioned the same as it. This shows that a man's saying to his wife: Join your family, is divorce when he intends thereby divorce, and is not divorce when he does not intend thereby divorce. And there was narrated what was from this woman to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to her at that, from another angle, with addition over what we narrated thereof in this chapter."
"708","641 - As Fahd and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman ibn al-Ghasil narrated to us, from Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd, from Abi Usayd, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we ended at a garden between two gardens, and we sat between them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sit here. He entered, and al-Jawniyyah had been brought, lodged in a house among palms: Umaymah daughter of al-Nu'man ibn Sharahil, with a companion of hers. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, he said: Give yourself to me. She said: Does a queen give herself to a commoner?! He inclined with his hand to place his hand upon her, and she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. He said: You have sought refuge in a Refuge. Then he came out to us and said: O Abu Usayd, clothe her with two garments of Raziqi linen and join her to her family."
"709","642 - And as Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman narrated to me, from Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd, from his father."
"710","And from Abbas ibn Sahl, from his father, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered palms of Banu Sa'idah, wherein was a woman of Kindah called Umaymah daughter of al-Nu'man ibn Sharahil, in a house. He said: Give your self to me. She said: Does a queen give herself to a commoner?! He struck with his hand toward her throat to calm her, and she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. He said: You have sought refuge in a Refuge, and he withheld his hand. Then he came out to us and said: O Abu Usayd, prepare her, join her, clothe her with two garments of Raziqi linen."
"711","643 - And as Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, Umar ibn al-Hakam narrated to me, he said: I heard Aba Usayd say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Baljawn, lodged her at al-Shawt beyond Dhubab in a fortress. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have brought her. He went out walking until he ended to her. He squatted and inclined to kiss her - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he married, would squat and kiss - and she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. He said to her: You have sought refuge in a Refuge, and he ordered me to return her to her family. In what we narrated in this chapter is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Abu Usayd to join this woman to her family, in the meaning of his commanding him to divorce her. Therein too is what needs examination: the carrying of this woman's load to him from her family, and returning her to her family from him with Abu Usayd, who is not of her unmarriageable kin by lineage, nor knew we between them suckling making him like the unmarriageable kin. What allowed him that in our view - and Allah knows best - therein is that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her, she thereby became a mother to the Muslims and thereby forbidden to them, so that became lawful for Abu Usayd therein, as he had by what we mentioned returned forbidden through her. Therein too is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him to prepare her or clothe her what he commanded him to clothe her or prepare her with. That in our view - and Allah knows best - possibly is enjoyment from him to her, for among the scholars were those who held for a divorcee before consummation, whether a dower was named for her or not, an enjoyment enjoined upon her divorcer, or taken for her thereby. Among those that is narrated from: Ali ibn Abi Talib, though most scholars are contrary to him about a divorcee before consummation with a named dower."
"712","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yahya ibn Ayyub and Musa ibn Ayyub informed me, from Iyas ibn Amir, from Ali, he said: For every divorcee is enjoyment. It may be that what he commanded for her of that was bounty from him to her, not as enjoyment from him to her as a divorcee is given enjoyment, and Allah knows best what He intended of that, and with Him is success."
"713","Chapter 92: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the woman he married, then when she was brought to him he saw whiteness on her flank, and what was from him in her affair thereafter. 644 - Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani Abu Yazid narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Jamil ibn Zayd al-Ta'i narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Ghifar and saw whiteness on her flank, so he released her. In this hadith is Jamil ibn Zayd narrating it from Ibn Umar, and Isma'il was opposed therein, narrating it from him, from other than Ibn Umar. We know none who agreed with Isma'il ibn Zakariyya therein from him except al-Qasim ibn Ghusn, for Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari mentioned from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti, from al-Qasim ibn Ghusn: He heard Jamil ibn Zayd, from Ibn Umar... then he mentioned the same exactly."
"714","645 - And therein is what Abu Imran Musa ibn al-Hasan ibn Abdullah al-Marwazi, known as al-Saqalli, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Warkani narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Ghusn narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Jamil - thus he said - from Ibn Umar: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Banu Ghifar. When he consummated with her he saw whiteness on her flank, withdrew from her and said: Let your garments upon you. Then he released her. As for those who opposed them therein from Jamil ibn Zayd, among them is Abbad ibn al-Awwam, he mentioned it from Jamil, he said: I heard Ka'b ibn Zayd al-Ansari."
"715","646 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, Jamil ibn Zayd al-Ta'i narrated to us, he said: I heard Ka'b ibn Zayd al-Ansari narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Ghifar and saw a spot on her flank, and said: Put your garments upon you and join your family. And among them is Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir, he narrated it from Jamil ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah."
"716","647 - As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Zayd al-Ta'i, from Zayd ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Banu Ghifar. When she was brought to him he saw whiteness on her flank, and said: Wear your garments and join your family, similar to it. Abu Mu'awiyah said, from a man, from Jamil, with this chain, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered for her the dower. And among them is Hafs ibn Ghiyath, who narrated it from Jamil, from Zayd ibn Ka'b."
"717","648 - As Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Qazwini Abu al-Qasim narrated to us, Abu Usamah Abdullah ibn Usamah al-Kalbi al-Kufi narrated to me, Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, from his father, from Jamil al-Ta'i, from Zayd ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told of a woman of Banu Ghifar and she was described, so he married her. When she was brought to him he saw what is in her - whiteness was on her flank - and disliked her, gave her enjoyment and said: Join your family. Then she was joined to her family. And among them is Muhammad ibn Abi Hafs, who narrated it from Jamil, from Zayd ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah."
"718","649 - As Abu Yazid Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani permitted me, from al-Mufaddal ibn Ghassan al-Ghallabi, that he narrated to him, he said: Ibn al-Himmani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Hafs narrated to us, Jamil ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Ghifar and consummated with her, finding whiteness on her flank. He said: Wear your garment, and gave her the dower and said: Join your family. So in this chapter is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the woman mentioned therein: Join your family. The discussion thereof is like the discussion of his saying to the woman seeking refuge from him mentioned before this chapter of this book: Join your family. And in this chapter is the giving of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the woman mentioned therein the dower. Then an objector said: In the hadith of Ibn Abi Hafs is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her enjoyment. It was said to him: This in our view does not contradict what is in this hadith of Ibn Abi Hafs, for it is allowed that he made her like one consummated with, by his seclusion and her enabling herself to him, and because his leaving was by his choice to touch her, not for other than it, so that stood from him in place of his touching her, though he did not touch her in reality. Then we sought to determine the states of this Muhammad ibn Abi Hafs: are they states requiring acceptance of the addition in his narration of this hadith over others who narrated it but fell short of mentioning the Prophet's command to that woman of the dower? We found al-Bukhari mentioned in his history this Muhammad ibn Abi Hafs, saying: He is Kufan; Abu Nu'aym heard from him."
"719","And Abu Ghassan narrated from him. Muhammad ibn Musa al-Hadrami mentioned to me that Abu Hafs Umar ibn Hafs ibn Aslam ibn Rashid al-Sakuni said - and he is from Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Imam who was with us here - he said: And this uncle of his was one of the trustworthy in Baghdad, that he narrated to him, he said: Abdullah - meaning Ibn Salih al-Ijli - narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar al-Attar narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Zayd al-Ta'i, from Sa'd ibn Zayd al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married a woman of Ghifar and consummated with her, commanding her to remove her garments. He saw whiteness of leprosy at her breast. In the morning he said: Take your garments and join your family, and he completed for her the dower. By what we mentioned we established the eminence of Muhammad ibn Abi Hafs in narration, by the narrations of authorities from him: from Abu Nu'aym, Abu Ghassan, Abdullah ibn Salih al-Ijli and Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani. Then we sought to determine Ka'b ibn Zayd, or Zayd ibn Ka'b, or Sa'd ibn Zayd: does he have Companionship or not? We found al-Bukhari in his history, when mentioning those named Ka'b among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioned among them: Ka'b ibn Amr Abu al-Yasar, Ka'b ibn Ujrah, Ka'b ibn Malik, Ka'b al-Ash'ari, Ka'b ibn Iyad, then Ka'b whose hand was cut on the day of Yamamah, then said: All these have Companionship. Then thereafter he mentioned Ka'b ibn Zayd, saying: And it is said: Zayd ibn Ka'b. Then after him he mentioned Ka'b ibn Mati', called al-Ahbar. That was proof of his including him among the Companions, or his nearness to them in his view. Since that is so, it is not far that this hadith is proof for one who says the dower is obligatory for one whose touching was possible but who divorced before touching, especially as a group of the eminent Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and of the rightly-guided caliphs went to that view, among them Umar and Ali."
"720","And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Sa'id - meaning Ibn Abi Arubah - narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, from Umar and Ali, they said: When he closes a door or lowers a curtain, she has the full dower and upon her is the waiting period."
"721","And thereby Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to me, and Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf, he said: Umar and Ali said: When the curtains are lowered and the doors are locked, the dower has become obligatory."
"722","And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar decreed regarding a woman a man marries: When he lowers the curtain, the dower has become obligatory for her."
"723","And as Fahd narrated to us, Ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Minhal, from Abbad ibn Abdullah, he said: Ali said: When the curtain is lowered and the door is closed, the dower has become obligatory."
"724","And as Bakkar narrated to us, Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Minhal, from Hayyan ibn Marthad, he said: Ali said: When the door is closed and the curtain is lowered, the dower has become obligatory."
"725","And as Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Awf - meaning al-A'rabi - informed us, he said: I heard Zurarah ibn Awfa in the mosque of Basrah say: The rightly-guided caliphs decreed that whoever closes a door or lowers a curtain, the dower has become obligatory and the waiting period has become obligatory. In this is addition over what preceded of what we narrated from Umar and Ali, including the rest of the rightly-guided caliphs in this view too. And there was narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit what shows he also held this doctrine."
"726","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abdurrahman ibn Abi az-Zinad informed me, from his father, Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me: that al-Harith ibn al-Hakam married a woman and entered upon her, and behold she was greenish-pale, so he disliked her, and he did not uncover her as he claims, and he was too shy to leave immediately, so he spoke while alone with her, then he went out and divorced her, and he said: she has half the dower and I did not uncover her, while she denied that to him, so the matter was raised to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, so he sent to Zayd ibn Thabit and said: O Abu Said, a righteous man, his affair was such-and-such, and he is just, is there upon him anything but half the dower? So Zayd ibn Thabit said to him: What do you think, if the woman now became pregnant, and she said: it is from him, would you carry out the hadd punishment upon her? So Marwan said: No, so Zayd ibn Thabit said: Rather she has her full dower."
"727","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid Abu Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Ibn Abi az-Zinad informed me, from his father, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, concerning a man who is alone with a woman and says: I did not touch her, while she says: he did touch me, he said: the statement is hers. So this Zayd ibn Thabit, his doctrine in that was as the doctrine of those we mentioned before him in this chapter. If a speaker says: that was only because of the woman's claim along with seclusion, of what she claimed of her husband's approaching her, it is said to him: if what you described were as you stated, her claim would not be accepted for what would impose upon him a liability that was not obligatory before that, while the one against whom it is claimed denies it, except with a proof imposing that upon him, and since she was not asked for a proof for that, the letting down of curtains and locking of doors and her giving her husband access to herself such that there is no preventer for him from her imposes the dower upon him, and by it he is in the ruling of one who touched her, even if he did not touch her. The statements of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that have been consecutively transmitted, and they agreed that the enabling we mentioned makes the one enabled like one who touched the woman who enabled him of herself, and we do not know any opponent to them besides them among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. If a speaker says: Rather Ibn Abbas opposed them in that."
"728","Then he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: When a man marries and tafwid is made to him, then he divorces before touching, she has nothing but muta provision. It is said to him: This is not in our view opposed to what we narrated before it concerning seclusion and enabling, from those we narrated them from in this chapter. And tafwid in our view mentioned in this hadith is tafwid to the husband in naming the dower for one who marries him off without a dower, so he does not do that, then he divorces before touching, so there is upon him nothing but muta, and it is not in our view concerning tafwid accompanied by seclusion nor enabling for intercourse. And when that is probable for what we mentioned, it is not in our view opposed to what we mentioned before it from those we mentioned in this chapter. If he says: the outward Quran indicates what we interpreted upon it from what was narrated from Ibn Abbas in this hadith, because Allah the Exalted said in His Book: {But if you divorce them before you have touched them and you have already appointed for them a dower, then half of what you appointed, unless they pardon or he pardons in whose hand is the marriage tie}, and it was understood by that that one who divorces and did not touch, what binds him by this verse is half the dower, not all of it. It is said to him: Those who said in this the obligation of the dower and the obligation of the waiting period are the Rightly Guided Caliphs Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali, and Zayd ibn Thabit joined them in that, and he is the scribe of the revelation and trusted with it, and the Quran came down in their language and they know its interpretation, and what was obscure to them of it they would ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about, so he would inform them of Allah the Exalted's intent by it, and in opposing them is declaring them ignorant and departing from their doctrines to what is besides them, from which we seek refuge with Allah, besides that we have found in the language what has permitted us to name one whom touching was enabled for but did not touch with the name of touching, as the son of Ibrahim, upon them both be peace, whether Ismail or Ishaq, was named dhabih, not because he was slaughtered, but when he enabled himself and his father enabled that of him by laying him on his forehead he was named by that dhabih though he was not slaughtered. So like that is what we mentioned of this woman's enabling herself to her husband for his intercourse with her until there was between him and that no barrier nor for him from it any preventer, it is permissible to apply upon him the name of one touching her, though he was not in reality touching her, and she enters thereby into the meaning of one divorced after touching, because he is in the meaning of one who divorces before it. And we have found what the Muslims have agreed upon, for they did not differ concerning one who sold something of his for the price of detaining it until he receives that price, then he was enabled to receive it and left between him and it, but he did not place his hand upon it nor take it, and destruction befell it, that it is destroyed from his wealth, not from his seller's wealth. And in that upon what we described is proof, along with the adherence of most jurists of the lands to this, among them: Abu Hanifa among his followers, and Malik among followers among his followers, and al-Layth among his followers, and al-Awzai among his followers, and ath-Thawri among his followers also, and Allah we ask for success."
"729","Chapter 93 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: For Allah does not tire until you tire. 650 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, my father informed me, from Aisha that the Prophet, upon him be peace, entered upon her while there was a woman with her, so he said: Who is this? So she said: So-and-so, she does not sleep, and something of her prayer was mentioned, so he said: Enough, upon you is what you can bear, for by Allah, Allah the Exalted does not tire until you tire, and the most beloved religion to Allah is what its doer persists upon."
"730","651 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Salama, from Aisha that the Prophet, upon him be peace, used to partition a mat at night and pray, and spread it by day and sit upon it, so people began gathering to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and praying with his prayer until they became many, so he turned to them and said: O people, take of deeds what you can bear, for Allah does not tire until you tire, and the most beloved deeds to Allah are what persists of them even if little."
"731","652 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Hajib ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Hiql ibn Ziyad as-Saksaki narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Abu Salama, from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: Take of deeds what you can bear, for Allah does not tire until you tire. She said: And the most beloved prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was what was persisted upon even if little, and when he prayed a prayer he persisted upon it. He said: And Abu Salama says: Allah says: {Those who are constant upon their prayer}. So a speaker said: How is it permissible for you to accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while in it is ascribing tiredness to Allah the Exalted in some state, and that is negated from Allah and is not of His attributes. So our answer to him in that was: that tiredness is negated from Allah as he mentioned, and what he imagined of what the interpretation of this hadith was carried upon is not as he imagined, rather it is with the people of knowledge of language upon the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah does not tire when you tire, since tiredness is imagined from you and not imagined from Him, Mighty and Majestic. And like that is speech current upon the tongues of people when they describe one they describe with strength in speech and eloquence in it and mastery of it: So-and-so does not break off from disputing his opponent until his opponent breaks off, they do not intend by that that he breaks off after his opponent's breaking off, for if they intended that they would not have affirmed a merit for the one they described, since he would break off right after his opponent's breaking off as his opponent broke off, but they intend that he does not break off after his opponent's breaking off as his opponent broke off from him, and that he remains of strength and mastery in his disputation after his opponent's breaking off from it as he was upon it before his opponent's breaking off from it. So like that, and Allah knows best, is the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah does not tire until you tire, and Allah does not tire until you tire, meaning you may tire and break off, while Allah after your tiring and breaking off is upon the state He was upon before that, of negation of tiredness and breaking off from Him, and with Allah is success."
"732","Chapter 94 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning Qutayla daughter of Qays whom he did not consummate with after marrying her until he died leaving her. 653 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abdulala ibn Abdulala narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas that the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, married Qutayla daughter of al-Ashath, thus Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith, rather she is the sister of al-Ashath, so he died before veiling her, so Allah the Exalted declared him clear of her. And there has been narrated concerning her affair by which Allah cleared His Messenger of her an addition upon this."
"733","654 - As Harun al-Asqalani permitted us from what he mentioned to us that al-Mufaddal al-Ghallabi narrated to him of it, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad, and he is Ibn al-Awwam, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Qutayla, then she apostatized with her people, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give her choice nor veil her, so Allah cleared him of her. Abbad said: meaning he did not veil her, he had not cast the veil upon her, and he did not give her choice as he gave choice to his wives. So in this hadith is an addition upon what is in the first, and in it is the apostasy of this Qutayla with her people from Islam, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not given her choice, meaning between the world and the Hereafter, as he gave choice to the rest of his wives besides her, so she would choose the world and he would part from her, or the Hereafter and he would keep her, and she would thereby be of his wives in it, and that the clearance that befell her was by her apostasy and by the absence of veiling and choice from her. And there has been narrated concerning her affair also from ash-Shabi."
"734","654M - What Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Dawud, from ash-Shabi that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Qutayla daughter of Qays and died leaving her, then Ikrima ibn Abi Jahl married her, so Abu Bakr wanted to kill him, so Umar said to him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not veil her, nor allot to her, nor consummate with her, and she apostatized with her brother from Islam and declared herself clear of Allah the Exalted and of His Messenger, so he kept at him until he left him. So in this hadith is that Abu Bakr wanted to kill Ikrima when he married this woman, because she was in his view of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who were made unlawful to people by the statement of Allah the Exalted: {And it is not for you to harm the Messenger of Allah} to the verse, and that Umar removed her from the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by her apostasy which was from her, since it was not fit for her with it to be a mother to the Muslims. And there has been narrated from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman concerning the reason by which it was made unlawful for the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to marry after him."
"735","What Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Dawud the author of at-Tayalisa narrated to us, Isa ibn Abdurrahman narrated to us, Abu Ishaq as-Sabii narrated to us, from Sila ibn Zufar who said: Hudhayfa said to his wife: If you wish to be my wife in Paradise, then do not marry after me, for the woman is for the last of her husbands, and for that Allah the Exalted made it unlawful for the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to marry after him."
"736","And what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Isa ibn Abdurrahman narrated to us, Abu Ishaq as-Sabii narrated to us, from Sila, from Hudhayfa... then he mentioned the like of it. And there has been narrated from Abu ad-Darda what indicates this meaning, as:"
"737","Fahd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abi az-Zahiriyya, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Umm ad-Darda that she said to Abu ad-Darda at death: You betrothed me to my parents in this world and they married me to you, and I betroth you to yourself in the Hereafter. He said: Then do not marry after me, so Muawiya betrothed her, so she informed him of what had been, so he said: Upon you is fasting. Besides that it has been narrated from Umar that he prevented this Qutayla from marrying, though he had removed her from the wives of the Prophet, upon him be peace, by what he removed her by of what we mentioned from him, as Muhammad ibn Abdulmalik ibn Zanjawayh mentioned from him, from Abdurrazzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulayka that he informed him, and Amr ibn Dinar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had married a woman of Kinda and did not consummate with her, then she married after the Prophet, upon him be peace, so Umar separated between them and beat her husband. So she said: Fear Allah concerning me, O Umar, if I am of the Mothers of the Believers then cast the veil upon me and give me like what you give them. He said: As for there, no. She said: Then let me marry. He said: No, nor any favor, nor do I give hope in that to anyone. So in this hadith is that Umar, though he had removed her from the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), yet prevented her from marrying other than the Prophet, upon him be peace. And in that is proof that the meaning by which he removed her from the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was her apostasy from Islam, not what is besides it of consummation with her and choice for her, because her apostasy was from Islam of her own deed, while choice for her and consummation with her were not of her deed, and that he only removed her by her deed, not by what is besides it. And in that is what indicated that he had not opposed Abu Bakr in the affair of Ikrima except in killing specifically, not in what is besides it, because he counted that a doubt that entered upon him, so he excused him by it and warded off killing from him because of it, not that he saw to affirm that woman with him and that she be a wife to him. And for that is a weighty aspect of knowledge, and it is that that woman had been before her apostasy from Islam of the wives of the Prophet, upon him be peace, deserving of the means which his wives deserve in his life and after his death, until she removed herself from that by her apostasy from Islam to what is besides it, so her rights were thereby nullified in what she argued with Umar about, while the rights that were upon her were not nullified, of leaving marriage to other than the Prophet, upon him be peace, after him, like the woman who is recalcitrant toward her husband, so her rights of maintenance upon her by the marriage between him and her are nullified. And likewise that woman, there was for her upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his marrying her rights, and there was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon her by it rights, so when apostasy was from her, her rights upon him which would have been for her upon him after his death were nullified by it, had that not been, of veiling her from people and spending upon her, while the rights of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon her remained after that as they were before it, among them that she is unlawful to people besides him. If a speaker says: We have seen the recalcitrant woman when she returns from her recalcitrance to what she was upon before it, she returns to her rights toward her husband which were for her upon him, and the Kindiyya mentioned has returned to Islam, for Ikrima had been a Muslim, and had she not returned to Islam he would not have sought to marry her, for the apostate woman is not lawful to the Muslim, so why did she not return after that to deserving what the wives of the Prophet, upon him be peace, deserve, of veiling them and spending upon them? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: is that the recalcitrant woman when she returns not recalcitrant deserves upon her husband what you mentioned, while the Kindiyya was not like that, for when apostasy from Islam was from her, she was in that state of those whom Allah the Exalted has barred from entering Paradise, and it was not fit for her with that to be a mother to the Muslims, and the rights of motherhood do not return after their removal, and when they do not return after their removal, the Kindiyya mentioned did not return to being a mother to the Muslims, and when she did not return to being a mother to the Muslims, she did not deserve in their wealth maintenance as the rest of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deserve the like of it by their motherhood of them, and with Allah is success."
"738","Chapter 95 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace: There is no emancipation nor divorce under ighlaq. 655 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi Abu Yaqub narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Shuja Abu Hammam narrated to us, Abdurrahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd who said: Adi ibn Adi al-Kindi sent me to Safiyya daughter of Shayba to ask her about things she used to narrate from Aisha, so she said: Aisha narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no emancipation nor divorce under ighlaq. And al-Bukhari mentioned this hadith, from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Sad ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad, from his father, from Ibn Ishaq, from Thawr ibn Yazid al-Kalai, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Abi Salih al-Makki, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. We intended by that the addition in this hadith in the lineage of Muhammad ibn Ubayd, that he is Ibn Abi Salih and that he is of the people of Mecca, though we have not heard mention of him in other than this hadith. Then we examined this hadith to ascertain what is intended by it. The best that occurred to us in it, and Allah knows best, is that ighlaq is closing tightly upon a thing, so it is probable thereby in our view that intended in this hadith by it is compulsion which closes upon the emancipator and upon the divorcer until emancipation and divorce come from him without choice from him for them, and he is not rewarded in emancipation as the rest of the emancipators are rewarded who intend Allah by their emancipation, nor like the divorcers whom sins befall in their divorce, those who place it in other than its place and those who effect of its number more than was permitted for them to effect of it. Its place which they were commanded to place it in is purity before touching, and the number they were commanded with is one, not what is above it. So a speaker said: To whose statement have you gone in imposing the divorce of the coerced, and to which hadith did you aim? So our answer to him in that was: that we went to a hadith which is better in isnad than this hadith, better known in men, and clearer in meaning."
"739","656 - And it is what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Abu Usama narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abdullah ibn Jumay, Abu at-Tufayl narrated to us, Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman narrated to us who said: Nothing prevented me from witnessing Badr except that I went out, I and my father, and the disbelievers of Quraysh seized us and said: You intend Muhammad, so we said: We intend nothing but Medina, so they took from us Allah's covenant and His pact that we would turn back to Medina and not fight with him. So we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, so he said: Turn back, we fulfill to them their covenant and seek Allah's aid against them."
"740","657 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-Walid, from Abi at-Tufayl, from Hudhayfa who said: I went out, I and my father Husayl, while we intended the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned it similarly. So in this hadith was what indicated that an oath under coercion binds as it binds upon willingness."
"741","Chapter 96 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: There is no divorce except after marriage, and no emancipation except after ownership. 658 - Umar ibn Abdulaziz ibn Imran ibn Ayyub ibn Miqlas al-Khuzai Abu Hafs narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Madani known as al-Jari narrated to me, Abu Shakir Abdullah ibn Khalid ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, from his father, from Said ibn Abdurrahman ibn Ruqaysh who said: I heard from paternal uncles of mine of Banu Amr ibn Awf and from my maternal uncle Abdullah ibn Abi Ahmad ibn Jahsh, from Ali ibn Abi Talib who said: I memorized for you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) six: There is no divorce except after marriage, and no emancipation except after ownership, and no orphanhood after puberty, and no fulfilling a vow in disobedience, and no silence a day till night, and no wisal in fasting."
"742","659 - Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no divorce for a man in what he does not own, and no emancipation for a man in what he does not own."
"743","660 - Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Matar, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: There is no divorce in what one does not own, and no emancipation in what one does not own, and no sale in what one does not own. So we examined the statement of the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace: There is no divorce except after marriage, and no emancipation except after ownership, and his statement: There is no divorce in what one does not own, and no emancipation in what one does not own, to ascertain its meaning."
"744","So we found Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham ar-Ruayni had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad that he said to Ibn Shihab while discussing with him this sort of divorce of one who did not marry and emancipation of one who did not own: Has it not reached you that the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: There is no divorce before marriage, and no emancipation before ownership. Ibn Shihab said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, did say it, but you applied it upon other than what the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, intended. It is only that a man mentions to a man a woman and it is said to him: Marry her, so he says: She is divorced outright, so this is nothing. As for one who said: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced outright, then he only divorced her when he married her, or said: She is free if I buy her, then he only emancipated her when he bought her."
"745","And we found Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Khalid al-Khayyat narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from az-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha who said: There is no divorce except after marriage. Az-Zuhri said: She only means by that: a man to whom it is said: We marry you to So-and-so, so he says: She is divorced. As for when he says: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced, divorce binds him. So what az-Zuhri related of that was upon a man's statement to a woman between whom and him there is no marriage: You are divorced, not upon his statement to her: When I marry you, you are divorced, upon what the people of knowledge differ in of that. Some of them impose divorce upon him in it if he marries her, among them: Abu Hanifa and those who say his statement, and among them: Malik and those who say his statement, while some of them do not impose divorce in that, among them: ash-Shafii, and he makes him in the ruling of his divorce like one who did not marry. Then we examined what is narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, in that."
"746","So we found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Sad ibn Amr ibn Sulaym az-Zuraqi, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: that a man said: The day I marry So-and-so, or: If I marry So-and-so she is upon me as my mother's back, so Umar ibn al-Khattab said: If you marry her, do not approach her until you expiate it. So this hadith was broken in isnad, not connected to Umar, so we sought it, do we find it from him connected?"
"747","So we found Rawh ibn al-Faraj had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab that Ubaydullah ibn Umar ibn Hafs ibn Asim ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to him, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Amr ibn Sulaym that a man said: The day I marry So-and-so... then he mentioned this hadith which we mentioned from Malik the same. Then we sought what indicates to us the meeting of Amr ibn Sulaym with Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"748","So we found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth informed me, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from an-Numan ibn Murra az-Zuraqi, from Amr ibn Sulaym az-Zuraqi that he saw Umar ibn al-Khattab at midday going to land of his at al-Jurf. He said: So I followed him until I caught him. He said: So we walked together, then we met Ali ibn Abi Talib carrying sticks of grapes, so Umar said to Ali: What remains of your vigor, so he cast what he was carrying then hastened, so Umar said to him: I see there remains of your vigor. Then we went on and passed, and we met a donkey of Umar carrying greens driven by a slave boy of his, so he said to his slave boy: Bring me the donkey quickly, so he brought it to him with no halter upon it nor saddlecloth, and he wanted to ride, so I wanted to place my cloak under him. He said: Keep your cloak from me, so he rode it without halter nor saddlecloth. So we understood by that that Amr ibn Sulaym is of those who accompanied Umar. Then we sought what was narrated in that from other than Umar of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreeing with what was narrated from Umar in it."
"749","So we found Abu Umayya had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Qays, meaning al-Asadi, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad that he was tried with that and said: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced, meaning then he married her, so Ibn Masud said: Your wife has become separated from you, so betroth her to herself."
"750","And we found Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn ar-Rabi al-Lului had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Jufi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Qays al-Asadi narrated to us, from Ibrahim an-Nakhai, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah... with the like of its meaning. So what we narrated in that from Umar and Ibn Masud was what agreed with the statement of those who went in that to imposing this statement upon its sayer. Then we looked: was there narrated from anyone of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite of them two in that."
"751","So we found Ali ibn Shayba had narrated to us, he said: Qabisa ibn Uqba narrated to us, he said: I heard ath-Thawri, and he was asked about a man who said: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced, so he mentioned from Abdulala, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas that he did not consider it anything."
"752","And we found Ahmad ibn Abdulmumin al-Marwazi had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Abu Hamza narrated to us, from Yazid an-Nahwi, from Ikrima who said: There was mentioned to Ibn Abbas the statement of Ibn Masud: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced, that if he marries her she is divorced? So Ibn Abbas said: I do not think he said this, and if he did say it, then how many a slip of a scholar. Allah Mighty and Majestic says: {O you who believe, when you marry believing women then divorce them}. As for the Followers, they differ in that like the difference of those before them and the difference of those after them. Then we examined what the evidences of the agreed-upon اصول require in that. So we found a man says: Every child this slave-girl of mine gives birth to is free, then she bears children after that then gives birth to them, that they are freed upon him, though at the time in which he said the statement by which they were freed upon him he was not owner of them, because they had not been created then. So they did not consider in that the time of the statement which was from him, and they considered the time of its occurrence, so they made it sufficient, and it was from him then. So like that by analogy is that the time in which the man we mentioned said: So-and-so is divorced if I marry her, or So-and-so is free if I own her, is not considered, and the time of the occurrence of his divorce and the time of the occurrence of his emancipation is considered. If a speaker says: This differs from what is before it because of the ownership by the sayer of this statement of the slave-girl to whom he said it at the time of his saying it to her, it is said to him: There was no disagreement in his ownership of her then, nor in the absence of his ownership of what he effected his emancipation upon then. And in what we mentioned is proof for what we described. And we found the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, in his answer to Umar ibn al-Khattab when he said to him: I own a hundred shares of Khaybar and I wanted to draw near by them to Allah Mighty and Majestic, upon what has..."
"753","661 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, ash-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that Umar owned a hundred shares of Khaybar and gathered them, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have acquired what I never acquired the like of, and I wanted to draw near by it to Allah Mighty and Majestic, so he said to him: Detain the principal and make the fruit a سبيل."
"754","662 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb ibn Ali an-Nasai had narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abdurrahman narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar... then he mentioned the like of it exactly. So in the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umar, in answer to his asking him, to detain the principal of these shares of his and to make سبيل of their fruit occurring in them, was what indicated the permissibility of contracts in things newly occurring from them of what they were not owners of at the time of their contracting what they contracted in them. So like that also is what a man contracts upon what he will own in the future of slaves, of emancipation, and upon what he will marry of women, of divorce; its ruling is like the ruling of what occurs from dedicated things, so that emancipation and that divorce run in what they were contracted upon as the aspects contracted upon the fruit occurring after dedication ran in dedicated things. And like that also is what they agreed upon permitting in agencies, in one upon whom a neck is due in zihar or oath expiation, so he deputizes a man to buy it and emancipate it from him for that, then the deputy does what he commanded him of that: that that suffices for him for the neck that was upon him, though the agency from him in it was before he owned it, so that did not harm him, and the time of the occurrence of his emancipation upon it was considered, and his deputizing for that before his owning it was not considered. And of that is what they agreed upon in bequests, so they permitted a man to bequeath a third of his wealth in what he bequeaths, so that is operative in what he was owner of on the day he bequeathed of what remains in his ownership until he dies, and in what he acquires after that until he dies of what remains in his ownership until he dies, and his ownership on the day he bequeathed was not considered, so his bequests are permitted in it, nor his lack of it so his bequests are nullified by it. Rather the continuation of his ownership when he dies upon the things he dies leaving while owner of them was considered, so his bequests were operated in them then, for their occurring in what was owned by him on the day they became due. So like that are the oath contracts we mentioned, of emancipation and of divorce: the ownership of their contractors of them on the day they contracted those oaths upon them is not considered, and their ownership of them at their occurrence upon them is considered. Then we examined this chapter also and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow for the son of Adam in what he does not own, and we shall mention that later in this book of ours, Allah willing, as he said: There is no divorce except after marriage. Then we found Allah the Exalted said in His Book: {And of them are those who covenanted with Allah: If He gives us of His bounty} to His statement: {and for what they used to lie}. So what was from them by their statement: If Allah gives us {of His bounty we shall surely give charity and surely be of the righteous}, was of what obligated them when He gave them what they promised to do in it when He gave it to them, and that was unlike their statement in what they do not own. So like that is a man's statement: If I marry So-and-so she is divorced; its ruling is unlike his ruling when he says: She is divorced, and did not say: When I marry her. So what he said in her binds him when he says: When I marry her she is divorced, while his statement to her: She is divorced, without saying: When I marry her, does not bind him, and with Allah, Majestic and Exalted, is success."
"755","Chapter 97 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning one who persists in an oath against his family. 663 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Sallam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: Whoever persists in an oath against his family, he is greater in sin. Meaning the expiation. So we examined what is intended by what is in this hadith. We found one who swore upon his wife not to approach her, preventing her of a right of hers upon him, and what was due upon him after his swearing that upon her was return to her, and retraction of his oath upon her preventing her right upon him. And of that is His Exalted statement: {For those who swear off from their wives is a wait of four months}... to His statement: {Hearing, Knowing}. So He mentioned in return mercy and forgiveness for the returner's return from preventing the right that is upon him by his oath which was from him, while He did not mention the like of that in his resolve upon divorce, because in his resolve upon divorce he persists in his persistence in preventing the right that is upon him. And of what enters into this meaning is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace: concerning one who swore an oath in severing kinship or in disobedience besides that."
"756","664 - As Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Abi Mabad, from Ibn Abbas, raised: Whoever swears an oath of severance or disobedience, then breaks it, that is expiation. Abu Jafar said: That is because his breaking it is a return from what he had sworn upon, so his return from that is expiation for him. So like that also is what we narrated of the hadith of Abu Hurayra; it is also of this kind, for one who swears against his family preventing her right which is for her upon him is disobeying his Lord the Exalted, and his expiation from that disobedience is his return from it. If a speaker says: There is not in the hadith his return nor his return to her. So our answer to him in that was: that address which was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to people was an Arabic address addressing an Arab people, so among what he addressed them with of that was what they understood thereby his intent, and it is what we mentioned, so that sufficed him from disclosing it to them with his tongue, like what the Quran came with in its statement in Surat an-Nur: {And were it not for Allah's bounty upon you and His mercy, and that Allah is Relenting, Wise}, sufficing thereby from what would have been were it not for His bounty upon them and His mercy to them. And like His statement in Surat ar-Rad: {And if there were a Quran by which mountains were moved, or the earth cut, or the dead spoken to; rather to Allah belongs the affair entirely}, without His mentioning what would have been, had it been, of doing that, for the understanding of those addressed thereby, of what He wanted them to understand from Him by that address with which He addressed them. So like that of the hadith of Abu Hurayra: Whoever persists in an oath against his family, he is greater in sin, meaning than others besides him of swearers with other than that oath. So, upon him be peace, sufficed with his knowledge that they had understood that from him, with addition of phrases in it disclosing what he intended from them of what he addressed them for with what is in that hadith."
"757","Chapter 98 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning Abu Bakr's interpretation, may Allah be pleased with him, by his command, of the dream he interpreted, and of his statement to him in his interpreting it: You were right in part and wrong in part. 665 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba that Ibn Abbas used to narrate that a man came to the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I see tonight in my dream a canopy dripping clarified butter and honey, and I see people scooping from it with their hands, some taking much and some little, and I see a rope reaching from heaven to earth, and I see you took hold of it and rose, then a man after you took hold of it and rose, then another man took hold of it and rose, then another man took hold of it and it broke, then it was joined for him and he rose. So Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father be for you, you shall let me and I shall interpret it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Interpret. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for the canopy, it is the canopy of Islam, and as for what drips of clarified butter and honey, it is its sweetness and softness, and as for what people scoop of that, it is those taking much of the Quran and those taking little, and as for the rope reaching from heaven to earth, it is the truth you are upon, so you took hold of it and Allah Mighty and Majestic raises you, then a man after you takes hold of it and rises by it, then another man takes it and rises by it, then another man takes it and it breaks with him, then it is joined for him and he rises by it. So inform me, O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be for you, did I hit right or wrong? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You were right in part and wrong in part. He said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, you shall inform me of what I was wrong in. He said: Do not swear."
"758","666 - Ishaq ibn al-Hasan at-Tahhan al-Mawqifi, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"759","667 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Khalid ibn Khali al-Kalai narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb al-Abrash narrated to us, az-Zubaydi narrated to us, from az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud that Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayra used to narrate that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw tonight a canopy dripping clarified butter and honey... then he mentioned the hadith."
"760","668 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, Salama ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas... the like of it, except that he said: As for what drips of clarified butter and honey, it is the Quran and its sweetness and softness."
"761","669 - Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamza az-Zubayri al-Madani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abdulaziz ibn Muhammad ad-Darawardi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Muslim az-Zuhri narrated to us, from his uncle, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it."
"762","670 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Abdulala ibn Hammad an-Narsi narrated to us, Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas who said: Abu Bakr said concerning something to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I adjure, O Messenger of Allah, did I hit right or wrong? He said: You were right, and he did not mention besides that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not swear."
"763","671 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Kufi narrated to us, Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, Abdurrazzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas who said: Abu Hurayra used to narrate that a man came to the Prophet, upon him be peace... then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Bahr exactly, except that he said: As for what drips of clarified butter and honey, it is the Quran, its sweetness and softness. So we examined what is in this interpretation mentioned in this hadith of the error which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed Abu Bakr had been from him in it. We found in it that he made the clarified butter and honey mentioned in it one thing, and it is the Quran, then described it with sweetness and softness, while we found the people of knowledge of interpretation hold that they are two things, each of them other than its companion, from two different roots. Abu Bakr had returned them to one root, and it is the Quran, though he had made softness and sweetness of their description. That does not prevent them being a description of one thing. And of the proof for them upon what they held of that was."
"764","672 - What ar-Rabi al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad an-Nadr ibn Abduljabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Lahia informed us, from Wahib ibn Abdullah al-Maafiri, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As that he saw in a dream as if in one of his fingers was honey and in the other clarified butter, and as if he licked them both. In the morning he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: You recite the two Books, the Torah and the Furqan. He said: So he used to recite them both. So in this hadith of the interpretation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the mentioned dream of Abdullah ibn Amr in it, concerning clarified butter and honey, was that they are for two different things from two different roots. The interpretation of Abu Bakr in the hadith of the canopy was that they are one thing from one root. So the error in the mention of interpretation with them was this. The correctness in it was what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his interpreting the mentioned dream of Abdullah ibn Amr in this hadith, and Allah we ask for success."
"765","673 - So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdulwahhab narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Abdullah al-Qummi narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Abdullah, from Said, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited a man of the Ansar, and when he drew near his house he heard him speaking inside. When he sought permission upon him and entered, he saw no one. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I heard speech with you. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, I entered inside grieved by people's talk because of the fever in me, then there entered upon me an entrant; I never saw after you a man nobler in مجلس nor better in speech. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And among you are men, if one of them were to swear upon Allah, He would fulfill it."
"766","674 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud, narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamza az-Zubayri narrated to us, Abdulaziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How many a disheveled one with two worn garments, from whom people's eyes turn away, if he swore upon Allah He would fulfill it."
"767","675 - And we found Bakkar and Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Bakr as-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd at-Tawil, from Anas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among Allah's servants are those who, if they swore upon Allah, He would fulfill it."
"768","676 - And we found Muhammad ibn Uzayz had narrated to us, he said: Salama narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: How many a weak, deemed weak one with two worn garments, if he swore upon Allah He would fulfill his oath. Among them is al-Bara ibn Malik."
"769","677 - And we found Abdulghani ibn Abi Aqil al-Lakhmi had narrated to us, he said: Abdurrahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Sulaym, from Muawiya ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to fulfill the oath of the swearer."
"770","678 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shuba narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: fulfilling the oath."
"771","679 - And we found Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, and Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mabad ibn Khalid, from Haritha ibn Wahb al-Khuzai who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise: every weak, deemed weak one, if he swore upon Allah He would fulfill it. Shall I not inform you of the people of Hell: every harsh, greedy, arrogant one."
"772","680 - And we found Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr ibn Bari narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Ubaydullah ibn Anas who said: I heard Anas say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How many a disheveled, dusty one with two worn garments, turned away at people's doors, if he swore upon Allah Mighty and Majestic He would fulfill it. So we understood by what we recited of Allah's Book and by what we narrated of the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the permissibility of swearing, for had swearing been disliked its user would be disobedient and Allah would not fulfill his oath. So a speaker said: What is the meaning of his statement to Abu Bakr when he swore upon him: Do not swear. It was said to him: Abu Bakr's oath was upon him to inform him of the reality of error from the reality of correctness, and that was not attainable in that meaning, for interpretation is only by conjecture and seeking, not by what is besides them both. The like of that has been narrated in it, as:"
"773","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Qutayba narrated to us, from Mahdi ibn Maymun, from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: Interpretation, meaning of dreams, is only a conjecture I conjecture, and it is neither lawful nor unlawful. Then he recited: {And he said to the one whom he thought would be saved of them}. Ahmad said: Meaning that Yusuf, upon him be peace, said to the one whom he thought would be saved of them. So the interpretation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the like of it was also of this kind. His forbidding (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Abu Bakr from swearing upon him to surely inform him of it was for this meaning, not for what is besides it. Of what indicated that is that Abu Bakr swore after the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, as:"
"774","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Umayya ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father who said: Abu Bakr had appointed Umar over Sham, then met him while I was tying camels with their saddles. When he wanted to depart, people said to him: Do you let Umar go to Sham while he is here sufficing you Sham? So he said: I adjure you, stay. So that indicated that the occasion of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding Abu Bakr was with Abu Bakr for what we mentioned, not for what is besides it of dislike of swearing. Ibn Abbas also swore after Abu Bakr, as:"
"775","Bakkar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, from Ismail ibn Raja, from Umayr, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away and Abu Bakr was made successor, al-Abbas and Ali came to Abu Bakr concerning things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left. So Abu Bakr said: A thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left unmoved, I shall not move. When Umar was made successor they disputed to him, so Umar said: A thing Abu Bakr left, I dislike to move it. When Uthman was given authority they disputed to him. He said: So Uthman fell silent and lowered his head. So Ibn Abbas said: So I struck with my hand upon al-Abbas's shoulders and said: O my father, I adjure you, hand it over to Ali. He said: So he handed it over to Ali. So that indicated that the meaning of what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that hadith, of his statement to Abu Bakr: Do not swear, was not in Ibn Abbas's view either upon dislike of swearing, but for the meaning we mentioned, and Allah we ask for success."
"776","Chapter 100 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: The dream is upon a bird's foot so long as it is not interpreted; when it is interpreted it falls. 681 - Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata who said: I heard Waki ibn Hudus narrate, from his uncle Abi Razin al-Uqayli who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dream is upon a bird's foot so long as it is not interpreted; when it is interpreted it falls. Let none relate it except to a beloved one, or an intelligent one, or one of affection. Thus is my memorization of it from him. In my book which I heard it from him is: upon a bird's foot so long as it is not narrated; when it is narrated it occurs. He said: I think he said: Let him not narrate it except to a loved one or an intelligent one. So a questioner asked about the meaning of his statement: The dream is upon a bird's foot so long as it is not interpreted, what is it? So our answer to him in that was that it is probable the dream before it is interpreted is suspended in the air, not fallen and not effecting anything until it is interpreted; when it is interpreted it effects then. He mentioned it as upon a bird's foot, meaning it is not settled. Like that is a man's statement: I am upon a bird's wing, when he is on a journey, meaning I am not settled until I exit my journey and settle in my residence. So this speaker said: Abu Bakr interpreted in the hadith of the canopy that mentioned dream in it, so the Prophet, upon him be peace, said to him: You were right in part and wrong in part. So it was understood that what of that was error does not operate in what he interpreted of that dream, what he interpreted of it upon him. So our answer to him in that was: that interpretation's operation in a dream, when it is interpreted by it, only operates when the interpretation is correct, or when the dream bears two aspects, one of them worthier of it than the other, so it is suspended upon the interpretation which returns it to one of them until it is interpreted upon it and returned to it, so it falls thereby, and that interpretation is its interpretation, while the other aspect which had been probable for it is negated from it, and Allah we ask for success."
"777","Chapter 101 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning the things which are the fitra in bodies, or of the fitra. 682 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Hanzala ibn Abi Sufyan informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: The fitra is: clipping nails, taking the mustache, and shaving the pubes."
"778","683 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fitra is five: circumcision, shaving, trimming the mustache, paring nails, and plucking armpits."
"779","684 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Sulayman al-Hadrami narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Khurasani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Salama ibn Muhammad ibn Ammar, from Ammar ibn Yasir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of the fitra are: rinsing the mouth, sniffing water, the siwak, trimming the mustache, paring nails, washing knuckles, plucking armpits, shaving, sprinkling, and circumcision."
"780","685 - Fahd narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdulhamid narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Zakariyya, meaning Ibn Abi Zaida, from Musab ibn Shayba, from Talq ibn Habib, from Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr, from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: Ten are of the fitra: trimming the mustache, leaving the beard, the siwak, sniffing water, clipping nails, washing knuckles, plucking armpits, shaving the pubes, and washing with water. Zakariyya said: Musab said: I forgot the tenth, unless it be rinsing the mouth. So a speaker said: This is severe contradiction, for in the first hadith of these hadiths you narrated in this book: the fitra is the three mentioned things in it, in the second of them the fitra is the five mentioned things in it, and in the third and fourth of them the fitra is the ten mentioned things in them. So our answer to him was: there is no contradiction in any of that, for it is permissible the fitra was first the three mentioned things in the first, then Allah the Exalted added in it the other two things mentioned in the second of them, then Allah added in it the things mentioned in the third and fourth of them which are not in the first two. So Allah made it worship for Him upon His creation in their bodies. So by what we mentioned it is negated there is in any of what we described contradiction, and with Allah is success."
"781","Chapter 102 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: Islam began strange and shall return as it began, so glad tidings to the strangers. 686 - Fahd narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange and shall return as it began, so glad tidings to the strangers. So it was said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Those who detach from tribes."
"782","687 - Fahd narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Manazil al-Kufi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"783","688 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdulaziz al-Wasiti narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi Hafs, from Abdullah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange and shall return strange. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who are the strangers? He said: Those who pull away from people."
"784","689 - Fahd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: These hadiths are from Yahya ibn Said, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Imran wrote to me with these hadiths, he said: Abu Ayyash narrated to me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange and it shall return as it began, so glad tidings to the strangers. They said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Those who set right when people corrupt."
"785","690 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Sad ibn Sinan, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange and shall return as it began, so glad tidings to the strangers."
"786","691 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Umayya ibn Bistam narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abdurrahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Religion began strange and religion shall return strange, so glad tidings to the strangers. So we examined these reports and found Islam entered upon things not of its kind, so it was thereby with them strange, unknown, as it is said of one who alights upon a people who do not know him that he is a stranger among them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed it shall return likewise, so one who pulls away from what the blameworthy character is upon to what the praiseworthy character was upon becomes a stranger among them. And of that is what has been narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, as:"
"787","Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abdurrahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, ath-Thawri narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythama, from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: There shall surely come upon people a time they gather in mosques while there is no believer among them. Abu Jafar said: We seek refuge with Allah from that time."
"788","Chapter 103 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning the thing which removes blame in suckling from the wet-nurse for one she suckled. 692 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth, Amr ibn al-Harith and Said ibn Abdurrahman al-Jumahi narrated to us that Hisham ibn Urwa informed them, from his father, from Hajjaj ibn Hajjaj al-Aslami, from his father that he said: O Messenger of Allah, what removes from me the blame of suckling? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A ghurra; a male slave or a female slave."
"789","693 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawraqi narrated to us, Yahya, meaning al-Qattan, narrated to us, from Hisham, meaning Ibn Urwa, my father narrated to me, from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj, from his father: I said, O Messenger of Allah: What removes from me the blame of suckling? He said: A ghurra; a male slave or a female slave."
"790","694 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn Abi az-Zinad narrated to us, from Abi az-Zinad and Hisham ibn Urwa, from Urwa, from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Malik al-Aslami, from his father that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. So a questioner asked about what is intended by what is in this hadith. Our answer to him in that was that the wet-nurse's right due upon one she suckled is undoubted, and that she thereby becomes to him a mother in the obligation of her right upon him. The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said concerning one whose right is below the mother's right:"
"791","695 - What Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No child requites his parent except that he finds him enslaved and buys him and emancipates him. So that was information from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that this deed of a child toward his parent is requital to him for what was from him in him by right of fatherhood. The right of the wet-nurse we mentioned had become due upon the suckled by her suckling him until she thereby became to him a mother, and until what was from her to him became a cause of his life. The rights of mothers upon their children are above the rights of their fathers upon them, and we shall mention that and what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, in it later in our book, Allah willing. Since that was so, and the suckled could not free one who suckled him from bondage when he is not enslaved, he was commanded to compensate her with that in which she can effect emancipation which is ransom for her from Hell, as has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, concerning one who emancipates a believing soul, of what we shall mention later in this book of ours, Allah willing. That soul was not made as other souls, but made of their choicest, and their choicest are their most eminent."
"792","As Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Ansari ad-Dulabi Abu Bishr narrated to us, Abu Yala as-Saji narrated to us, al-Asmai narrated to us, he said: Abu Amr ibn al-Ala said: No black male slave nor black female slave is accepted in blood-money, and it is the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): In the fetus is a ghurra, a male slave or a female slave. Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not intended thereby the white, he would have said: In the fetus is a male slave or a female slave. He said: All this is in the hadith of Abu Bishr. Abu Jafar said: So likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning what removes the blame of suckling, had he not intended the eminent of slaves he would have said in it: it is a male slave or a female slave, and not said: it is a ghurra. In what we mentioned is what indicated that the suckled, if able to emancipate one who suckled him from bondage because he is so, and emancipates him, is thereby requiting him, as a child by the like of it is requiting his father, and Allah we ask for success."
"793","Chapter 104 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated concerning the splitting of the moon in the time of the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, confirming Allah Mighty and Majestic's statement: The Hour drew near and the moon split. 696 - Ali ibn Abdurrahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughira al-Makhzumi al-Kufi narrated to us, Luwayn narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Muawiya al-Jufi narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi Hudhayfa, Abu Jafar said and he is Salama ibn Suhayb al-Arhabi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The moon split while we were with the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace."
"794","697 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mughira, from Abi ad-Duha, from Masruq, from Abdullah who said: The moon split at Mecca, so Quraysh said: This is sorcery by which Ibn Abi Kabsha bewitched you."
"795","698 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abi Mamar, from Ibn Masud who said: The moon split in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet, upon him be peace, said: Bear witness."
"796","699 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Umar al-Wasiti al-Jawaribi Abu Abdullah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abi Mamar, from Abdullah who said: The moon split in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into two halves; the mountain covered a half, and a half was above the mountain. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bear witness."
"797","700 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, upon him be peace... the like of that."
"798","701 - Fahd narrated to us, Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim ibn Mukhawwal ibn Rashid al-Kufi narrated to us, Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us. And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Israil narrated to us. Then both joined, and each one of them said in his hadith: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ibrahim an-Nakhai, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud who said: The moon split, and I saw the mountain between the two clefts of the moon."
"799","702 - And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abi Mamar, from Abdullah who said: The moon split, and a part of it broke off behind the mountain. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Bear witness."
"800","703 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abi Mamar, from Abdullah who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina, then the moon split, and a half of it went behind the mountain. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bear witness."
"801","704 - Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd ar-Ruayni, and Fahd, said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Jafar ibn Rabia, from Irak ibn Malik, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The moon split in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"802","705 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Bakr and Ibn Lahia narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"803","706 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Hudba ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Ata ibn as-Saib, from Abi Abdurrahman as-Sulami who said: I went out with my father to Friday prayer at al-Madain, between us and it a farsakh, while Hudhayfa was over al-Madain. So he praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: {The Hour drew near and the moon split}. Lo, the Hour has drawn near; lo, the moon has split."
"804","707 - Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah an-Nakhai narrated to us, from Ata ibn as-Saib, from Abi Abdurrahman as-Sulami... then he mentioned from Hudhayfa the like of it."
"805","708 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Shuba, from Qatada, from Anas: {The Hour drew near and the moon split}. He said: It has split. So in what we mentioned, from Ali, Ibn Masud, Hudhayfa, Ibn Umar, Ibn Abbas and Anas, was their affirmation of the splitting of the moon. Of them, one who said in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of them one who did not say that, while his meaning in that is as their meaning in it. We do not know narrated from anyone of the people of knowledge in that other than what was narrated from them in it. They are the exemplar and proof; none departs from them except an ignorant one, and none turns away from what they were upon except a loser. One who claims interpretation, uses his opinion in it, confines to that, and leaves mention of what those before him in it were upon, of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their Followers, has claimed it did not split, that it shall only split on Resurrection Day, and that the meaning of Allah the Exalted's statement: {and the moon split} is upon a clause mentioned after that in the sura in which that is mentioned, and it is His Exalted statement: {The Day the caller calls to a terrible thing}, meaning: the moon shall split then, making that of the things that shall be at Resurrection. He mentioned in his ignorance that none narrated it had been except Ibn Masud, and that had it been of what passed as narrated from him, people would be equal in it, and it would not need ascribing to one of them to the exclusion of others. That suffices as ignorance, since what he ascribed to Ibn Masud's solitude in it, five others besides him of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shared in it, whom we mentioned in the reports we narrated at the beginning of this chapter. As for what he mentioned, that Allah the Exalted's statement: {and the moon split} only refers to what he mentioned as its clause of what we mentioned from him, of the sura in which that is mentioned, then in Allah the Exalted's statement: {And if they see a sign they turn away and say: continuous sorcery}, is proof against what he said in it, and proof it did not mean Resurrection Day thereby, for signs are only in this world before Resurrection, as Allah the Exalted said: {We send signs only as fright}. In His Exalted statement: {So turn away from them}, meaning: turn from them, as the Exalted said: {So turn away from them till a time}, and as He said: {So turn away from them, for you are not blamed}, is proof of the completion of what He mentioned before that, and the beginning of other than it, and it is His statement: {The Day the caller calls to a terrible thing}, which is an adverb for what He mentioned after it, of their exiting graves {as if they were spread locusts}. It is negated that be a clause for the speech cut off which preceded it. Then this eccentric said: The statement of Ibn Masud may bear - meaning what this eccentric related from him, and it is that he mentioned from him that he said: The statement of Ibn Masud may bear: as if I look to it in two halves, with Hira between them, meaning: as if I see it when it splits thus. His speech was corrupt, for he negated its splitting in Ibn Masud's time, and mentioned its splitting shall be after that. If it were as he said, it would be permissible Ibn Masud not see it then. He said: It would be permissible he see it where he said, and permissible he see it in other than that place. This eccentric claimed that shall only be at Resurrection, not in this world, while Hira then is a mountain of the mountains concerning which Allah the Exalted informed of what shall be of them then: {They ask you about mountains, so say: My Lord shall scatter them utterly, and leave them}, to the verse, and said: {And the Day We move mountains and you see earth bare}, and said: {And mountains shall be like carded wool}. How can Hira then be between the two halves of the moon? We seek refuge with Allah from opposing the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and departing from their doctrines, for that is like arrogance toward Allah's Book. Whoever is arrogant toward Allah's Book and toward the doctrines of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their Followers in it is fit for Allah to bar him its understanding."
"806","As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyayna say concerning Allah the Exalted's statement: {I shall turn away from My signs those who are arrogant in the land without right}. He said: I bar them understanding of My Book. A questioner asked about the meaning of Quraysh's statement at the splitting of the moon: This is sorcery by which Ibn Abi Kabsha bewitched you, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): what was their intent by that, and who is Abu Kabsha to whom they ascribed him? Our answer to him in that was: the best we found of what was said in that is what entered into what Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani permitted us, from al-Mufaddal ibn Ghassan al-Ghallabi, he said: Wahb, grandfather of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), father of his mother, Qayla daughter of Abi Qayla, and the name of Abu Qayla is Wajz ibn Ghalib, of Khuzaa. He was the first to worship ash-Shira al-Ubur, and he used to say: ash-Shira cuts the sky across, and I see in the sky no sun, moon, nor star cutting the sky across besides it. This Wajz is Abu Kabsha to whom Quraysh used to ascribe the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Arabs used to think none knows anything except by a strain pulling him in resemblance. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed Quraysh's religion, Quraysh said: Abu Kabsha pulled him, because Abu Kabsha opposed people in worshipping ash-Shira. So they used to ascribe the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him for that. Abu Kabsha was a chief in Khuzaa; they did not revile the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by him for any shortcoming in him, but they intended to liken him to him in opposing what people were upon."
"807","Chapter 105 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, of his forbidding the miller's qafiz. 709 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Ata ibn as-Saib, from Ibn Abi Num, from some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Prophet, upon him be peace, that he forbade the stud fee of the he-goat, the earnings of the cupper, and the miller's qafiz."
"808","710 - Al-Hajjaj ibn Imran ibn al-Fadl al-Mazini al-Basri narrated to us, Hilal ibn Yahya ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Ata ibn as-Saib, from some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it, without mentioning in it Ibn Abi Num."
"809","711 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Isa ibn Masarjis, freedman of Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us. And Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, they said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan, meaning ath-Thawri, from Hisham Abi Kulayb, from Ibn Abi Num, from Abi Said al-Khudri who said: The stud fee of the stallion and the miller's qafiz were forbidden. So we examined that and found the people of knowledge do not differ that its meaning is what they used to do in Jahiliyya, and what ignorant people do to this day, of handing wheat to the miller for him to mill it for them for a qafiz of its flour which he mills of it. That was hiring by the hirer with what is not with him, since the flour of his wheat is not with him at the time he hires. In that was what indicated hiring is not with what is not with the hirer on the day he hires, as buying is not with what is not with the buyer on the day he sells, nor with what is not with the buyer on the day he buys, of things not with him whose meaning is not the meaning of prices, like dirhams and dinars and like others of fungibles which may be debt in liabilities, and with Allah is success."
"810","Chapter 106 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was from the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, between his two prostrations in his prayer: is it remembrance of Allah the Exalted or silence without remembrance? 712 - Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Salim as-Saigh narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr, judge of Kirman, narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Murra informed me, he said: I heard Abu Hamza, a man of the Ansar, narrate from a man of Banu Abs, from Hudhayfa that he came upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying voluntary at night. He said: Allah is Greatest, Possessor of dominion, compulsion, grandeur and greatness. Then he recited al-Baqara, then bowed, and his bowing was about as his standing. He used to say in his bowing: Glory to my Lord the Mighty. Then he raised his head and stood as long as he bowed, saying: To my Lord is praise, to my Lord is praise. Then he prostrated, about as his standing, saying: Glory to my Lord the Most High. Between the two prostrations about as his prostration, saying: My Lord, forgive me, my Lord, forgive me. So he prayed four rakas, reciting in them al-Baqara, Al Imran, an-Nisa, al-Maida and al-Anam."
"811","713 - And with it, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from al-Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, from Sila ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfa the like of it. And he said: He did not pass a verse of mercy but stopped and asked his Lord Mighty and Majestic, nor pass a verse of torment but stopped and sought refuge."
"812","714 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say between his two prostrations in every raka of those rakas of his prayer: My Lord, forgive me, my Lord, forgive me. We do not know of anyone of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to do that in his prayer besides Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, for it has been narrated from him that he used to do that in it."
"813","Al-Kaysani narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, from Abi Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali with that. We do not know anyone of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides him, nor of their Followers, nor of those after their Followers to this day, who held that, besides some who professed hadith, who held that and said by it. This in our view of his statement is good, and its use is reviving a sunna of the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace. To it we go and it we use. We found analogy supports it: we saw prayer built upon parts, among them the takbir by which one enters it, among them the standing following it of it, in which is remembrance, namely the opening and what is recited after it of the Quran in it. Then follows that bowing, in which is remembrance, namely glorification. Then follows it rising from bowing, in which rising is remembrance, namely: Allah hears one who praises Him, and what is besides that of what some of the imams say, of Our Lord, to You is praise, while the rest do not say. Then follows it prostration, in which is remembrance, namely glorification. Then follows it a sitting between the two prostrations, which is the one in which is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he used to say in it, of his asking his Lord Mighty and Majestic forgiveness for him twice. Then follows it a sitting in which is remembrance, namely the tashahhud and what is after it in the place it is in, of prayer upon the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, and of supplication supplicated there. So all parts of prayer had remembrance of Allah the Exalted used in them, not devoid of that, besides the sitting between the two prostrations we mentioned. By analogy upon what we described, the ruling of that part of prayer is also like the ruling of its other parts: there is in it remembrance of Allah the Exalted as in its other parts, and with Allah is success."
"814","Chapter 107 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning the reward of one who emancipates a neck, and concerning whom he aimed at thereby of necks, of males and of females. 715 - Abu Umayya, Fahd and Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi Abu Ishaq said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Abi Num al-Bajali narrated to us, Fatima daughter of Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated to me, she said: My father said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever emancipates a Muslim or believing neck, Allah the Exalted shields with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"815","716 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Murra, from al-Qasim, from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever emancipates a neck, Allah emancipates with every limb of it a limb of him."
"816","717 - Fahd narrated to us, Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us, Sulaym ibn Amir narrated to me that Shurahbil ibn as-Simt said to Amr ibn Abasa: Narrate to us a hadith with no addition nor forgetfulness. So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever emancipates a Muslim neck, it is his ransom from Hell, limb for limb."
"817","718 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, ash-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Shuba al-Kufi who said: I was with Abu Burda ibn Abi Musa on a rooftop, then he called his sons and said: O my sons, I heard my father say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever emancipates a neck, Allah emancipates with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"818","719 - Ibn Marzuq and Abu Umayya said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Said ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, from Said ibn Marjana who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever emancipates a believing neck, Allah emancipates with every joint of it a joint of him from Hell, such that He emancipates with the hand the hand, with the foot the foot, and with the private part the private part. Abu Umayya said in his hadith: from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, freedman of the family of az-Zubayr."
"819","720 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Asim ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Muhammad, from Said ibn Marjana who said: Abu Hurayra said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever man emancipates a Muslim man, Allah rescues with every limb of him a limb of him from Hell."
"820","721 - Ar-Rabi ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Umar ibn Ali ibn Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib that he said: I heard Said ibn Marjana narrate to my father, say: I heard Abu Hurayra say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever emancipates a believing neck, Allah emancipates with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell, even his private part for her private part."
"821","722 - Ibn Khuzayma and Fahd said: Ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn al-Had narrated to me... then they both mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"822","723 - Ar-Rabi ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad an-Nadr ibn Abduljabbar narrated to us, Nafi ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had that Umar ibn Ali ibn Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated to him, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"823","724 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Salih ibn Ubayd, he narrated to him, from Nabil, owner of the cloak, he narrated to him, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever emancipates a believing neck, Allah shields him with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell. So what we narrated of these reports, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was upon emancipating a neck described in some of them with faith or with Islam, and in some of them: Whoever emancipates a neck, without mention of it with faith nor Islam. So we looked: was there narrated from him in this chapter a distinction between male necks and female ones? And was there narrated from him a distinction between emancipators of males and females?"
"824","725 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Abu Muawiya narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Shurahbil ibn as-Simt who said: We said to Kab ibn Murra: O Kab ibn Murra, narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and beware. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, say: Whoever emancipates a Muslim man, he is his ransom from Hell; requited, every bone in place of every bone of him. Whoever emancipates two Muslim women, they are his ransom from Hell; requited, in place of every two bones of them a bone of him."
"825","726 - And we found Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Salim, from Shurahbil who said: We said to Kab ibn Murra, or Murra ibn Kab: Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah bless your father, and beware. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whichever Muslim man emancipates a Muslim man, he is his ransom from Hell, requited with every bone of his bones. Whichever Muslim man emancipates two Muslim women, they are his ransom from Hell, requited with every two bones of them a bone of his bones. Whichever Muslim woman emancipates a Muslim woman, she is her ransom from Hell, requited with every bone of her a bone of her bones."
"826","727 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Masud narrated to us, from Khalid, Hisham narrated to us, Qatada narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan ibn Abi Talha, from Abi Najih, Abu Jafar said and he is Amr ibn Abasa, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whichever Muslim man emancipates a Muslim man, Allah makes the shield of every bone of his bones a bone of the bones of his freed one from Hell. Whichever Muslim woman emancipates a Muslim woman, Allah Mighty and Majestic makes the shield of every bone of her bones a bone of the bones of her freed one from Hell."
"827","728 - And we found Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar had narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Zaida narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur narrate from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad who said: I was told from Kab ibn Murra al-Bahzi that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it."
"828","729 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman ar-Rahawi narrated to us, Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Zaida, from Mansur, from Salim who said: I was told, from Kab ibn Murra al-Bahzi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"829","730 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Mufaddal ibn Muhalhal narrated to me, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Kab ibn Murra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it."
"830","731 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Mansur, from Salim, from Kab ibn Murra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it."
"831","732 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzayma had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abi Qilaba that Shurahbil ibn Hasana said: Who is a man to narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So Amr ibn Abasa said: I. So he said: Speak, may Allah bless your father, and beware. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever emancipates a Muslim neck, it is his ransom from Hell, a bone of his bones for a bone of its bones. Whoever emancipates two Muslim necks, they are his ransom from Hell, a bone of the bones of his freed one for a bone of his bones. Ayyub said: I thought he means two women. So we understood by that that he, upon him be peace, by what he mentioned in the first reports, intended of emancipators and emancipated equivalence in that: the emancipator, if male, what he ransoms himself from Hell with is a Muslim male, or two Muslim females; the emancipator, if female, what she ransoms herself from Hell with is a Muslim female. All that was made only in believing necks, to the exclusion of disbelieving necks besides them, and with Allah is success."
"832","Chapter 108 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning what he commanded those who mentioned to him of Banu Sulaym that a companion of theirs had incurred, in emancipation for that. 733 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu an-Numan Muhammad ibn al-Fadl as-Sadusi, whose لقب is Arim, narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla, from al-Gharif ibn Ayyash, from Wathila ibn al-Asqa who said: A party of Banu Sulaym came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: A companion of ours has incurred. He said: Let him emancipate a neck; Allah shall ransom with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"833","734 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, al-Mualla ibn al-Walid al-Qaqai narrated to us, Hani ibn Abdurrahman narrated to us, my uncle Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla al-Uqayli narrated to me, he said: I met men of the Companions of the Prophet, upon him be peace; I saw two men of them, I spoke to one of them and did not speak to the other. Abu Ubayy ibn Umm Haram al-Ansari informed us, who was of those who witnessed the two qiblas with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I saw upon him a dusty khazz cloak. I saw Wathila ibn al-Asqa but did not speak to him. Al-Gharif ibn ad-Daylami stood to him until he sat to him. When he rose from him I met him and said: What did he narrate to you? So he said: He narrated to me that a party of Banu Sulaym came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Tabuk expedition and said: O Messenger of Allah, a companion of ours has incurred, meaning Hell. So he said: Command him to emancipate a neck; Allah shall expiate with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"834","735 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu Mushir narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla narrated to me, al-Gharif ibn Ayyash ibn Fayruz ad-Daylami narrated to me: that Wathila ibn al-Asqa narrated to him, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Tabuk expedition, then people of Banu Sulaym came and said: O Messenger of Allah, a companion of ours has incurred. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let him emancipate a neck; Allah the Exalted shall free thereby with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"835","736 - Al-Layth ibn Abda ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Asad al-Khushshi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abdurrahman ibn Hassan al-Filastini al-Kinani narrated to me, from one who heard Wathila, and they asked him to narrate to them a hadith with no delusion nor deficiency. So Wathila grew angry and said: You renew looking in masahif morning and evening, while you err, add and omit. Then he said: People came to the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, and said: O Messenger of Allah, this companion of ours has incurred. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Command him to emancipate a neck, for Allah emancipates with every limb of the emancipated a limb of him."
"836","737 - Al-Walid said, and I say: Malik ibn Anas and others narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla that he narrated to them, from Abdullah ibn ad-Daylami, from Wathila with the like of it. So in these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded those who asked him about what they asked him about in them, commanding them to command their companion with what they mentioned to him in them, to emancipate for himself a neck to be his ransom from Hell. These reports have been narrated with other than these wordings."
"837","738 - As ar-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Damra narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla who said: I heard him mention from al-Gharif ibn ad-Daylami who said: We came to Wathila and said to him: Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with no addition nor omission. So he grew angry and said: One of you recites while his mushaf hangs in his house, yet adds and omits. We said: We only intended a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with no one between you and him. He said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning a companion of ours who had incurred, meaning Hell, by killing. So he said: Emancipate for him a neck; Allah shall emancipate with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell."
"838","739 - Ali ibn Abdurrahman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla narrated to me, he said: I was sitting at Ariha, then Wathila passed by me leaning upon Abdullah ibn ad-Daylami, so he seated him, then came to me and said: Wondrous what the shaykh narrated to me, meaning Wathila. We said: What did he narrate to you? He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Tabuk expedition, then a party of Banu Sulaym came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, a companion of ours has incurred. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Emancipate for him a neck; Allah shall emancipate with every limb of it a limb of him from Hell. So in these two reports was other than in the first reports, for what is in them is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those who asked him to emancipate for their companion a neck. In the outward of that is his intent of their emancipating it for him, and that that is ransom for him from Hell, while it was not mentioned in them that be from them for him by his command. Their outward is that their emancipating it for him without his command is ransom for him from Hell, as their emancipating it for themselves is ransom for them from Hell. We found Allah the Exalted's Book has repelled the like of this meaning from sinners, and it is His Exalted statement concerning requital for expiation of game killed in ihram in Surat al-Maida upon what He mentioned in it, then He followed it with His statement: {that he may taste the consequence of his deed}. So He informed He placed expiation in killing game in ihram upon its killer that he taste the consequence of his killing. Like that in every expiation for a sin is only intended thereby the sinner's tasting its consequence. In that is what bars another's expiating for him in that by emancipation for him or by other. Then we sought what is in these two of this meaning: can we correct its meaning upon the meanings of the reports we mentioned in the first section of this book. We found all the reports we narrated in this chapter divide into two parts. One: Command him to emancipate a neck. Its narrators thus, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla, author of this hadith, were four men: Malik, Ibn al-Mubarak, Yahya ibn Hamza and Hani ibn Abdurrahman. The other part: Emancipate for him a neck. Those who narrated that from Ibrahim were two men: Abdullah ibn Salim and Damra ibn Rabia. Four are worthier of retention than two, especially with among the four Malik and Ibn al-Mubarak, who in firmness and retention upon what they are upon are worthier than Ibn Salim and Damra. If carrying this chapter upon what the more numerous in number and ضبط in narration narrated is due, then what the companions of the first section narrated, namely: Command him to emancipate a neck, is worthier than what the two who narrated in the second section narrated of what opposes it, namely: Emancipate for him. If carrying it upon what is sound in language is due, then the Arabic language applies to one emancipated by one of a tribe that it is said: that tribe emancipated him; they say: Khuzaa emancipated him, for a man of Khuzaa's emancipating him, and they say: Sulaym emancipated him, for a man of Banu Sulaym's emancipating him. So it was open for this hadith's narrators also to say, relating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what was in it: Command him to emancipate a neck, and to say, relating from him: Emancipate for him a neck, by your commanding him and urging him to emancipate a neck for himself, its emancipation ascribed to you and to him together. So thereby the meanings of what is in these two sections return to one meaning: the emancipation by the man from whom that sin was, for himself, of the neck which is expiation for his sin and ransom for him from Hell from it, and with Allah is success."
"839","Chapter 109 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: I intended not to pray upon him, meaning the emancipator of his six slaves who were all his wealth at his death, and of his anger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from that. 740 - Yusuf ibn Yazid, and Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Kindi Abu Ali, said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, Abu Qilaba narrated to us, from Abi Zayd al-Ansari that a man of the Ansar emancipated six slaves of his at his death, with no wealth besides them. That reached the Prophet, upon him be peace, so he grew angry from that and said: I intended not to pray upon him. Then he called his slaves and divided them into three parts, cast lots among them, freed two and enslaved four."
"840","741 - Yusuf narrated to us, Said narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur, and he is Ibn Zadahan, narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, from the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace... the like of it."
"841","742 - Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hasan, from Imran, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"842","743 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abi Qilaba, from Abi al-Muhallab, from Imran that a man emancipated six slaves of his at his death, with no wealth besides them. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said concerning it a severe statement. Then he called them, divided them into three parts, freed two and enslaved four. So in what we narrated from him, upon him be peace, was his disapproval upon the emancipator in his death illness of all his slaves, his anger from that, and his intending for its sake not to pray upon him. A questioner asked about the meaning for which that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: That sick man was owner of his slaves when there was from him in them what was of emancipation for them. Our answer to him in that was that the deeds of the sick in their illnesses from which they die are shortened for them therein from executing of all their wealth, returned to thirds of their wealth, not exceeding to what is more of their wealth. Since that was so, one befallen by an illness which may be he dies in it and may be he exits from it must not expand in his wealth as the healthy expand in their wealth, for it may be an illness barring him from that, and may be an illness not barring him from that, except the worthier for him is caution for himself and for those for whom he detained the rest of his wealth after his third, of those who inherit him. When he departs from that and expands in all of it as the healthy expand in its like, he is thereby blameworthy. Of the sunna of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his leaving prayer upon the blameworthy. This in our view is the aspect of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intending to leave prayer upon that deceased, this blame having befallen him, and his anger from his deed for which he occupied that station with him. Another questioner asked about lots in the like of this: is it used now or not? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid: is that the people of knowledge differ in that. A party of them say: it is used in that, among them many of the people of Hijaz and ash-Shafii. A party of them say: it is abrogated, and what is due in its place upon the emancipated slaves is earning in two-thirds of their value for their emancipator's heirs. Of those who said that of them: Abu Hanifa and his companions, and many of the people of Kufa besides them. They argue its abrogation by that they and their opponents together made the hadith we narrated in the sick's emancipation we mentioned a proof for them and a proof against their opponent who claims the sick's emancipation and gifts of all his wealth are as the healthy's emancipation and gifts, arguing thereby that his wealth was not owned against him until those deeds of his occurred in it. When that must be so, its likes must be returned to it and its similars adjoined to it, of what the sick does in his death illness, for it is a root for it: what is due in illness, when he has six hundred dirhams which are all his wealth, then gifts in his death illness every hundred of them to a man and hands it to him, then dies, is to cast lots among them in them as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots in the emancipated slaves we mentioned, so his gift is secured to whom the lot falls, and the rest returns as inheritance, like what was from the Prophet, upon him be peace, in the emancipated slaves. In their leaving that and exiting from it to sharing among gift recipients in them, and leaving lots upon them, it had been used in other than the emancipation we mentioned, then left and its opposite used in its place. Of them is claiming lineages when equal from their claimants."
"843","744 - As Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, Jafar ibn Awn al-Umari or Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us - the shaykh said: I doubt which of them narrated it to me from him - from al-Ajlah, from ash-Shabi, from Abdullah ibn al-Khalil al-Aslami, from Zayd ibn Arqam who said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of Yemen came to him while Ali was then there. He said: O Messenger of Allah, three men came to Ali disputing over a child; they had intercourse with his mother in one purity. So he cast lots among them, and the lot fell upon one of them, so the child was handed to him. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared, or he said: his teeth."
"844","745 - And Ali ibn al-Husayn Abu Ubayd narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi ar-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, Abdurrazzaq informed us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salih, from ash-Shabi, from Abd Khayr al-Hadrami, from Zayd ibn Arqam who said: Ali was in Yemen, then a woman was brought to whom three men had intercourse in one purity. So he asked two to affirm the child to this one, but they did not affirm. Then he asked two to affirm the child to this one, but they did not affirm. Then he asked two until he finished asking two by two, all but one, but they did not affirm. So he cast lots among them, attached the child to whom the lot fell, and placed upon him two-thirds of the blood-money. That was raised to the Prophet, upon him be peace, so he laughed until his molars appeared. In the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving disapproval of that from Ali was his pleasure with it from him, and that the ruling was in it with him then thus. Then we found Ali after this, or after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was brought in the like of this story and ruled in it contrary to this ruling."
"845","As Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi ar-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, Abdurrazzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Qabus, from Abi Zibyan, from Ali who said: Two men who had intercourse with a woman in a purity came to him. So he said: The child is between you both, and he is for the survivor of you. So it was impossible in our view, and Allah knows best, that Ali would judge contrary to what he judged in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove nor return the ruling in it to contrary of what he judged in it, with contrary of that, unless what he judged in the time of the Prophet, upon him be peace, in that was abrogated to what he judged in his own time. Had it not been thus, there would be fall of his justice, far be it from Allah that it be so. Rather he returned from an abrogated he had been upon to its abrogator, and Allah knows best. If a speaker says: How can lots be abrogated while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it among his wives when intending travel with one of them?"
"846","746 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from az-Zuhri, from Urwa, Said, Ubaydullah and Alqama, from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended travel, used to cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, he went out with her."
"847","747 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"848","748 - And as Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham narrated to us, Said ibn Isa ibn Talid narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Fadala al-Qitbani narrated to me, from Abi at-Tahir Abdulmalik ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his uncle Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm who said: My maternal aunt Amra daughter of Abdurrahman narrated to me, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her... then he mentioned the like of it. He said: How is it permissible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would use what had been abrogated before that? He said: Of that is what Muslims acted upon in their divisions, upon which their affairs ran to now, using lots in them. Our answer to him in that was that what we mentioned of abrogated lots is the lots used in judgments thereby, binding as what is judged in by other than them of proofs and others. As for what you mentioned, it was not used by way of judgment thereby, but used for pleasing hearts and negating suspicions, not for what is besides that. Except he sees that it was for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to travel without any of his wives, and since it was for him to travel without them, it was for him to travel without some of them. In that is what indicated his casting lots among them, for what he cast lots among them, was not upon a judgment among them, nor upon them, nor for them. Rather it was to please their hearts, that there not fall into some of their hearts inclination from him to one he travels with of them to the exclusion of the rest. Divisions, had parts been equalized, then each owner of a part given a part of those parts without lots upon that, that would be permissible, sound. That indicated lots were only used in that to negate suspicions thereby from one undertaking division among its folk, of inclination to one of them or what is besides that. There is not in any of what we mentioned, of travel with wives and of divisions in which lots are used, when used in them, judgment by lots. So likewise we say: whatever is of the likes of these two kinds in which by lots no judgment falls, but pleasing hearts and negating suspicions fall, there is no harm in using lots in it. Whatever is besides that in which judgment and rulings fall, there is no way to use them in it, for what we related in its like from Ali in the time of the Prophet, upon him be peace, and in his leaving after him that and using its opposite. Each of these two kinds we mentioned is upon what has been narrated in it of what we described; one kind does not enter into the other of them, and each is upon what what we described in it in this chapter requires, and Allah we ask for success."
"849","Chapter 110 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, from his statement: The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. 749 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us. And Abu Umayya narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from ash-Shabi who said: I heard an-Numan ibn Bashir say: The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Perhaps he said: doubtful, and I shall strike for you a parable: Allah has a sanctuary, Allah's sanctuary is what He forbade, and whoever pastures around the sanctuary is about to pasture in it."
"850","750 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida narrated to us, from ash-Shabi who said: I heard an-Numan say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters unknown to many people. Whoever wards off doubts clears his honor and religion, while whoever falls into doubts falls into the unlawful, like a shepherd pasturing around the sanctuary about to fall into it. Lo, every king has a sanctuary; lo, Allah's sanctuary is His prohibitions."
"851","751 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, al-Mualla ibn Mansur ar-Razi narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abdulhamid narrated to us, from Mughira, from ash-Shabi who said: I witnessed an-Numan ibn Bashir upon this pulpit of ours say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, say: The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between the lawful and unlawful are doubtful matters. Whoever leaves them clears his religion and honor, while whoever pastures in them is about to fall into the unlawful, like one who pastures around the sanctuary about to pasture in it. Lo, every king has a sanctuary, and the unlawful is Allah's sanctuary which He forbade His servants."
"852","752 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdala, from Khaythama and ash-Shabi, from an-Numan ibn Bashir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Clear lawful, clear unlawful, and doubts between that. Whoever leaves doubts is more leaving of the unlawful, and Allah's prohibitions are a sanctuary; whoever pastures around the sanctuary is about to pasture in it. So a questioner asked about the meaning intended by this hadith. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success: is that Allah has laws He legislated and worshipped His servants with them. Of them what He mentioned in His Book firm, disclosing to them its meaning, and of them what He mentioned in His Book ambiguous. Of that is His statement in His Book: {It is He who sent down upon you the Book; of it are firm verses, mothers of the Book, and others ambiguous}. The firm of it which He disclosed to them its meaning was His Exalted statement: {Forbidden to you are your mothers} to His statement: {and daughters of the sister}. The ambiguous of it which He did not disclose to them His intent in it of it was His statement: {The thief and the thiefess, cut their hands}, and of it His statement in fasting: {Eat and drink until the white thread is clear to you from the black thread of dawn}, and of it His statement in the verse in which He mentioned what He forbade you: {And married women except what your right hands own}, and of it His statement: {And to join between the two sisters except what has passed}. So the firm and ambiguous He mentioned in His Book are the two kinds we mentioned. Of them is what He ran upon His Prophet's tongue, upon him be peace, upon this meaning, running some upon his tongue firm, disclosed in meaning, like the five prayers in day and night, like what the traveler shortens of them in his travel, and like what he does not shorten of them in it, being in it in his travel as he was in it in his residence. Of them is what women count in their menstruation days, of leaving prayer and fasting, and of making up fasting after that in their purity days and leaving making up prayer after that. That was of what He ran upon his tongue firm. Of what He ran upon his tongue ambiguous of it was His statement: {The two sellers have choice so long as they do not separate}. Of it His statement: {The cupper and cupped broke fast}, in things of that sort. So they needed to seek their realities and what is upon them in them. That was of the kind Allah sent down upon him in His Book ambiguous, while the first meaning was of the kind He sent down upon him in His Book firm. So the meaning of His statement: {The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear} is what was of firm lawful and firm unlawful. The meaning of His statement: {And between them are doubtful matters} is what may be of clear lawful and may be of clear unlawful, like what we mentioned of joining between two sisters by right-hand ownership, which some returned to permission and some returned to prohibition, and likes of that in which proof stands in some of their hearts permitting that, and in some of their hearts prohibiting it. At that the pious differ from others: the pious stop at doubts, suspecting their opinions in them, while others advance upon them. So a speaker said: Does what you mentioned bar judges from judging in what enters upon them of what you described? Our answer to him in that was that what is imposed upon judges in that, after exerting their opinion in it, is executing what their opinions lead them to in it, as the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, commanded them."
"853","753 - As Salih ibn Abdurrahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith and Bakr ibn Idris ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, they said: Abu Abdurrahman al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywa ibn Shurayh narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith at-Taymi, from Busr ibn Said, from Abi Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, from Amr that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the judge judges and exerts and hits right, he has two rewards; when he judges and exerts and errs, he has a reward. He said: So I narrated this hadith to Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, so he said: Thus Abu Salama ibn Abdurrahman narrated to me, from Abu Hurayra. In that is what indicated what is imposed upon judges is using exertion in what they judge, that with it may be correctness and in it may be error, that they were not tasked in that with hitting correctness, but tasked in it with exertion, that it is wide for them in that to execute judgments upon it. Then those judged for in that return to the meaning they were upon before those judgments for them, of piety from entering them and of advancing upon them. If a speaker says: Can you disclose that for us in a question of this kind till we ascertain it? We said to him: Yes. The people of knowledge differed concerning a man who said to his wife: You are upon me unlawful. Sayers of them said: She is divorced upon him three divorces, not lawful to him after them till she marries another husband. Sayers of them said: It is an oath by which he is an ilaer. Sayers of them said: It is zihar, expiated by what expiates zihar. Sayers of them said: It is a divorce by which she separates from him, unless he means of divorce three, binding him that. Sayers of them said: It is a divorce in which he owns her return, unless he intends more of divorce than it, binding him that. So one in charge of those who see her unlawfulness upon him by a statement of these statements, then disputed to a judge who does not see her unlawfulness upon him by it and sees she remains upon his marriage, upon what one who said it said in that of those we mentioned of the people of knowledge in it, so he judged for him by that, fell into difference of the people of knowledge. A party of them say: He may use that and leave his opinion in it opposing it. Of those who said that: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. A party of them say: Rather he uses in that what he sees, and leaves that judgment, since it is only a judgment for him, not a judgment upon him. Of those who said that: Abu Yusuf, and it is worthier of the two statements in our view of truth, and Allah knows best."
"854","Chapter 111 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning what Allah the Exalted was silent about. 754 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Arara ibn al-Birind narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Jassas narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Qurra, from people of the Companions of the Prophet, upon him be peace, that they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Bedouins bring us sliced meats, cheese, clarified butter and furs; we do not know the reality of their Islam. He said: Look at what He forbade you, withhold from it. What He was silent about, He pardoned for you. {Your Lord was never forgetful}. Mention Allah Mighty and Majestic's name. The things intended in this in our view, and Allah knows best, are things of the kind mentioned in this hadith, as latitude from Allah upon His servants in food they eat, of sacrifices Allah permitted them, from hands of those whose sacrifices He permitted them, forbidding them sacrifices of their opposites, of Magians and idol worshippers. He gave them using their outward, upon that they are of what He permitted, till they know other than that of what was forbidden them. Had He, Mighty and Majestic, willed, He would have straitened that upon them and not permitted them eating anything of meats till they know who slaughtered them, and whether they are of those whose sacrifices are lawful or of others. In that would be Allah the Exalted's burdening them, as He said: {Had Allah willed He would have burdened you}. But He lightened that and lifted it from them, as mercy from Him to them and favor from Him upon them. He distinguished between that and the laws He legislated for them in their religion and worshipped them with them in it, commanding them to seek its difficult of its firm and of what is sought of its like, upon what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. Like this hadith is what was narrated from Ibn Abbas."
"855","Of what Abu Umayya narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abi ash-Shatha, from Ibn Abbas who said: The people of Jahiliyya used to eat things and leave things in disgust. So Allah sent His Prophet and sent down His Book, permitted His lawful and forbade His unlawful. So what He permitted is lawful, what He forbade is unlawful, and what He was silent about is pardon. Then he recited: {Say: I find not in what was revealed to me forbidden}, to the verse."
"856","And of what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Muhammad narrated to us, from Amr... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So what is intended by what is in the hadith in our view is what is intended by what is in the hadith we mentioned before it in this chapter, and Allah knows best, and Him we ask for success."
"857","Chapter 112 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him concerning the affair of the two men who disputed to him over things whose affair had grown old and those who knew them had gone, that they divide them between them and each absolve his companion after that. 755 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Qabisa narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Usama ibn Zayd, from Abdullah ibn Rafi, from Umm Salama who said: Two men disputed to the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, over land whose folk had perished and those who knew it had gone. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am only a man, and nothing has come down upon me in it. Perhaps some of you are more eloquent in proof than others. Whoever I cut for him a piece of his brother's wealth unjustly shall come Resurrection Day a brand of fire in his face. So the two men wept, and each of them said: O Messenger of Allah, my right is for him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Aim, then cast lots, then let each of you absolve his companion."
"858","756 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Usama narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Rafi, freedman of Umm Salama, informed him, from Umm Salama that two men of the Ansar sought permission upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he permitted them. They disputed to him over land whose affair had advanced and those who knew its affair had perished. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: I only judge between you by exertion of my opinion in what has not come down upon me. I judge between you upon the like of what I hear from you. Whichever of you has merit in speech over his companion, so I judge for him while I see it his right, when it is of his brother's right, I only judge for him a piece of Hell, encircling him of seven earths, coming with it a brand in his neck Resurrection Day. When they heard that they both wept, and each of them said: O Messenger of Allah, my share is for him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go, exert in dividing the land in halves, then cast lots. When each of you takes his share, let him absolve his brother."
"859","757 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Rafi, freedman of Umm Salama, from Umm Salama who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when two men came to him disputing over inheritances and things long past. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I only judge between you by my opinion in what has not come down upon me. Whoever I judge for with a judgment, thinking he cuts thereby a piece unjustly, only cuts thereby a piece of fire, a brand coming with it in his neck Resurrection Day. So the two men wept, and each of them said: O Messenger of Allah, this right of mine I seek is for my companion. He said: No, but go, aim, then cast lots, then let each of you absolve his companion."
"860","758 - Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi al-Madani as-Saigh narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to me, hearing it from Abdullah ibn Rafi, from Umm Salama who said: Two men of the Ansar came disputing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over inheritances between them long past, with no proof for them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am only a man; a litigant comes to me, and perhaps some of you are more eloquent than others, so I judge for him thereby, thinking him truthful. Whoever I judge for with a Muslim's right, it is only a piece of fire, so let him take it or leave it. So the two men wept, and each of them said: My right is for my brother. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Since you have done this, go, divide and aim at the truth, then cast lots, then let each of you absolve his companion."
"861","759 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"862","760 - Ar-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, Usama ibn Zayd narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So a speaker with no knowledge of the aspects of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths said: what is in this hadith of what is ascribed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his commanding each of the two mentioned men therein, after their dividing what they disputed to him about, to absolve each his companion of a right, if any for him, in what his companion took by right of division, is absurd. For absolution only operates in what is in the liabilities of the absolvers, not in what is in their hands of what is goods or a share in goods. Were a man to say to a man: I absolved you of my house in your hand, or of my slave in your hand, that absolution does not own thereby the absolved anything of that house's substance nor of that slave's substance. This is of what there is no disagreement in. How is it permissible to accept from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you narrated in this hadith, of his commanding each of the two litigants who disputed to him, after dividing his companion, to absolve him of a right, if any for him, in his hand? Our answer to him was: the absolution in this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not intend by it what he imagined upon him. Rather he intended by it that the thing they divide may have in what one of them took a right of his companion, so its taking is unlawful upon him, and its benefiting unlawful upon him. When he absolves him of it, benefiting by it becomes lawful for him, while it would have been unlawful upon him had that absolution not been. What they were in admitted no freeing for them of any of its means besides that, for they could not contract sale in it, since each of them did not know what he seeks to sell of that. When that in sale was not possible, in gift and charity it was likewise also. Each of them was further in operation in that than sale's operation in it. What was possible in that was absolution, of its being in the hand of one for whom benefiting by it is not lawful. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them with what was possible in that, transferring them thereby from a state of unlawfulness before it to a state of lawfulness after it. That was a favor from Allah upon him in his judgment, and with Allah is success."
"863","Chapter 113 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him, upon him be peace, concerning what is intended by Allah the Exalted's statement: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, People of the House, and purify you thoroughly}. Who are they? 761 - Ar-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, Bukayr ibn Mismar narrated to us, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father who said: When this verse came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn, upon them be peace, and said: O Allah, these are my family. So in this hadith is that those intended by what is in this verse are the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn."
"864","762 - Fahd narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abdulhamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Jafar ibn Abdurrahman al-Bajali, from Hakim ibn Sad, from Umm Salama who said: This verse came down concerning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn, upon them be peace: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, People of the House, and purify you thoroughly}. So in this hadith is the like of what is in the first."
"865","763 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Musa ibn Yaqub az-Zamai narrated to us, Ibn Hashim ibn Utba narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Wahb, from Umm Salama that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered Fatima, al-Hasan and al-Husayn, then entered them under his garment, then implored Allah the Exalted: Lord, these are my family. Umm Salama said: So I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you enter me with them? He said: You are of my family. So in this hadith is the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umm Salama, in answer to her when she said to him: Do you enter me with them: You are of my family. That was of what may be he intended thereby that she is of his family because she is of his wives, and his wives are his family, as he said in the hadith of al-Ifk which has:"
"866","764 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from az-Zuhri, from Urwa, Said, Alqama and Ubaydullah, from Aisha that the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, in the hadith of al-Ifk, stood upon the pulpit seeking excuse from Abdullah ibn Ubayy, saying: O company of Muslims, who excuses me for a man whose harm reached my family? By Allah, I knew of my family only good. They mentioned a man of whom I knew only good, and he would not enter upon my family except with me. So His statement: {Who excuses me for a man whose harm reached me in my family} means in his wife in whom was his harm. In that was what indicated a wife is named by this name. So it is probable His statement to Umm Salama: You are of my family, is also of this meaning: that she is of the people of the recited verse in this chapter. Of what indicates that is what has:"
"867","765 - Al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari al-Kufi narrated to us, Mukhawwal ibn Mukhawwal ibn Rashid al-Hannat narrated to us, Abduljabbar ibn Abbas ash-Shibami narrated to us, from Ammar ad-Duhni, from Amra daughter of Afa, from Umm Salama who said: This verse came down in my house: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, People of the House, and purify you thoroughly}, meaning concerning seven: Jibril, Mikail, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, Fatima, al-Hasan and al-Husayn, upon them be peace. I was at the house door, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, am I not of the People of the House? He said: You are of the Prophet's wives, upon him be peace. He did not say: You are of the People of the House."
"868","766 - And what al-Husayn also narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Jafar al-Ahmar narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Salama, and Abdulmalik, from Ata, from Umm Salama who said: Fatima came with food of hers to her father while he was at his dwellings. So he said: O my daughter, bring me my children, my son and thy cousin. She said: Then he covered them, or she said: he wrapped the cloak over them, and said: These are my household and my kin; remove impurity from them and purify them thoroughly. Umm Salama said: O Messenger of Allah, am I with them? He said: You are of the Prophet's wives, upon him be peace, and you are upon good, or toward good."
"869","767 - And what Abu Umayya narrated to us, Bakr ibn Yahya ibn Zabban narrated to us, Mindal narrated to us, from Abi al-Jahhaf, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Salama who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in my house, then Fatima, upon her be peace, came to him with khazira. So he said: Call me thy husband. So she called him and her two sons. Then he came with a cloak and wrapped them in it, took its edge with his hand, then raised his hands and said: O Allah, these are my progeny and my household, so remove impurity from them and purify them thoroughly. She said: So I raised the cloak and entered my head in it, saying: Me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You are upon good."
"870","768 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Atiyya, from Abi Said, from Umm Salama who said: This verse came down in my house: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, People of the House, and purify you thoroughly}. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, am I not of the People of the House? He said: You are upon good; you are of the Prophet's wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the house were Ali, Fatima, al-Hasan and al-Husayn."
"871","769 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Salama that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatima: Bring me your husband and your two sons. She brought them, so he cast over them a Fadak cloak, then placed his hand over them, then said: O Allah, these are the family of Muhammad, so place Your prayers and blessings upon the family of Muhammad, indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. Umm Salama said: So I lifted the cloak to enter with them, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pulled it and said: You are upon good."
"872","770 - What Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us... and what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us... both said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to us: Shahr narrated to us, he said: I heard Umm Salama when the news of the killing of al-Husayn ibn Ali came, saying: They killed him, may Allah kill them, they stripped him and humiliated him, may Allah curse them. For I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Fatima came to him in the morning with a pot in which she had made porridge, carrying it on a tray until she placed it before him. He said to her: Where is your cousin? She said: He is in the house. He said: Go, call him and bring me your two sons. She went leading her two sons, each of them, with Ali walking behind them until they entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He seated the two boys in his lap, Ali sat on his right and Fatima on his left. Umm Salama said: He pulled from beneath me a Hibara cloak that was our spread over the bed in Madina, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrapped it over all of them, took with his left the two ends of the cloak and raised his right hand to his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, saying: O Allah, remove defilement from them and purify them thoroughly, three times. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, am I not of your household? He said: Yes indeed. He said: So enter into the cloak. She said: So I entered after he had finished his supplication for his cousin Ali and his two sons and his daughter Fatima, upon them be peace."
"873","771 - What Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan al-Wasiti Abu Ishaq narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Aban al-Wasiti narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubayd al-Makki, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Umar ibn Abi Salama who said: This verse was revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the house of Umm Salama: Allah only intends to remove defilement from you, O people of the house, and to purify you thoroughly. She said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called al-Hasan, al-Husayn and Fatima and seated them before him, and called Ali and seated him behind his back, then covered all of them with the cloak, then said: O Allah, these are my household, so remove defilement from them and purify them thoroughly. Umm Salama said: O Allah, make me of them. He said: You are in your place, and you are upon good."
"874","772 - What Fahd narrated to us: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Abu Sakhr, from Abu Muawiya al-Bajali, from Amra al-Hamdaniya who said: I came to Umm Salama and greeted her. She said: Who are you? I said: Amra al-Hamdaniya. Amra said: O Mother of the Believers, inform me about this man killed amongst us, loved and hated, meaning Ali ibn Abi Talib. Umm Salama said: Do you love him or hate him? She said: I neither love him nor hate him. She said: Allah revealed this verse: Allah only intends... to the end of it, while in the house there was none but Gabriel, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn, upon them be peace. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, am I of the people of the house? He said: You have with Allah good. I wished he had said yes, for it would have been dearer to me than all the sun rises and sets upon. This shows from these reports that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umm Salama did not mean she was of those intended by the verse recited in this chapter, and that those intended by it are: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali, Fatima, Hasan and Husayn, upon them be peace, to the exclusion of others. And likewise what indicates the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying to Umm Salama in these reports: You are of my household."
"875","773 - What Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami and Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, both said: Bishr ibn Bakr al-Bajali narrated to us, from al-Awzai: Abu Ammar informed me: Wathila narrated to me, he said: I came to Ali but did not find him, so Fatima said: He went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to invite him. He said: So he came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they entered and I entered with them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called al-Hasan and al-Husayn, seating each on his thigh, brought Fatima and her husband near his lap, then wrapped a garment over them while I stood apart, then said: Allah only intends... the verse, then said: O Allah, these are my household, they are the people of truth. I said: O Messenger of Allah, am I of your household? He said: And you are of my household. Wathila said: It is among my greatest hopes. Wathila is further from him (peace be upon him) than Umm Salama, for he is only a man of Banu Layth, not of Quraysh, while Umm Salama's standing in Quraysh is what it is. So his saying to Wathila: You are of my household, is in the sense of your following me and believing in me, so by that you entered into my company. And we find Allah mentioning in His Book what proves this meaning, in His saying: And Nuh called upon his Lord and said: My Lord, indeed my son is of my household, and He answered him: Indeed he is not of your household, indeed it is unrighteous conduct. So as He could exclude him from his household though his son, for opposing him in religion, so He may include in his household whoever agrees with him in religion though not of his kin. Likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered Umm Salama: You are of my household, may bear this meaning. Likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered Umm Salama: You are of my household, may be upon this meaning also, and his saying that to her is like his saying the like to Wathila. And the hadith of Sad and what we mentioned with it of the hadiths at the beginning of this chapter make known thereby who are the people of the recited verse therein, for we know comprehensively that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invited whom he invited of his household at its revelation, none of its intended people remained besides them. Since that is so, it is impossible that others enter with them in what it was intended with. In what we mentioned of that is the explanation of what we described. If one says: The Book of Allah proves the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are meant by that verse, for He said before it in its sura: O Prophet, say to your wives: If you desire, to His saying: O wives of the Prophet, you are not, to His saying: the former ignorance. All that was meant with them, for it is upon feminine address, not masculine. Then He said: Allah only intends to remove defilement from you, the verse. Our answer: What he recited to the end of what precedes His saying: Allah only intends, the verse, is address to his wives. Then He followed that with His address to his household with His saying: Allah only intends to remove, the verse. It came upon masculine address, for He said therein: to remove defilement from you, O people of the house, and to purify you. Such is masculine address. What precedes it came with the nun, likewise feminine address. So we understood His saying: Allah only intends to remove, the verse, is address to whom He meant thereby of men, to inform them of His honoring them and raising their measure, that He made their women of what He described, for what He described them with in the recited verses before what He addressed them with. And of what proves that also:"
"876","774 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he went out for the dawn prayer, would say: The prayer, O people of the house: Allah only intends... the verse."
"877","775 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from Ubada - Abu Jafar said, he is Ibn Muslim al-Fazari of Kufa, from whom Abu Nuaym narrated - he said: Abu Dawud narrated to me - Abu Jafar said, he is Nufay al-Hamdani the blind, also of Kufa - he said: Abu al-Hamra narrated to me, he said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nine months; when morning came he would come to the door of Fatima, upon her be peace, and say: Peace be upon you, O people of the house: Allah only intends to remove defilement from you, O people of the house, the verse. In this too is proof of who the people of this house are, and with Allah is success."
"878","Chapter 114 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) affirming ill-omen and what was narrated from him denying it. 776 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamza and Salim, sons of Abdullah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) who said: Ill-omen is only in three: in woman, horse and house."
"879","777 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: al-Qanabi narrated to us: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"880","778 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except he did not mention Hamza. In this hadith is affirmation of ill-omen in these three things, yet there is narrated from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), on that what contradicts this meaning."
"881","779 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us: Utba ibn Muslim narrated to me, from Hamza ibn Abdullah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: If ill-omen be in anything, it is in three: in horse, dwelling and woman. What is in this is conditional: if ill-omen exists, it is in these three things, not affirming it in them. And what is in this hadith agrees with what was narrated from Jabir and Sahl ibn Sad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this meaning."
"882","780 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: If ill-omen be in anything, it is in three: in woman, horse and house."
"883","781 - As al-Kaysani narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Hazim that he heard Sahl ibn Sad narrating, from the Prophet (peace be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"884","782 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr who heard Jabir narrating, from the Prophet (peace be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it exactly. And there is narrated from Aisha her rejection of that and her report that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it only reporting from the people of ignorance that they used to say it, except she mentioned it from him (peace be upon him) with tiyara not shu'm, and the meaning is one. Since that is so, what was narrated from her of what she preserved from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), attaching that speech to the people of ignorance, is more worthy than what was narrated from others from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for she preserved from him therein what others fell short of preserving from him, so she is worthier than others thereby, especially as there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the negation of tiyara and ill-omen."
"885","783 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no ghoul, no tiyara and no ill-omen."
"886","784 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: There is no contagion, no Safar and no ghoul. In that is proof of the removal of the statement attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirming ill-omen in the three things we narrated from him that ill-omen is in them. And there is narrated from him (peace be upon him) also the negation of ill-omen, and that its opposite, good fortune, may be in these three things."
"887","785 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Jabir al-Tai, from Muawiya ibn Hakim, from his uncle Mikhmar ibn Muawiya who said: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: There is no ill-omen, but good fortune may be in woman, horse and riding-beast. Thus he said, and it is possible the place of riding-beast is house, and Allah knows best. In that is confirmation of what we mentioned of the removal of affirming ill-omen in these things, and with Allah is success. As for the hadith of Aisha which we mentioned in this chapter."
"888","786 - What Ali ibn Mabad ibn Nuh al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Hassan who said: Two men of Banu Amir entered upon Aisha and informed her that Abu Hurayra narrates from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: Tiyara is in woman, house and horse. She became angry, a part of her flying to the sky and a part to the earth, and said: By the One who sent down the Quran upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never said it at all; he only said: The people of ignorance used to take omens from that, and Allah knows best."
"889","Chapter 115 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on the ghoul, affirming it and denying it. 787 - Bakkar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi narrated to us; and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother Isa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Ayyub that he was in a store-room of his and the ghoul would come and take, so he complained to the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said to him: When you see her, say: In the name of Allah, answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he seized her and she swore not to return. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said to him: What did your captive do? He said: She swore not to return. He said: She lied and she will return. He did that two or three times; each time he seized her she swore not to return, and he would come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who would say: What did your captive do? He would say: She swore not to return. He would say: She lied and she will return. Then he seized her and she said to him: I will teach you something; when you say it nothing will come near you: Ayat al-Kursi, recite it. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: What did your captive do? He said: She said: Ayat al-Kursi, recite it, for nothing will come near you. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said to him: She told the truth though she is a liar. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirming the ghoul, while we mentioned in the chapter before this from him that he said: There is no ghoul, therein negating the ghoul. One may say this is contradiction. It is said to him: It is not, by praise of Allah, contradiction, for it is possible the ghoul was as in the hadith of Abu Ayyub, then Allah removed it from His servants."
"890","Chapter 116 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his saying: Leave birds upon their roosts. 788 - al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Sibaa ibn Thabit, from Umm Kurz who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiya and heard him saying: Leave birds upon their roosts. I heard al-Muzani saying: al-Shafii said on his saying: Leave birds upon their roosts: one of them, when he left his house in the morning intending an affair, would fly the first bird he saw; if it passed from his left veering to his right he said: These are birds of good omen, so he proceeded in his need, seeing he would succeed in it; if it passed from his right to his left he said: These are birds of ill omen, so he turned back and said: This is an ill-omened need. If he saw no passing bird but saw a bird in its nest, he moved it from its nest to fly, to see which path it takes, of ill omen or of good omen. So his saying: Leave birds in their roosts resembles: do not move them, for moving them and what you do of tiyara avails nothing; what avails in what you turn to is Allah's decree."
"891","Chapter 117 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him on his commanding Ali ibn Abi Talib in his hajj to stand over his sacrificial camels and what he commanded him therein and addressed him with. 789 - Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to stand over his camels, to divide their skins and coverings, and commanded me not to give the butcher anything from them, saying: We shall pay him from ourselves. It is possible the Abd al-Karim from whom this hadith is narrated is Abd al-Karim ibn Malik al-Jazari, who is a proof with the people of hadith. And it is possible he is Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, who is not with them a proof in hadith, so we investigated that to know its reality."
"892","790 - Yunus ibn Mabad narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ali who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to the butcher who slaughtered his camels and commanded me to give in charity their cloths, meats and skins, and not to give him anything thereof, saying: I shall pay him from other than that."
"893","791 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us: Sayf ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us: Mujahid narrated to us: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me: Ali narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me concerning his camels, their meats, so I divided them; he commanded me concerning their coverings, so I divided them; and he commanded me concerning their skins, so I divided them."
"894","792 - Yazid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: Ibn Abi Najih narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ali who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me over the camels, then he mentioned the like of it."
"895","793 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me that Mujahid informed him that Ibn Abi Layla informed him that Ali informed him that the Prophet of Allah commanded him to stand over his camels and commanded him to divide all his camels, their meats, coverings and skins, among the poor, and not to give for their slaughter anything thereof. I said to al-Hasan: Did he name whom to divide that among? He said: No. In this hadith is the explanation that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding Ali from giving the butcher anything thereof was concerning his slaughter-wage which he deserved, and he did not mean by it not to give him if he was poor thereof as he gives others of the poor thereof."
"896","794 - al-Hasan ibn Bakr al-Marwazi narrated to us: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us: Israil informed us: Abd al-Karim informed us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ali who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) gifted one hundred camels, among them the camel of Abu Jahl bridled with a silver nose-ring; sixty of them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning he slaughtered them with his hand and gave Ali forty, saying: Give in charity their coverings and do not give the butcher anything thereof. One asked about the benefits in these reports from points of fiqh. Our answer: In them are eight benefits of that kind. Among them: That the Prophet (peace be upon him), his ruling on his camels was to delegate another to slaughter them for him, that slaughter by his deputy being mingled with his intention without an intention of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) mingling with it. Had he slaughtered them himself he would need his intention for what he intends therewith to mingle with his slaughter, and this deputizing suffices for that meaning, a weighty chapter of fiqh. Also his commanding Ali to give in charity the cloths and halters of his camels, showing what is intended for camels of coverings and halters returns to their ruling and is treated as they are treated. Also his permitting Ali to hire one to slaughter them for a wage either in his liability or in the liability of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not in kind, and that he may own labor not in kind over the butcher for a wage not in kind which the butcher owns for his slaughter, differing between that and contracts in sales on things not in kind for replacements not in kind, and his returning that in sale contracts to debt for debt which he (peace be upon him) forbade."
"897","795 - As Bakkar and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubayda al-Rabadhi, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. It is debt for debt. The people of hadith tolerated this hadith from the narration of Musa ibn Ubayda though it has what it has, a weighty chapter of fiqh too. In it also: That the camels, he had concerning what he slaughtered thereof and Ali had concerning what he slaughtered thereof from him, that they eat of their meats, and they did so and ate of their meats."
"898","796 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Hatim narrated to us: Jafar narrated to us, from his father who said: We entered upon Jabir ibn Abdullah and he narrated to us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his hajj on the day of sacrifice turned to the slaughter-place and slaughtered sixty-three with his hand, gave to Ali who slaughtered the rest and associated him in his offering, then ordered from every camel a piece to be placed in a pot and cooked, and they ate of its meat and drank of its broth. In it also his (peace be upon him) permitting partnership in offerings, and his permitting eating thereof. In it what shows the wage for what a man hires for another falls upon the agent who undertook the hiring, not upon the principal for whom it was undertaken; for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed Ali not to give him of the camel meats for his wage, and had that not been upon Ali he would have no need to forbid him, for he would not be liable for it; the wage is upon his principal for what he undertook meriting wage. In it also his permitting use of silver in the nose-ring for offerings, unlike using it in eating and drinking, and Allah we ask for success."
"899","Chapter 118 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his saying: There came to you the people of Yemen, they are softest of hearts and tenderest of minds; faith is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni; and who are the people of Yemen he meant thereby? 797 - al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: There came to you the people of Yemen, softest of hearts and tenderest of minds; faith is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni."
"900","798 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: The people of Yemen came, they are the most tender of people in minds; faith is Yemeni, understanding is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni."
"901","799 - Fahd narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani and Abdullah ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin who said: Abu Hurayra narrated to us, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"902","800 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Faith is Yemeni, disbelief is toward the east, tranquility is in the people of sheep, and pride and show are in the loud-voiced people of horses and camels."
"903","801 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us: Ubayda ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni; there came to you the people of Yemen, they are softest in minds and tenderest in hearts."
"904","802 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: The people of Yemen came, they are most tender in minds; faith is Yemeni, understanding is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni. Abu Umayya narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Awn that Muhammad used to raise this hadith from the hadith of Abu Hurayra, saying it from the Prophet (peace be upon him). In what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) is his mention of the people of Yemen with what he mentioned them in this hadith. Some went to that he meant thereby the people of Tihama, among them Sufyan ibn Uyayna, as Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan said: He means by his saying: There came to you the people of Yemen, the people of Tihama, for Makka is Yemen and it is Tihami. So we examined what they said of that, whether it is as they said or not."
"905","803 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Masud al-Ansari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with his hand toward Yemen and said: Faith is here; indeed harshness and hardness of hearts are in the loud-voiced owners of camels where the horn of Satan rises, in Rabia and Mudar. So he attached harshness and hardness of hearts to the loud-voiced of Rabia and Mudar, in that proof that those to whom faith, wisdom and understanding are attached are their opposites who are not of Rabia nor Mudar. In that what should be that he meant by what is in the reports of the first section the people of Tihama, for those or most of them are of Mudar. Then we found from him (peace be upon him) on this meaning what is clearer than this hadith."
"906","804 - It is what Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ruayni narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Kalai al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us, from Abu Hamza al-Ansi of Hims - Abu Jafar said: he is Isa ibn Sulaym al-Rastani; Amr ibn al-Harith, Isa ibn Yunus and others narrated from him - that he narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr al-Hadrami, Rashid ibn Sad al-Maqrai and Shubayb al-Kalai, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Amr ibn Abasa who said: Horses were presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) with Uyayna ibn Badr present. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Uyayna: I am more skilled with horses than you. Uyayna said: If you are more skilled with horses than me, I am more skilled with men than you. He said: How? He said: The best of men who wore cloaks, placed their swords on their shoulders and laid spears across the withers of their horses are the men of Najd. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You lie; rather they are the people of Yemen, faith is Yemeni to Lakhm, Judham and Amila, the food of Himyar is better than its produce, Hadramawt is better than Banu al-Harith, and he named the chiefs the fettered. In what we narrated in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) is his clarification of the people of Yemen he meant by what is in the first reports, that they are the people of these Yemeni tribes, not others."
"907","805 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of al-Hudaybiya, and he mentioned a long hadith in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall come peoples whose deeds you will deem your deeds small beside theirs. We said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah, Quraysh? He said: No; the people of Yemen, they are tenderest in minds and softest in hearts. We said: Are they better than us, O Messenger of Allah? He said: If one of them had a mountain of gold and spent it, he would not reach your mudd nor its half. The superiority between us and people is this verse: Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest, the verse. In this is proof of the reality of the people of Yemen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in the first section, who they are, and that they are other than the people of Tihama as Ibn Uyayna mentioned."
"908","806 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Mani narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Humayd informed us, from Anas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall come a people more tender than you in minds. Then the Asharis came, among them Abu Musa, and they began chanting rajaz. In that is proof also that the intended people of Yemen as in the first reports are the Asharis and their likes of those coming from true Yemen, to the exclusion of others."
"909","807 - Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas who said: When the people of Yemen came, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: The people of Yemen have come, they are softer in hearts than you, and they are the first who came with handshaking. What is in this chapter of reports is many; we sufficed with what we brought thereof in this chapter by which is clarified what we mentioned of the reality of the intended people of Yemen therein, and that they are not the people of Tihama as Ibn Uyayna said, and Allah we ask for success."
"910","Chapter 119 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) in his saying: The best reciter of them - meaning his community - of the Book of Allah is Ubayy ibn Kab, the most knowledgeable of them in inheritance is Zayd, and the most knowledgeable of them in lawful and unlawful is Muadh ibn Jabal. 808 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Affan narrated to us: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: The most merciful of my community to my community is Abu Bakr, the sternest of them in Allah's command is Umar, the truest of them in modesty is Uthman, the best reciter of them of the Book of Allah is Ubayy ibn Kab, the most knowledgeable of them in inheritance is Zayd ibn Thabit, the most knowledgeable of them in lawful and unlawful is Muadh ibn Jabal. Indeed every community has a trustee; indeed the trustee of this community is Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah."
"911","809 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Qabisa ibn Uqba narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha and Asim, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it, except he did not mention in his hadith: And the best reciter of them of the Book of Allah is Ubayy ibn Kab."
"912","810 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Khalaf ibn al-Walid al-Ataki narrated to us: al-Ashjai narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him)... the like of it, except he said: The most knowledgeable in inheritance is Zayd and the most knowledgeable in lawful and unlawful is Muadh. One asked about what is meant by each of Ubayy, Zayd and Muadh in this hadith, and does that require him to be above the Rightly-Guided Caliphs and the rest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the meaning he was mentioned with? Our answer: Whoever is eminent in rank in a meaning, it is allowed to say he is the best of people in that meaning, even if among them is one like him or above him. Of that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he said to Ali: That the most wretched of them shall kill him, meaning mankind."
"913","811 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq. And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Wahb informed me: Muhammad ibn Salama narrated to us: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Muhammad ibn Khuthaym, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, from Muhammad ibn Khuthaym, from Ammar ibn Yasir who said: I and Ali were companions in a campaign - Ahmad ibn Dawud mentioned it in his hadith and Ahmad ibn Shuayb did not - when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted there and stayed, we saw people of Banu Mudlij working at a spring or palms of theirs. Ali said to me: O Abu al-Yaqzan, shall we go to them and see how they work? I said: If you wish. We came to them and looked at their work a while, then sleep overcame us, so I and Ali went until we lay in the shade of a wall of palms in fine dust, and slept. By Allah, none woke us but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moving us with his foot, while we were dustied from that dust we slept in. That day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: What is with you, O Abu Turab, for what he saw of dust on him, then said: Shall I not tell you of the most wretched of people? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The red man of Thamud who hamstrung the she-camel, and the one who shall strike you, O Ali, on this - placing his hand on his forehead - until he wets this from it, taking his beard. Then of that also is what was narrated from Ali, not attributed to the Prophet (peace be upon him), though we know he did not say it by opinion nor derivation, since the like of it is not said thus."
"914","As Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nuaym narrated to us: Fitr ibn Khalifa narrated to us: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us, he said: Ali called the people to allegiance, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam came and he turned him back twice, then said: What holds back the most wretched of them, he shall dye or place this from this, his beard from his head, then he recited these two verses. We know Ibn Muljam was of the people of monotheism, and what was from him by which he is called absolutely the most wretched of people is the enormity of what he did, the gravity of his crime and his rent in Islam. And we know with that that more wretched than him is one who never professed Allah's unity an hour, ascribed a son to Allah and met Allah upon that, being in wretchedness above Ibn Muljam. Of that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Kharijites, among whom is Ibn Muljam."
"915","812 - As Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti narrated to us: Abu al-Mughira Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj al-Khawlani narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Qatada, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) - in his describing the Kharijites with prayer and fasting - then he said: They shall pass out of religion as the arrow passes out of the game, the worst of creation."
"916","813 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awzai... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. And we know that whoever ascribes a son to Allah or associates with Him and kills His prophets and belies His messengers is worse than them, for the enormity of what was from them. So it is allowed thereby to say they are the worst of creation, and allowed for one distinguished among them by what he did to Ali to be said: He is the most wretched of mankind, even if therein is one like him in wretchedness or above him. So likewise what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on each of Ubayy, Zayd and Muadh in the hadith we narrated at the head of this chapter: it is allowed to apply that for him upon what is in the hadith, for the eminence of his measure in the meaning attached to him therein and the loftiness of his rank therein, even if it is possible that among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is one like him or above him in that meaning. This is for the latitude of language and the knowledge of the addressed of the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he addressed them therein. Were it not so, it would not be allowed to say to one eminent in knowledge: He is the most knowledgeable of people, since the sayer does not know all people nor the measures of their knowledge. Since that is allowed him despite falling short of knowing all people and their knowledge, when he knows none like the one he described, we understand thereby that what is meant by the like of it is whom the sayer knows, and that combining all people in his saying is allowed upon metaphor, not reality."
"917","Chapter 120 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his forbidding swearing by other than Allah, and what was narrated from him of his swearing by other than Him, and what was abrogated of its opposite. 814 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Muqri narrated to us: Zaida ibn Qudama narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey of his, or he said: on travel, and I said: No, by my father. A man behind me said: Do not swear by your fathers. I turned, and behold it was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"918","815 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar... then he mentioned the like of it."
"919","816 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us: Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard Umar saying: By my father, by my father. He said: Indeed Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers. Umar said: By Allah, I never swore thereby after that, neither remembering nor quoting."
"920","817 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us: Ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab: Salim ibn Abdullah informed me that Abdullah ibn Umar informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Indeed Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers. Umar said: By Allah, I never swore thereby since I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding it, nor uttered it."
"921","818 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that the Prophet (peace be upon him) found Umar in a company swearing by his father, and said: Indeed Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers; let one who swears swear by Allah or be silent."
"922","819 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found him in a company swearing by his father... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"923","820 - Yazid narrated to us: al-Qanabi narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not swear by your fathers; whoever swears, let him swear by Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He said: Quraysh used to swear by their fathers, so he said: Do not swear by your fathers. In these reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his (peace be upon him) forbidding swearing by other than Allah. And other reports are narrated from him therein containing his swearing by other than Allah, among them:"
"924","821 - What Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abu Suhayl Nafi ibn Malik, from his father, from Talha that a Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) disheveled, saying: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of what Allah imposed on me of prayer. He said: The five prayers, unless you volunteer something. He said: Inform me of what Allah imposed on me of fasting. He said: Fasting the month of Ramadan, unless you volunteer something. He said: Inform me of what Allah imposed on me of zakat. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him of the laws of Islam. He said: By the One who honored you with truth, I shall neither volunteer nor decrease from what Allah imposed on me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He prospered, by his father, if he is truthful; he entered Paradise, by his father, if he is truthful. And among them:"
"925","822 - What Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Kufi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us: Umara - he is Ibn al-Qaqa - narrated to us, from Abu Zura - he is Ibn Amr ibn Jarir - who said: I heard Abu Hurayra saying: From the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: A man came to him saying: O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? He said: Indeed, by your father, you shall be told: That you give charity while healthy, miserly, fearing poverty and hoping richness, and do not delay until when it reaches the throat you say: For so-and-so such, and for so-and-so such, while it is for so-and-so. And among them:"
"926","823 - What Abu Umayya narrated to us: Abu Nuaym narrated to us: Uqba ibn Wahb ibn Uqba al-Amiri narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating from al-Fujay that he came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying to him: What is lawful for us of carrion? He said: What is your food? He said: We take morning milk and evening milk. Uqba explained to me: a cup in the morning and a cup in the evening. He said: That, by the father of hunger. So he permitted them carrion in this state. In these established reports is permission of what prohibition came of in the first. One ignorant of the aspects of the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is severe contradiction. Our answer: There is no contradiction therein, but two different meanings, one in a time and the other in another time, the latter abrogating the former, unobjectionable, since the Book of Allah contains what abrogated other therein. Then we sought which of them abrogates the other."
"927","824 - Salih ibn Shuayb ibn Aban al-Basri informed us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Masudi: Mabad ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Yasar, from Qutayla bint Sayfi al-Juhaniya who said: A rabbi of the rabbis came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Muhammad, good people are you, were it not you associate. He said: Glory be to Allah. He said: You say when you swear: By the Kaba. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) waited a while, then said: He said: Whoever swears, let him swear by the Lord of the Kaba. In this hadith is mention of the cause of the forbidding by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of swearing by other than Allah, and in that proof that the later of the two different meanings we mentioned in this chapter is the forbidding of swearing by other than Allah, not the permitting it. So is clarified, by praise of Allah, contrary to what this ignorant imagined, and Allah we ask for success."
"928","Chapter 121 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on one who swears by other than Allah, what is his ruling therein? 825 - Bakkar narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us: Abu Awana narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Sad ibn Ubayda who said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar and he heard a man saying: Nay, by my father. He said: Umar used to swear thereby, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: It is shirk, so do not swear thereby."
"929","826 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us: Israil narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Ibn Umar, from Umar who said: No, by my father. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears by something besides Allah has associated. In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) is that whoever swears by something besides Allah has associated. That with us, and Allah knows best, did not mean the shirk by which one exits Islam so its doer is outside Islam, but it meant one should not swear by other than Allah, and whoever swore by other than Allah made what he swore by as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sworn by, thereby making whom he swore by or what he swore by a partner in what is sworn by, grievous. So he was made an associator thereby, a shirk other than the shirk by which one is a disbeliever in Allah, outside Islam. Like that is what was narrated from him on tiyara."
"930","827 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Isa ibn Asim al-Asadi, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abdullah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tiyara is shirk, and none of us, but Allah removes it by trust."
"931","828 - As Yazid narrated to us: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us: Shuba narrated to us, from Salama, from Isa, a man of Banu Asad, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abdullah ibn Masud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"932","829 - As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir and Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, both said: Shuba narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except he said: And none of us except that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, removes it by trust. What was meant by that shirk was not disbelief in Allah, but that something whose doing Allah, Mighty and Majestic, undertook was said of: if you wish its doing, it is such of what omens are taken from. So likewise the shirk mentioned in the first hadith is of the genus of this shirk, not of shirk in Allah which necessitates disbelief in Him. Then we examined the hadith of Ibn Umar we narrated in this chapter from the two hadiths of al-Amash and Said ibn Masruq, from Sad ibn Ubayda, and found it of corrupt isnad, and that is:"
"933","830 - Because Ibn Marzuq said: Wahb narrated to us: Shuba narrated to us, from Mansur, from Sad ibn Ubayda who said: I was with Ibn Umar, then I rose leaving with him a man of Kinda. I came to Said ibn al-Musayyib, then he came alarmed saying: A man came to Ibn Umar saying to him: May I swear by the Kaba? He said: No, but swear by the Lord of the Kaba, for Umar used to swear by his father, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not swear by your fathers; whoever swears by other than Allah has associated."
"934","831 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Sad ibn Ubayda who said: I and a companion of mine of Kinda were sitting with Ibn Umar, then I rose and sat with Ibn al-Musayyib. My companion came to me saying: Rise to me, his color changed and his face yellowed. I said to him: Did I not leave you just now? Said said: Rise to your companion. I rose to him and he said: Did you not see what Ibn Umar said? I said: What did he say? He said: A man came to him saying: May I swear by the Kaba? He said: No, why swear by the Kaba? Swear by the Lord of the Kaba, for Umar swore by his father with the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said to him: Do not swear by your father, for whoever swears by other than Allah has associated. So we found Mansur ibn al-Mutamir added in the isnad of this hadith over al-Amash and Said ibn Masruq, from Sad ibn Ubayda, an unknown man between him and Ibn Umar in this hadith, so its isnad is thereby corrupt. Yet we mentioned in its interpretation what, if sound, its interpretation is what we mentioned therein, and Allah we ask for success."
"935","Chapter 122 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of what he commanded one who swears by al-Lat and al-Uzza to say. 832 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us; and Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, both said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad who said: I swore by al-Lat and al-Uzza when the covenant was recent, and came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: I swore by al-Lat and al-Uzza when the covenant was recent. He said: You said vain speech; spit to your left three times, say: There is no god but Allah alone, seek Allah's forgiveness and do not return. We examined this hadith and found therein proof that what was from Sad mentioned therein was because of nearness of covenant, i.e., with their custom was what he swore by, so his swearing was upon what his custom ran with until he said what he said of what he swore by upon what overcame his heart, with forgetfulness of Allah's forbidding that upon him by his Islam wherein he is. The root is that a man, when he swears upon what he sees as what he swore upon but it is otherwise - like saying to a man he sees coming: This, by Allah, is Zayd, seeing him so, while he is Amr - his oath is idle, no sin upon him therein, for it enters into the idle which Allah does not take to account. Since idle in the oath itself is this ruling, idle in the thing the swearer sees as sworn by but is not so is likelier to be idle and not taken to account. If one says: In this hadith the Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded Sad not to return to what was from him. It is said: Its meaning with us, and Allah knows best: that he guard himself henceforth lest this occur from him. until such forgetfulness from him be. And there is narrated from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what enters into this meaning."
"936","833 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf that Abu Hurayra said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you swears and says in his oath: By al-Lat, let him say: There is no god but Allah; and whoever says to his companion: Come, I gamble with you, let him give charity."
"937","834 - What Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Kathir ibn Ubayd narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it. What is in this hadith is aimed at particular people, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying therein: Whoever of you swears, i.e., whoever of you used to worship al-Lat and al-Uzza, so this was from him upon what his custom ran before his Islam, then he forgot in his Islam until this was from him, that he follow that with Allah's unity and that there is no god besides Him, and Allah we ask for success."
"938","Chapter 123 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on one who swears by a religion other than Islam lying. 835 - Yunus narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us: al-Awzai narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us: Abu Qilaba al-Jarmi narrated to me: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Whoever swears by a religion other than the religion of Islam lying, he is as he said."
"939","836 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi Abu Bakr narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya who said: Abu Qilaba narrated to me: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak narrated to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. We examined this hadith and found therein a fine point of fiqh: that whoever swears saying he is a Jew if such-and-such for what he knows has been, what he suspended is meaningless, for suspending oaths on past things is thus - like a man saying: My wife is divorced if such was, for what he knows has been, his wife is divorced, as one who said: My wife is divorced without suspending on anything. So likewise whoever says: He is a Jew if such-and-such for what has been, he is thereby as one who said: He is a Jew, thereby an apostate. That is not the ruling in future things as this meaning; for a man saying: He is a Jew if such be, is not thereby a disbeliever, for in his oath he did not presently impose Judaism on himself, he imposed it only if what he swore upon be, as one saying to his wife: If such be, you are divorced, he is not presently divorcing her. So is clarified that swearing by a religion other than Islam in the hadith we narrated is only in swearing thereby on past things, not on future things, and with Allah is success."
"940","Chapter 124 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on the vow, that it neither hastens nor delays anything. 837 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Sufyan narrating from Mansur, from Abdullah ibn Murra, from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade us vowing, saying: It neither hastens nor delays anything, but is extracted thereby from the miser."
"941","838 - Ibn Mabad narrated to us: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abdullah ibn Murra, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it, except he said: Is extracted thereby from the niggardly."
"942","839 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abdullah ibn Murra, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade vowing, and commanded fulfilling it. In what we narrated in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding vowing. It is possible his forbidding it is when it neither hastens nor delays anything, not his forbidding it because it is disobedience, but that it is intended thereby for what avails nothing therein. Proof is his (peace be upon him) commanding fulfillment thereof upon what is in the hadith of Sharik, and his saying in the hadith of Sufyan: But is extracted thereby from the miser, or from the niggardly. And Allah said that in His Book: They fulfill vows and fear a day whose evil is widespread, i.e., if they do not fulfill, as punishment for leaving that."
"943","840 - Yunus narrated to us also: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Abu Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Khuzai narrated to me - Yunus said, meaning Fulayh - that Said ibn al-Harith narrated to him that he heard Ibn Umar when a man of Banu Kab called Masud ibn Amr came to him saying: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, my son was in the land of Persia among those with Umar ibn Ubaydullah al-Taymi, and a severe plague fell in Basra. When that reached me, I vowed if Allah brings my son, he shall walk to the Kaba. He came sick and died, so what do you see? Ibn Umar said: Were you not forbidden vowing?! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The vow neither brings forward nor delays anything; by the vow is only extracted from the miser. Fulfill your vow. He said: I only vowed my son to walk! He said: Fulfill your vow. I said to al-Khuzai: Go to Ibn al-Musayyib then inform me of what he says. He informed me he said to him: Walk on behalf of your son. I said to him: Do you see that sufficing for him? He said: Yes; tell me, if your son left a debt and you paid it for him, do you see that sufficing for him? He said: I said: Yes."
"944","841 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us: Fulayh narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. And there is narrated from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), in this chapter."
"945","842 - What Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah said: The vow does not bring to the son of Adam anything I did not decree for him, but it is something I extract thereby from the miser; he gives Me thereupon what he would not give Me upon miserliness."
"946","843 - What Fahd narrated to us: al-Qanabi narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, from Amr, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The vow does not bring near to the son of Adam anything not decreed, but the vow meets the decree, so is extracted thereby from the miser what he did not intend to bring out. What is in this hadith of Abu Hurayra on the vow, that it neither brings forward nor delays anything, is like what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar of this meaning. In what we narrated from them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), his informing people that what they vow brings near nothing undecreed, is proof that the forbidding mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Umar is meant to inform them not to vow for this meaning by which they seek bringing near what they love, not therein proof that the vow itself by which they seek nearness to Allah is forbidden, and with Allah is success."
"947","Chapter 125 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his saying: Reviling the Muslim is fisq and fighting him is kufr. 844 - Ibn Mabad narrated to us: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ibn Abi Zaida narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Sad, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Reviling the Muslim is fisq and fighting him is kufr."
"948","845 - Ali ibn al-Husayn Abu Ubayd narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Mamar narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Umar ibn Sad who said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it. Zakariya ibn Abi Zaida and Mamar ibn Rashid differed over Abu Ishaq on the son of Sad between him and Sad in this hadith: Zakariya mentioned he is Muhammad, Mamar mentioned he is Umar, and Allah knows the reality thereof of them, who he is."
"949","846 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Shuba narrated to us, from Mansur who said: I heard Abu Wail; and Shuba from al-Amash who said: I heard Abu Wail; and Shuba from Zubayd who said: I heard Abu Wail, from Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Reviling the Muslim is fisq and fighting him is kufr."
"950","847 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: Zubayd narrated to us, from Abu Wail who said: Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it."
"951","848 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. He said: I said to Abu Wail: Did you hear from Abdullah? He said: Yes."
"952","849 - Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us: Abu Abdullah Huraym ibn Misar al-Azdi al-Tirmidhi narrated to me: al-Fudayl ibn Iyad and Mansur informed us, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj saying... then he mentioned the like of it."
"953","850 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Masud, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it. We examined this hadith and found his saying: Reviling the Muslim is fisq, clear in meaning; the fisq meant therein is خروج from the praiseworthy command to the blameworthy command, like Allah's saying on Iblis: So he departed from his Lord's command, i.e., he went out from his Lord's command. Of it is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying on the mouse and what he mentioned with it of what he permitted killing in the sanctuary and ihram: Five are fasiq, killed in the sanctuary and ihram. That fisq from them is their خروج to the harm by which they harm people. And his saying: And fighting him is kufr, is not upon disbelief in Allah so he is thereby an apostate, but upon his covering him thereby and consuming him thereby, for kufr is covering the thing, the covering that consumes it. Of it is Allah's saying: Like rain whose growth pleases the kuffar. There is no disagreement among the people of knowledge of interpretation that the kuffar meant here are the sowers, for they cover what they sow in the earth, the covering by which they consume it. Of what proves that kufr mentioned in this hadith did not mean disbelief in Allah: we find him killing his brother yet not thereby a disbeliever in Allah; since by killing him he is not a disbeliever in Allah, by fighting him he is likelier not to be thereby a disbeliever."
"954","851 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas in his hadith of the sun's eclipse, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: And I saw the Fire, and saw most of its people women. It was said: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: For their kufr. It was said: Do they disbelieve in Allah? He said: They are ungrateful to the husband and ungrateful for kindness; if you do good to one of them a lifetime then she sees something from you, she says: I never saw good from you at all. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made this doing of theirs kufr, for their covering thereby the kindness done to them."
"955","852 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us: Abu Nuaym narrated to us: Qays narrated to us, from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifa ibn Husayn, from Abu Nasr, from Ibn Abbas who said: There was something between al-Aws and al-Khazraj in ignorance, and they recalled what was between them, and some rose against others with swords. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was informed and it was mentioned to him, so he went to them, and this verse was revealed: How do you disbelieve while Allah's verses are recited to you and among you is His Messenger; And hold fast to Allah's rope all together and do not divide. What was from them of fighting, for which Allah revealed at it this verse mentioning what was from them as kufr, was not upon disbelief in Allah, but upon their covering what they were upon before of unity and brotherhood, until what was from them was named kufr, not meant thereby disbelief in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, but the kufr we mentioned besides it. Likewise what was narrated from Ibn Abbas in interpreting Allah's saying: Whoever does not judge by what Allah sent down, those are the disbelievers, upon what he interpreted it."
"956","As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Tawus, from his father who said: It was said to Ibn Abbas: Whoever does not judge by what Allah sent down, those are the disbelievers. He said: It is his kufr, not like one who disbelieved in Allah and the Last Day."
"957","Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Tawus who said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Whoever does not judge by what Allah sent down is a disbeliever. He said: It is his kufr thereby, not like one who disbelieved in Allah, the Last Day, His Books and His Messengers. Likewise also what Abu Hurayra narrated, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"958","853 - As Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri: Haywa ibn Shurayh narrated to us: Jafar ibn Rabia al-Qurashi informed me that Irak ibn Malik informed him that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Do not turn away from your fathers; whoever turns away from his father, it is kufr. That with us, and Allah knows best, is upon like what we mentioned of its like in this chapter. Likewise also what Uqba ibn Amir narrated, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"959","854 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, both said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Jabir: Abu Sallam narrated to me: Khalid ibn Zayd narrated to me, he said: Uqba said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Whoever abandons archery after learning it, turning away from it, it is a blessing he disbelieved. So likewise the kufr by which the Muslim is described from his fighting is this kufr, not disbelief in Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and Allah we ask for success."
"960","Chapter 126 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on one who says to his brother: O disbeliever. 855 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: Abu Zura Wahbullah ibn Rashid al-Hajri informed us: Haywa informed us: Abu al-Aswad informed us, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: When a man says to another: O disbeliever, kufr falls upon one of them."
"961","856 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him; and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it. Thus Yunus narrated it to us in the Muwatta of Malik."
"962","857 - Zakariya ibn Yahya ibn Aban narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih and Miskin ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, both said: al-Layth narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad, from Bukayr, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace be upon him)... the like of it."
"963","858 - Ibn Wahb informed us dictation: Malik informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar the like of it, except he said: When a man says to another: O disbeliever, one of them has disbelieved; if the one said to is a disbeliever, so it is as he said, else the other returns with disbelief."
"964","859 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it."
"965","860 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Affan narrated to us: Sakhr ibn Juwayriya narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it."
"966","861 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us: Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever man declares a man a disbeliever, if he is as he said, else he returns with disbelief."
"967","862 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from his father, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, from Abu Dharr who said: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: No man casts at a man fisq or kufr except it returns upon him if his companion is not so."
"968","863 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abu Mamar narrated to us: Abd al-Warith narrated to us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"969","864 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Ismail ibn Aban al-Warraq narrated to us: Mandal ibn Ali narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Abu Said al-Khudri who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: No man testifies upon a man with disbelief except one of them returns with it; if he is a disbeliever, it is as he said, and if he is not a disbeliever, he has disbelieved by declaring him so."
"970","865 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us: al-Salt ibn Mihran narrated to us: al-Hasan narrated to us: Jundub ibn Abdullah al-Bajali narrated to us in this mosque that Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman narrated to him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of what I fear for you is a man who read the Quran until its splendor was seen upon him and he was a support for Islam, then he lapsed to what Allah wills, stripped out of it, cast it behind his back, went out against his neighbor with the sword and accused him of shirk. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of them is worthier of shirk, the accused or the accuser? He said: No, rather the accuser. We examined what is in this hadith seeking what is meant thereby. We found one who says to his companion: O disbeliever, its meaning is that he is a disbeliever because what he is upon is kufr; if what he is upon is not kufr but faith, then making him a disbeliever makes faith kufr, thereby disbelieving in Allah, for whoever disbelieves in Allah's faith has disbelieved in Allah. Of it is Allah's saying: Whoever disbelieves in faith, his work is vain and he is in the Hereafter of the losers. This is the best we found of this hadith's interpretation, and Allah we ask for success."
"971","Chapter 127 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his forbidding killing the ant, bee, hoopoe and shrike. 866 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Said ibn Salim narrated to us - al-Rabi said: I think him from Ibn Jurayj - from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Four of beasts are not killed: the ant, bee, hoopoe and shrike."
"972","867 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from a man who narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him)."
"973","868 - Bahr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"974","869 - al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Mahdi Abu Tahir narrated to us: Abu Musab narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to me, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade killing four: the hoopoe, shrike, ant and bee. We needed to seek the man between Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Shihab, who he is, that its isnad stand for us from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj as it stood for us from the hadith of Mamar."
"975","870 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Dulabi narrated to us, from Salih ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Ali ibn al-Madini who said: I heard Yahya ibn Main saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I was informed, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned this hadith. Yahya said: It was weak with me, so I erased it. Then he said: I saw it in the book of Sufyan ibn Said, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Labid, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas. And Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani permitted us from al-Ghallabi who said: al-Thawri narrated this hadith, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Labid, from al-Zuhri. Al-Ghallabi said: I heard this from Abu Dawud. So we knew thereby that the unnamed man in this hadith from the narration of Ibn Wahb from Ibn Jurayj we mentioned in this chapter is Ibn Abi Labid. So we understood this hadith is sound for us from Ibn Jurayj's narration as its soundness for us from Mamar's narration. And we found Abu Muawiya narrated it from Ibn Jurayj, differing with Ibn Wahb in its isnad."
"976","871 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us: Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade killing four: killing the hoopoe, shrike, ant and bee. We examined what is in this hadith seeking to derive what is meant thereby. We found the hoopoe what its meat is not benefited from, people deem it foul, and we found no harm from it upon people, so killing it is wanton, not for else. That is forbidden, as was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what of this genus is killed without its right."
"977","872 - As al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafii narrated to us: Sufyan informed us: Amr informed us: Suhayb, freedman of Abdullah ibn Amir, informed us, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a sparrow or above it without its right, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, shall ask him about killing it. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is its right? He said: He slaughters it and eats it, not cutting its head and throwing it."
"978","873 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us: Khalid ibn Yazid al-Kahili narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Aban ibn Salih, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No sparrow at all. Abu Jafar said: As if he means: no sparrow was ever killed wantonly. Abu Bakr said: Nor above it nor below it, except it shall cry to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, on Resurrection Day: O Lord, so-and-so killed me; neither did he benefit by me nor leave me to live in His plenty. So the killer of the hoopoe enters into this meaning, and Allah knows best, and likewise the killer of the shrike, for he cannot gather of its kinds what enables spread in eating their meats, so killing what is thus also returns to wantonness, not else, and its killer incurs the threat in these two hadiths we narrated. As for the bee, it is not of this genus at all, but of what is benefited by, and its killer has no benefit in killing it, so killing it combines two matters: cutting its benefits, and lack of benefiting by it, so its killer's crime exceeds the killer of hoopoe and shrike. As for killing the ant, it has neither benefit with it nor cutting harm thereby, and it is described with a praiseworthy meaning narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."
"979","874 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us. And as Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) that an ant bit a prophet of the prophets, so he ordered the ant village burned. Allah revealed to him: Because an ant bit you, did you burn a community of communities glorifying?"
"980","875 - As Muhammad ibn Uzayz narrated to us: Salama ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab: Abu Salama informed me, from Abu Hurayra that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A prophet of the prophets went out with people seeking rain from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, when they saw an ant raising some of its legs. The Prophet said: Return, for you are answered for this ant. What is thus, killing it cuts the like of these two meanings mentioned in these two hadiths, and its killer thereupon enters into the two hadiths of Abdullah ibn Amr and al-Sharid we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). And there is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the ant, when harm is from it, permission to kill it."
"981","876 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prophet of the prophets alighted under a tree, an ant stung him, so he ordered his gear removed from beneath it, then ordered it burned with fire. Allah revealed to him: Why did you not take a single ant? As if he burned the ant village upon what is in the hadith of Yunus and Bahr we narrated in this chapter returning to Said and Abu Salama. In that is proof of permitting killing what harms of ants, and in what precedes it the forbidding of killing what does not harm thereof. In the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Ibn Jurayj is a meaning differing from the hadith of al-Qasim ibn Abdullah from Abu Musab we narrated in this chapter: in Ibn Wahb's hadith from Ibn Jurayj that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Four of beasts are not killed... then he mentioned them, so thereof proof that others are not of their meaning, for what is confined by number, other than that number does not enter therein. In al-Qasim's hadith from Abu Musab: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing four, so it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing these four not by confining with a number preventing others entering, but aiming the forbidding only at killing them, and their likes may join what is in the hadith thereof or may not. In Ibn Wahb's hadith from Ibn Jurayj is confining what he forbade killing by the number he mentioned therein, so that forbidding therein is aimed at that number. so that forbidding mentioned therein was aimed at that number, not other than it of its genera, and Allah knows best the reality thereof however it was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah we ask for success."
"982","Chapter 128 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his saying: One of you is answered so long as he does not hasten, saying: I supplicated and was not answered. 877 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Ubayd, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: One of you is answered so long as he does not hasten, saying: I supplicated and was not answered."
"983","878 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Ubayd, from Abu Hurayra... the like of it, and he did not raise it."
"984","879 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to us: Haywa ibn Shurayh informed me, from Ibn Ajlan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The servant is answered so long as he does not hasten. It was said: What is his hastening? He said: He says: I called upon Allah but He did not answer, and I called upon Allah but He did not answer."
"985","880 - al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us: Abu Zura Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us: Haywa narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Ajlan narrating from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. One said: We find a man supplicating but not answered, though he did not say this speech mentioned in the hadith by which he is barred from answer. Our answer: What was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith is as narrated, no breaking His word; but the answering therein was not explained to us what it is in this hadith, and it is established for us in another, mentioning therein what it is."
"986","881 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Ibn Thawban, from his father, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Ubada ibn al-Samit who narrated to them that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: There is no Muslim man on earth supplicating Allah, Mighty and Majestic, with a supplication but Allah gives it to him or averts from him like it of evil, so long as he does not supplicate with sin or severing kinship. A man of the people said: Then we shall increase, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Allah is more."
"987","882 - As Fahd narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us: Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ali ibn Ali, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Abu Said who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Muslim servant's supplication is not rejected except by one of three, so long as he does not supplicate with sin or severing kinship, or evil is averted from him by the measure he supplicated. In these two hadiths the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained to us the answering from Allah to who supplicates Him, after what he supplicates is neither sin nor severing kinship: that He gives who supplicated what he supplicated so he knows that, or averts from him of evil what is better for him than what he supplicated, so he does not know that. So is clarified by what we mentioned the meaning of what is in the first hadith, that the answering from Allah to who supplicates Him of His servants with what is allowed him to supplicate is given inevitably, except it is of what we know by agreement: the gift supplicated, so he knows he was answered, or He gives him other than what he supplicated of averting what He averts from him, so the answering was from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, though he did not know it. So is made clear by what we mentioned the explanation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying we mentioned from him in the hadith we narrated at the head of this chapter."
"988","Chapter 129 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on Gabriel's (peace be upon him) delay from him at the time he promised to come to him therein at his house, because of the puppy in his house unknown to him. 883 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi al-Basri Abu Bakr narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymuna who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us languid. I said: O Messenger of Allah, why do I see you languid? He said: Gabriel promised me and never broke me at all. He remained his day and night, while in the house was a puppy under a bed of theirs. He brought it out, then took water with his hand and sprinkled its place. Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to him, saying: What prevented you? He said: We do not enter a house wherein is a dog or image. So he ordered killing dogs; if he was spoken to on the small dog, he would not permit it."
"989","884 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Salama, from Aisha that Gabriel delayed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then came to him. He said to him: What held you? He said: A puppy in your house. They looked, and behold a puppy under the bed. The Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered it brought out."
"990","885 - Fahd narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Gabriel promised the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at an hour to come to him therein. The hour passed and he did not come. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and behold Gabriel (peace be upon him) at the door. He said: What prevents you entering the house? He said: In the house is a dog, and we do not enter a house wherein is a dog or image. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the dog brought out, then ordered dogs to be killed."
"991","886 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Salama, from Aisha who said: Gabriel promised the Prophet (peace upon them both) at an hour to come to him therein. The hour came and he did not come, while in his hand was a small stick. He cast it from his hand saying: Allah does not break His promise nor His messengers. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned, and behold a puppy under the bed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whence this dog? She said: By Allah, I did not know of it. He ordered it brought out, and Gabriel came to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You promised me at an hour and I sat for you but you did not come. He said: The dog in your house prevented me; we do not enter a house wherein is a dog or image."
"992","887 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abu Thabit Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah al-Madini narrated to us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Kurayb, from Usama ibn Zayd who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) with gloom upon him, and asked him about that. He said: Gabriel promised to come to me, and when he promised me he never broke me... and he mentioned it. In what we narrated is that Gabriel promised the Messenger of Allah (peace upon them both) to come to his house at a particular hour without exception in his promising, then delayed coming at it to his house since therein was what prevents his entering it, the dog therein, for in the law he does not enter a house wherein is a dog or image. That was by the law excepted from his promise, though he did not except it with his tongue. So likewise a man promising a man to sit with him in his house when he asks sitting with him therein at a time he names, while in his house at that time is what the law prevents him entering that house with therein, of wine drunk therein or other disobediences the law prevents attending, so he stays from entering his house for that, he does not thereby enter the ruling of one who promised then broke. Likewise one promising his wife intercourse at a time he names, then menses reaches her at that time, so by leaving intercourse he is not in the ruling of one who promised then broke. Likewise a man placing upon himself fasting tomorrow, the night wherein so-and-so arrives, then so-and-so arrives in a night whose morrow is sacrifice day, so he leaves fasting for its sanctity, he is not by leaving that a breaker. He is not thereby blameworthy, rather he is praised therein, not entering into one who promised then broke, since what prevented him fulfilling what he said is the law. Likewise a man promising a man to sit for him in his place waiting till he comes, then prayer comes, so he stands for it leaving waiting him: he is not thereby breaking his promise, since his standing to it is standing to what Allah called him to before his promising the man he promised waiting him in that place. That was excepted by the law though he did not except it in his promise with his tongue. And there is narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakhai the like of that also."
"993","As Bakkar narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us: Ismail ibn Zakariya al-Khulqani narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah who said: I said to Ibrahim al-Nakhai: A man I promise to wait for him but he delays for me, till when do I wait? He said: Until prayer time comes. What we narrated from Ibrahim agrees with what we mentioned, and Allah we ask for success."
"994","Chapter 130 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) on the major sins, which Allah promised His servants avoiding them expiation of their other evils. Allah said: If you avoid the major sins of what you are forbidden, We shall expiate from you your evils and admit you a noble entrance. What was from Him was the utmost generosity, for He expiated from avoiders of these majors their other evils, promising them thereby to admit them a noble entrance without work from them necessitating that, but for right upon them and His generosity to them. Then we returned to seeking these majors, what they are? 888 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which sin is greatest? He said: That you make for your Creator a rival while He created you. I said: Then which? He said: That you kill your child fearing he eats with you. I said: Then which? He said: That you commit adultery with your neighbor's wife. He said: Then the Quran came confirming the Prophet's (peace be upon him) saying: And those who do not call upon another god with Allah... to the end of the verse."
"995","889 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, Mansur and Wasil al-Ahdab, from Abu Wail, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah the like of it."
"996","890 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Amr ibn Shurahbil who said: Ibn Masud said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, which sin is greatest with Allah?... then he mentioned the like of it. So is clear to us upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that these three things mentioned in this hadith are of the majors, the greatest being making a rival for Allah, then next: a man killing his child fearing he eats with him, then next: his adultery with his neighbor's wife. There was in this hadith of them only these three, and we seek refuge in Allah from them. In it some are greater than others. In Abdullah's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was nothing requiring an answer greater than what he answered of what he asked of what he mentioned his asking about."
"997","891 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Shayban narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, what are the majors? He said: Associating with Allah. He said: Then what? He said: Then disobedience to parents. He said: Then what? He said: Then the engulfing oath. Abu Umayya said to us: In my book in a place: Shayban, in another place: Sufyan, in this hadith's isnad. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answering his asker in this on the majors, what they are, that they are associating with Allah, is as his answering Ibn Masud that shirk is the greatest of majors, and what follows thereof is disobedience to parents, and what follows thereof is the engulfing oath. It is possible that is upon killing the child and disobedience to parents being thereof in one degree, and the engulfing oath thereof and a man's adultery with his neighbor's wife in a degree following it, so neither of the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Abdullah ibn Amr contradicts the other. And his first answer of the asking mentioned therein is as he answered in the mentioned hadith his asking of what he asked, except we examined after that these two hadiths and found in their interpretation what is worthier of them than this interpretation we mentioned, finding it allowed that a man's killing his child fearing he eats with him and his disobedience to his parents be in one degree following shirk in Allah, so he answered Ibn Masud with one of them and answered his asker in the hadith of Ibn Amr with the other thereof. Like this of sound speech is to say to a man: Who is bravest of people? Saying: So-and-so, then asked again saying: So-and-so. Like this of sound speech is to say to a man: Who is bravest of people? Saying: So-and-so, then said to him: Then who? Saying: Then so-and-so, for another man likewise, while another like him he was silent of his name and did not mention, so that is sound speech. So likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answering Ibn Masud and his answering in the hadith of Ibn Amr, in that proof there is no contradiction in either for the other. Then who is in the third station in the hadith of Ibn Masud and Ibn Amr is as who is in the second station in both their hadiths, upon what we mentioned therein."
"998","892 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us: al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakra, from his father, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: Shall I not inform you of the greatest of majors? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Associating with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and disobedience to parents. He said: He was reclining, then sat saying: Indeed false speech, or false testimony - al-Jurayri doubted - and kept saying it until we said: Would he be silent. What is in this hadith in the first degree of majors is as in the first two hadiths. What is in this hadith of his (peace be upon him) saying: And disobedience to parents, or false speech, or false testimony, is of what may be those three things joined by and, the intent therein as the intent in then in the first two hadiths. Like that is to say to a man: Who is bravest of people? Saying: So-and-so and so-and-so, one of them in bravery above the other."
"999","893 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Muaddib narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Muhajir ibn Qunfudh al-Taymi, from Abu Umama al-Ansari, from Abdullah - he is Ibn Unays - from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: Of the greatest of majors are shirk in Allah, disobedience to parents, the engulfing oath; no swearer swears by Allah a binding oath introducing therein even a gnat's wing but it is a spot in his heart on Resurrection Day. The speech in this hadith is as the speech in the hadith of Abu Bakra we narrated before it."
"1000","894 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Zayd, from Abu al-Ghayth, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Avoid the seven ruinous. It was said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Shirk in Allah, sorcery, killing the soul Allah forbade except by right, devouring the orphan's wealth, turning on battle day, and slandering chaste unaware believing women. Al-Rabi did not mention to us in his hadith of the seven he mentioned therein but these six we mentioned from him, so we compared this hadith with others' narration, whether we find therein the seventh thing completing these seven."
"1001","895 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi known as al-Baytari narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us... then he mentioned al-Rabi's hadith with the rest of its isnad and its text, with the lack of one of the seven mentioned therein. So we knew thereby that the lack of the seventh in this hadith was not its falling from al-Rabi nor from who narrated to al-Rabi from him; but it was in the hadith itself, and Allah knows best. There is not in these seven things mentioned in this hadith mention of aggravating some over others, so thereby is removed its contradicting any of the hadiths we mentioned before it in this chapter; but they are all majors. The place of shirk thereof is as its place in the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Ibn Amr, and the other things thereof have degrees, Allah knows which degrees they are, and whether they are equal or differ."
"1002","896 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Amr ibn Uthman ibn Said narrated to us: Baqiya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Bahir - he is Ibn Sad - from Khalid - he is Ibn Madan -: Abu Ruhm al-Samai narrated to me that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies worshiping Allah, associating nothing with Him, establishing prayer, giving zakat, fasting Ramadan and avoiding the majors, Paradise is his. A man asked him: What are the majors? He said: Associating with Allah, killing the soul Allah forbade, and fleeing on battle day. The speech in this is as the speech in the hadiths of Abu Bakra, Abu Hurayra and Abu Ayyub alike."
"1003","897 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us: Shuba narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: The greatest of majors are associating with Allah, disobedience to parents, killing the soul, and false testimony - or: false speech. The speech in this hadith is as the speech in the hadith of Abu Bakra also."
"1004","898 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Muadh ibn Hani narrated to us: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Sinan, from Ubayd ibn Umayr ibn Qatada al-Laythi that his father narrated to him - he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him) - that he said in the Farewell Hajj: Indeed Allah's allies are the praying, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever establishes the five prayers written upon him, fasting Ramadan reckoning its fast and seeing it a right upon him, gives his zakat reckoning it obligatory, and avoids the majors Allah forbade, then a man of his Companions said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the majors? He said: Nine; greatest of them: associating with Allah, killing the believer without right, fleeing on battle day, sorcery, devouring the orphan's wealth, devouring usury, slandering the chaste woman, disobedience to Muslim parents, and violating the Sacred House, your qibla, living and dead. Then he said: No man dies not doing these majors, establishing prayer and giving zakat, but he shall accompany Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a beloved abode whose gates are of gold. What is in this hadith has no advancing some of the nine mentioned therein over others, except therein are things of what is in the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Ibn Amr, whose place of the majors is their place in those two hadiths."
"1005","899 - Yunus narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us; and Ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, both said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. Then each of Yunus, Ibn Khuzayma and Fahd said in his hadith: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying: Of the majors is a man's insulting his parents. They said: O Messenger of Allah, does a man insult his parents? He said: Yes; he reviles a man's father, so he reviles his father, and reviles a man's mother, so he reviles his mother. This hadith's place is the place of disobedience in the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Ibn Amr we mentioned in this chapter. This is the aspect of what we found in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the number of majors. And we found from Ibn Masud and Ibn Abbas therein of what we know they did not say by opinion nor derivation, for the like of it is not said thereby, and they said it only as tawqif from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1006","What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim, from Masruq, from Abdullah who said: The majors are from the beginning of Surat al-Nisa to: If you avoid the major sins of what you are forbidden. I said to Muslim: Ibrahim narrated to me! He said: I narrated to Ibrahim. I said to Ibrahim, and he said: Alqama narrated to me, from Abdullah."
"1007","What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: If you avoid the major sins of what you are forbidden. He said: From the beginning of the sura to this speech. This too is of what we know they stood upon of what increased in the number of majors we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, of what is in Surat al-Nisa to: If you avoid the major sins of what you are forbidden. And all what is in this sura of the majors and what is in the hadiths we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter have joined one another. It is possible there are no majors besides them, and possible there are majors besides them Allah did not inform His servants of, that they be wary of falling therein, and that be a deterrent from all evils fearing what they fall into thereof is of those majors. If one says: How is it allowed they are barred from something not clarified to them what it is, to avoid it and not fall therein? It is said: This with us - and Allah knows best - is like what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what preceded in this book of his: The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are dubious matters; who falls therein is as grazing beside a sanctuary, about to fall into it. Allah did not clarify them to them upon His Messenger's tongue, and had He willed He would have clarified, but it is allowed He left that that they avoid all dubieties. Like that is what was narrated from him on Laylat al-Qadr, that it is in Ramadan, then they asked in which of it, and he informed them it is in the last ten. in the last ten thereof, not informing them which night of its nights it is. He said to them in the hadith of Abu Dharr from him therein: Had He willed to inform you of it, He would have informed you of it. We shall mention that in its chapter hereafter in this book of ours, God willing. Leaving informing them which night of the last ten nights it is was that they work in all of them the work of its seekers hoping its meeting. So likewise, if there are majors of evils besides what we mentioned in this chapter in the reports, it is possible He left clarifying them, that that be a cause for their leaving all evils, for they are thereof, and with Allah is success."
"1008","Chapter 131 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his saying to Ibn Umar and his Companions when they returned to him after fleeing from battle, their saying to him: We are the fleers, he said: Rather you are the returners. 900 - Abu Umayya narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ibn Umar who said: I was in a detachment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); people swerved a swerving, I among who swerved. We said: How do we do, having fled from battle incurring wrath? We said: Would we enter Madina and spend night therein? We said: Would we present ourselves to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if repentance is ours, else we go. We came to him before dawn prayer, he came out saying: Who are the people? We said: We are the fleers. He said: Rather you are the returners; I am your company, or: I am the company of the Muslims. We came to him until we kissed his hand."
"1009","901 - Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ibn Abi Layla who said: Ibn Umar said... then he mentioned this hadith, except he said therein: People swerved a swerving, in place of what is in Abu Umayya's hadith: People swerved a swerving, and he did not mention therein: So we came to him and kissed his hand."
"1010","902 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman who said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me that he was in a detachment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and people swerved a swerving... then he mentioned the like of Abu Umayya's hadith exactly. One said: al-Akkarun with the Arabs are the returners to battle, so how was it allowed in this hadith to say this speech to the fleers? Our answer: What is meant thereby is that when they returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he being their company, to return to what he commands them and turn in what he turns them, that was a return from them to him and a coming back to what they were upon of offering themselves to fight their enemy, so thereby they deserved to be returners, and Allah knows the reality. In this hadith is of what must be noted of what joins the majors: that some went to Allah's saying: Whoever turns to them his back that day, is only on the people of Badr particularly, to the exclusion of others, for the Muslims had no company that day but present at Badr."
"1011","As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Said who said: It was revealed on Badr day: Whoever turns to them his back that day."
"1012","As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abu Dawud, meaning al-Harrani, narrated to us: Abu Zayd al-Harawi narrated to us: Shuba narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Said: Whoever turns to them his back that day, he said: It was revealed on the people of Badr. What we narrated from Abu Said, that this verse was revealed on Badr day or on the people of Badr, does not require the ruling therein on other than the people of Badr as on the people of Badr, and after Badr as it was in Badr. Proof is that Ibn Umar's entering into fighting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) admitting him therein was only in the year of the Trench, after his turning him back before that and leaving admitting him therein, this after Badr. So proves the ruling of fleeing from battle without maneuvering to battle or joining a company remains to Resurrection Day, entering into the majors, and Allah we ask for success."
"1013","Chapter 132 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him of his saying: When Allah is pleased with the servant, He praises him sevenfold of good he did not do; and what was narrated from him on wrath like that. 903 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Haywa ibn Shurayh informed me, from Salim ibn Ghaylan, from Darraj, from Abu al-Haytham, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) who said: When Allah is pleased with the servant, He praises him sevenfold of good he did not do, and He said on wrath the like of it."
"1014","904 - Bakkar and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Haywa... then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"1015","905 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us: Haywa narrated to us: Salim ibn Ghaylan informed me that he heard Darraj narrating from Abu al-Haytham, from Abu Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) the like of it. We examined this hadith's meaning and found what therein of Allah's praising His servant when Allah is pleased with him sevenfold of good he did not do, may be that the servant, when Allah is pleased with him for his righteous deeds, praises him sevenfold of good he did not do, of what He knew he shall do in the future, though he may do in the future multiples of good with which He does not praise him, for he does not deserve that since he did not do it; but Allah by His grace upon him and love for him for the good he is upon, praised him with what He willed to praise him of what he shall do in the future. Had Allah willed not to praise him anything of that since he did not do it, He would not have praised him anything thereof. Since He may not praise him with anything of what we mentioned, He may praise him with what He wills thereof and leave praising him by Himself. This on who He is pleased with; as for who He is wrathful with, it is also allowed he praises him with sevenfold of evil he did not do, of what he shall do in the future, and perhaps he shall do in the future multiples of evil. Had Allah willed not to praise him thereby, He would have, since he did not do it to that time, so He praised him with what He willed of what he shall do, leaving not praising him with other thereof like what He praised him with. of what He praised him with, Glorious and Mighty, and Allah we ask for success."
"1016","Chapter 133 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his statement: If the Quran were placed in a skin and then thrown into the Fire, it would not burn. 906 - Narrated to us by Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam, and Bakr ibn Idris, they said: narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri, narrated to us by Ibn Lahi'ah, from Mishrah ibn Ha'an, from Uqbah ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the Quran were placed in a skin, then thrown into the Fire, it would not burn. We examined this hadith and found that earlier scholars of this meaning held two different views about it. The first: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed his community by this statement that whoever has the Quran with him, it will prevent the Fire from affecting him even if he were thrown into it; his intent by 'skin' was the human being who carries the Quran, and that Allah Most High protects him by it from the Fire, just as He protected Ibrahim, His close friend (peace be upon him), because of his station with Him, from the Fire acting upon him, and by His statement to it: Be cool and safe upon Ibrahim. The second view: the skin mentioned in this hadith is the skin upon which the Quran is written; Allah Most High, in declaring the Quran far above the Fire, withholds it from it and removes it from the skin until that skin is empty of the Quran, then the Fire burns the skin while no Quran is in it. Each of these two meanings is good, and this hadith can bear either. Allah knows best what His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by that statement as interpreted upon these two mentioned meanings, whether it is one of these two meanings or another meaning besides them which He has not shown us and our knowledge has not reached. We ask Allah for success."
"1017","Chapter 134 - Explanation of the problematic report narrated by Abu Hurayrah from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The child of adultery is the worst of the three. 907 - Narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Abu Hudhayfah, narrated to us by al-Thawri, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child of adultery is the worst of the three."
"1018","908 - Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud, narrated to us by Abu Umar al-Hawdi, narrated to us by Khalid ibn Abd Allah, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1019","909 - Narrated to us by al-Rabi' al-Jizi, narrated to us by Hassan ibn Ghalib, narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The offspring of adultery is the worst of the three. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this and found it unrestricted over all children of adultery, requiring that every child of adultery is worse than his mother and than the adulterer by whom she conceived him, although the adultery was the choice of his mother and of the adulterer, while their children were innocent of that. A questioner asked: how can children of adultery, who had no act in the adultery, be worse than those from whom they came among those who committed the adultery, and greater in that. Our answer to him was that this hadith was transmitted from Abu Hurayrah as we mentioned, and it is reported from Aishah that she rejected that upon him and reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by that statement only a specific man, for a quality in him by which he stood apart from other children of adulterers."
"1020","910 - As narrated to us by Salih ibn Shu'ayb ibn Aban al-Basri, narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq, narrated to us by Salamah ibn al-Fadl, from Ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah who said: It reached Aishah that Abu Hurayrah says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child of adultery is the worst of the three. She said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Hurayrah, he heard poorly and so answered poorly; thus in the hadith, while the people of language say: he heard poorly and so responded poorly, without alif. Then we return to the hadith of al-Zuhri from Urwah, from Aishah: the hadith was not upon this; rather there was a man who used to harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed, besides what is with him, he is a child of adultery; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is the worst of the three. This hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repels what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we narrated before it. What is in this hadith is more fitting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, because Allah said in His Book: No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another, and He said: Man shall have nothing but what he strives for, and his striving shall be seen, then he shall be recompensed for it with the fullest recompense. The child of adultery had no striving in the adultery of his mother nor in the adultery of the adulterer by her through which she conceived him. It became clear to us by the hadith of Aishah that the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned by Abu Hurayrah, 'the child of adultery is the worst of the three,' was only about a specific man, because of harm he caused the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by which he became a disbeliever, worse than his mother and than the adulterer by whom her conception of him occurred."
"1021","Chapter 135 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise. 911 - Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud, narrated to us by al-Muqaddami, narrated to us by Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri, narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Mujahid who said: I stayed with Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd, and he was detained one night then came and said: Have you given dinner to your guest? They said: We waited for you. He said: Abu Hurayrah kept me busy; may Manbudh be bereft of his mother if what Abu Hurayrah says is true. I said: What did he narrate to you? He said: He narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise."
"1022","912 - And narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus, narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan, narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman ibn Maghra, narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Mujahid who said: May Manbudh be bereft of his mother if what Abu Hurayrah said is true. I said to him: What did he say? He said: Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise."
"1023","913 - Narrated to us by Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Adi, narrated to us by Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Dhubab who said: Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise. We examined what is in this hadith, since it is attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and since it is among what was asked about like the first hadith mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. What is in this hadith, in our view, and Allah knows best, is intended for one who is steeped in adultery until it dominates him, so he deserves to be ascribed to it and it is said: he is a son of it, just as those steeped in the world are ascribed to it and called sons of the world for acting for it and being absorbed in it and abandoning all else, and as one steeped in caution is called son of cautions, and one steeped in speech son of sayings, and as the traveler is called son of the road, and as those cut off from their wealth by the distance between them and it are called sons of the road, as Allah Most High said regarding the categories of zakah recipients: Alms are for the poor, until He mentioned among them the son of the road; and as Badr ibn Hazaz said to al-Nabighah, meaning: if only he were cautious and shrewd; and as one says: so-and-so is the son of a city, for the city he is absorbed in; and from it the saying of al-Akhtal. Like that is 'son of adultery,' said of one who is steeped in adultery until by his absorption in it he is ascribed to it and adultery dominates him, that he shall not enter Paradise because of this station he is in. By it was not intended one who is not among the people of adultery but is born of adultery. This is more fitting for the meaning of this hadith, for the reasons we mentioned regarding its like in the chapter before this chapter. This hadith is also narrated with a different wording, in which in place of 'son of adultery' occurs: child of adultery."
"1024","914 - As narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, narrated to us by Shayban, meaning al-Nahwi, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jaban, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise."
"1025","915 - And as narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Sabiq, narrated to us by Abu Isra'il, from Mansur, from Abi al-Hajjaj, from a freedman of Abu Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There shall not enter Paradise one disobedient to his parents, nor a reminded giver of favors, nor a child of adultery, nor an addict of wine. In what we narrated in this section of these hadiths is proof that 'child of adultery' may be said of one steeped in adultery just as 'son of adultery' is said of one steeped in adultery. Since that is so, what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we narrated in this chapter before this chapter, from the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the child of adultery is the worst of the three, may refer to one whom adultery dominates, so that by that he is worse than others who are not so."
"1026","Chapter 136 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the appearance of children of sin at the end of time. 916 - Narrated to us by Yunus, reported to us by Ibn Wahb, reported to me by Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This community shall remain upon a law so long as three things do not appear among them: so long as knowledge is not taken away from them, and the child of sin does not increase among them, and the Saqqarun do not appear among them. They said: Who are the Saqqarun? He said: A generation at the end of time whose greeting among themselves when they meet is mutual cursing. The meaning of this hadith, according to the people of knowledge, regarding the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), 'and the Saqqarun appear among them,' whom he described as he did with foul speech, and his ascribing them to the Saqr because of the foulness of the Saqr's mouth: their ascription to it is like the foulness of what comes from their mouths of foul speech compared to the foul-mouthed Saqr. Concerning his mention (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of them as children of sin, our intent therein, and Allah knows best, is his ascribing them to sin and that they are children of it, for the reason we mentioned in the chapter before this, that it is permitted to say of one absorbed in a thing dominated by it that he is a child of that thing, just as it is permitted to say: he is a son of it."
"1027","Chapter 137 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement about freeing a child of adultery: there is no good in it. 917 - Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us by Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Abu Yazid al-Danni, from Maymunah daughter of Sa'd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about freeing a child of adultery and said: There is no good in it; two sandals with which one is aided are dearer to me than freeing a child of adultery. The meaning of this hadith is, in our view, and Allah knows best, about freeing one steeped in adultery until by that he is ascribed to it and made a child of it. Included in that is what we mentioned before in these chapters, and it is permitted to say 'child of adultery' for one of this description, just as it is said of him: son of adultery. It is reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that."
"1028","What was narrated to us by al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud, narrated to us by Hassan ibn Ghalib, narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Suhayl, from his father who said: Abu Hurayrah used to say: That I carry a whip in the path of Allah is dearer to me than freeing an offspring of adultery."
"1029","And what was narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us by Abu Ja'far, meaning al-Razi, from Yahya al-Bakka who said: It was said to Ibn Umar: They say about the child of adultery: the worst of the three. He said: Rather he is the best of the three; Umar freed slaves of his from among children of adultery, and if it were wicked he would not have done it. As for what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah about this, it is like what he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his statement: The offspring of adultery is the worst of the three. What we narrated from Ibn Umar about it is like what we narrated from Aishah about it earlier in this book, chapter 134. What is in this hadith from Umar is proof for the interpretation we gave the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, since what Umar did was in the presence of others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they did not reject it nor oppose him in it, which proves their agreement with him upon it. Success is from Allah."
"1030","Chapter 138 - Explanation of the problematic content in the Book of Allah Most High, where mercy is mentioned with 'wind' and 'winds,' and what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the preferred of those two readings. Narrated to us by Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us by Abu Ubayd who said: The reading we follow for wind and winds is that what is of mercy is plural, and what is of punishment is singular. He said: The basis by which we considered this reading is the hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when the wind blew he would say: O Allah, make them winds and do not make it a wind. What Abu Ubayd related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has no basis. It would have been more fitting for him, given his high rank and truthfulness in narration, other than this hadith, not to attribute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what the people of hadith knowledge do not know from him. Then we considered what is in the Book of Allah indicating the correct view on this meaning, and found Allah said in His Book: He it is who enables you to travel on land and sea, until when you are in ships and they sail with them by a pleasant wind and they rejoice in it, there comes to it a stormy wind and waves come to them from every place. The pleasant wind from Allah was mercy, and the stormy wind from Him, Mighty and Majestic, was punishment. In that is proof of the invalidity of what Abu Ubayd narrated as we mentioned from him. May Allah forgive him. Then we considered what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering into this meaning."
"1031","918 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ali ibn al-Madini. And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn al-Shahid; both said: narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Fudayl, narrated to us by al-A'mash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Dharr, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not curse the wind; when you see from it what you dislike, say: O Allah, we ask You for the good of this wind and the good within it and the good it was commanded with, and we seek refuge in You from the evil of this wind and the evil within it and the evil it was commanded with."
"1032","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us by Jarir, from al-A'mash, then he mentioned with its chain the same, except that he did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but stopped it upon Ubayy."
"1033","And we found Ahmad narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Mansur, reported to us by al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, reported to us by Shu'bah, from Habib who said: I heard Dharr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that the wind blew fiercely in the time of Ubayy, then he mentioned the same, and did not raise it."
"1034","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb said: And it is correct. And we found Ahmad narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us by Ibn Abi Adi, reported to us by Shu'bah, from Habib, then he mentioned the same with its chain, and did not raise it. This is what we found from Ubayy ibn Ka'b. And we also found from Abu Hurayrah."
"1035","919 - What Yunus narrated to us, narrated to us by Bishr ibn Bakr, reported to us by al-Awza'i, from Muhammad ibn Muslim; reported to me by Thabit al-Zuraqi, that Abu Hurayrah said: Wind seized the people on the road to Makkah while Umar ibn al-Khattab was on pilgrimage, and it bore hard upon them, so Umar said to those around him: What is the wind? They gave him no answer. What Umar asked about reached me, so I urged my mount until I caught up with him and said to him: Commander of the Faithful, I was told you asked about the wind; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The wind is from the mercy of Allah; it comes with mercy and comes with punishment, so do not curse it, ask Allah for its good and seek refuge with Him from its evil."
"1036","920 - And what Bakkar narrated to us, narrated to us by Abu Asim, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from Thabit ibn Qays, from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned the same exactly."
"1037","921 - And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, narrated to us by Rawh ibn Ubadah, narrated to us by Ibn Jurayj; narrated to me by Ziyad, that Ibn Shihab reported to him; reported to me by Thabit ibn Qays, that Abu Hurayrah said, then he mentioned the same."
"1038","922 - And what Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, narrated to us by Salamah ibn Rawh, from Uqayl; narrated to me by Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1039","923 - And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, narrated to us by Kathir ibn Ubayd, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Harb, from al-Zubaydi; reported to me by al-Zuhri, from Thabit al-Zuraqi, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same."
"1040","924 - And what Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Salih; narrated to me by al-Layth ibn Sa'd; narrated to me by Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with its chain the same, except that he said: And seek refuge with Allah from its evil. This is what we found from Abu Hurayrah, agreeing with what we found from Ubayy ibn Ka'b. And we found from Aishah."
"1041","925 - What Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb who said: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating from Ata, from Aishah who said: When the wind blew fiercely the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: O Allah, I ask You for its good and the good within it and the good it was sent with, and I seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil within it and the evil it was sent with. When the sky clouded, his color changed and he went in and out, came forward and turned back; when it rained he was relieved. Aishah asked him, and he said: Perhaps it is as the people of Ad said: When they saw it as a cloud facing their valleys, they said: This is a cloud that will rain upon us. This is what we found from Aishah on this meaning. And he narrated to us from Anas ibn Malik about it also."
"1042","926 - What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah, narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, from al-Muthanna ibn Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when a strong wind blew he would say: O Allah, I ask You for the good of what it was commanded with, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what it was commanded with. This is what we found from Anas. In all we narrated, the wind may come with mercy and may come with punishment, and there is no difference between them except in mercy and punishment, and it is one wind, not winds. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also, entering into this meaning."
"1043","927 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, narrated to us by Abu Amir al-Aqadi and Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris, both saying: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was aided by al-Saba, and Ad were destroyed by al-Dabur."
"1044","928 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by al-Khidr ibn Shuja', narrated to us by Miskin ibn Bukayr, narrated to us by Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Amir and Uthman ibn Umar differed with Miskin ibn Bukayr over the man between al-Hakam and Ibn Abbas: Abu Amir and Uthman said he is Mujahid, while Miskin said he is Sa'id ibn Jubayr. And we found it from other than the hadith of Shu'bah and other than the hadith of al-Hakam."
"1045","929 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, narrated to us by Shayban, from al-A'mash, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. In what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is that he was aided by al-Saba, which is one wind, and that Ad were destroyed by al-Dabur, which is one wind. In that is proof of what we mentioned."
"1046","Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Imran, narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Adam, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash who said: A man recited to Asim: And We sent the wind as pollinators, so Asim said: And We sent the winds as pollinators; had it been wind it would have been pollinating. He said: I mentioned that to al-A'mash, who said to me: None of the winds pollinates except the south; when it disperses it becomes winds. In what we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is proof that the preferred choice in what the readers differed over, of winds and wind, is wind, not winds."
"1047","Chapter 139 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, that Sa'd ibn Ubadah said to him: Messenger of Allah, if I found a man with my wife, should I grant him delay until I bring four witnesses? He said: Yes. 930 - Narrated to us by Yunus, narrated to us by Ibn Wahb; narrated to me by Malik, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that Sa'd ibn Ubadah said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If I found a man with my wife, should I grant him delay until I bring four witnesses? He said: Yes."
"1048","931 - And narrated to us by al-Muzani, narrated to us by al-Shafi'i, from Malik, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, the same. We examined this hadith to extract its jurisprudence. We found it obligatory upon Muslims to change evils and deter their people from them, yet in Sa'd leaving the man he found with his wife upon what he found them in, there is leaving them to persist in disobedience, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him that. That, in our view, and Allah knows best, is so that proof stands against them for what they are in, until punishment is carried out upon them. In that is proof that the like of this, until its punishment is carried out, is permitted. In it is proof for whoever says, about four who testify against a man and woman for adultery and say: we deliberately looked, that they are praiseworthy in that and their testimony is accepted, since they did that only so Allah's penalty is carried out against whoever deserves it. Thus Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad used to say about this."
"1049","As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi', narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad, narrated to us by Muhammad, reported to us by Ya'qub, from Abu Hanifah to that effect as we mentioned, and he related no disagreement in any of it. A critic rejected that upon them and nullified the testimony of the witnesses for deliberately looking at what they testified to. The view on that, in our view, is the first view, and Allah knows best. In this hadith also is the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Sa'd to leave deterring that man and his wife from the disobedience they were in until he brings four witnesses besides himself testifying against them. In that is proof that his own testimony in that is not permitted, since he is the woman's husband testifying against her, as Malik, al-Shafi'i, and the rest of the Medinans say about that."
"1050","And as reported from Ibn Abbas, from what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Mansur, narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from Ubayd Allah, who is Ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, about four who testify against a woman for adultery, one of them the husband: the husband performs li'an and the three are flogged. Abu al-Zinad said: That is the view of the people of our city."
"1051","And as narrated to us by Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl, narrated to us by Abu Nu'aym, narrated to us by Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas who said: The husband performs li'an and the three are flogged, because finding three with him testifying to that is easier for him than finding four besides him testifying to that. He was only allowed the omission of what he saw of disobedience from them so that proof stands against them by witnesses he brings testifying against them. Since those sought for that are four besides him, not three who with him are witnesses to that, that proves his testimony in it is not accepted. Had it not been so, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in answer to his question, would have said to him: What need have you of four testifying to that; seek three besides yourself so that you and they are witnesses to that, since that is easier for him and shorter in time than seeking four besides him. We ask Allah for success."
"1052","Chapter 140 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who looks at a man in his house without permission: may he gouge his eye for that or not? 932 - Narrated to us by Bakkar, narrated to us by Abu Asim, narrated to us by Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man looked at you and you threw a pebble at him and gouged his eye, there would be no sin upon you. In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the denial of sin for one who throws at a man who looked at him in his house and thereby gouges his eyes, since it is his right to stop looking at his house and at what is in it which no one may lawfully look at."
"1053","933 - Narrated to us by Yunus, narrated to us by Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, who heard him say: A man looked through a hole into the room of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had an iron comb with which he was scratching his head. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I known you were looking at me, I would have stabbed your eye with it; seeking permission was made only because of looking."
"1054","934 - Narrated to us by Yunus, narrated to us by Ibn Wahb; reported to me by Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, that Sahl ibn Sa'd reported to him, then he mentioned the same."
"1055","935 - Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi, narrated to us by Adam ibn Abi Iyas, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, that a man looked through a hole in the door of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was scratching his head with a comb, and he said: Had I known you were looking, I would have stabbed your eye; permission was made only because of seeing. In this is the extension of what is in the first for the one looked upon against the one looking."
"1056","936 - Narrated to us by Ibn Khuzaymah, narrated to us by Mu'alla ibn Asad, narrated to us by Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever looks into the house of a people without their permission, it is lawful for them to gouge his eye."
"1057","937 - Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Musa ibn Isma'il al-Minqari, narrated to us by Aban ibn Yazid; narrated to us by Yahya, who is Ibn Abi Kathir, that Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to him, from Anas, that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pressed his eyes to the slit of the door. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, took an arrow or sharpened stick and came to gouge the Bedouin's eye, but the Bedouin withdrew and left. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you remained, I would have gouged your eye."
"1058","938 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, narrated to us by Musaddad, narrated to us by Hammad, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Anas, that a man looked into one of the rooms of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood toward him with an arrowhead, or he said arrowheads. Anas said: It is as if I am looking at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking stealth to stab him. In what we narrated of these reports is proof that since it is the house owner's right to prevent looking into his house, he may stop that from his house, even if stopping it causes loss of the looker's eye. Whoever is entitled to do a thing and does it is reasonably free of liability in it. And it is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he denied any required compensation in that upon the doer, neither retaliation nor blood-money."
"1059","939 - As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi, narrated to us by Ali ibn al-Madini, narrated to us by Mu'adh ibn Hisham, from his father, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever looks into the house of a people without their permission and they gouge his eye, there is neither blood-money nor retaliation."
"1060","940 - And as narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri, narrated to us by Mu'adh ibn Hisham, then he mentioned with its chain the same. These reports came with what they contain as we mentioned, coming successively, strengthening one another. We did not find the jurists of the cities applying them as such. Repelling the looker's gaze from another's house without his order, from looking at what is in his house, can be done by verbal rebuke and threats of speech. It may be that one who abandons that and proceeds to gouge the looker's eye incurs liability for gouging it. We examined that and found jihad against the enemy obligatory upon us: if we do it with a call from us to the enemy to what we fight them for preceding our fighting them, it is good; had we fought them without a call from us to them to that, knowing they know what we call them to and what we fight them for, we would be blameless in that and not liable for what we inflict upon them of their lives, wealth, and children. Like that, in our view, and Allah knows best, is the case of this looker into another's house: if we call him from what he attempts and inform him that if he does not desist from what he is in, we shall do it to him, it is good; if we do not do that with him but apply what is in these reports we narrated, knowing he knows what we want from him of desisting from looking at what he looks at of what is forbidden to him, it is permitted for us. Like that is the apostate from Islam to disbelief: if we ask him to repent before killing him, it is good; if we kill him without asking him, knowing he knows what we want by asking him of it, it is permitted. What we mentioned in these reports, of denying retaliation and denying blood-money against the gouger of the looker's eye we described, is among what admits no disagreement nor any contrary view, given what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, then what reason and sound reflection indicate."
"1061","This statement we selected is also reported from Umar, as narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Yazid, narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Mansur, narrated to us by Hushaym, narrated to us by Ash'ath ibn Abd al-Malik, from al-Hasan, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever looks at a people and they wound him, he has no blood-money."
"1062","Chapter 141 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his answer to al-Miqdad when he asked him about the disbeliever who cut off his hand then sought refuge by a tree and said: I have submitted to Allah, Mighty and Majestic; may I kill him? 941 - Narrated to us by Yunus, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr; narrated to me by al-Layth ibn Sa'd. And narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb, reported to us by Qutaybah ibn Sa'id, narrated to us by al-Layth; then both together said: from Ibn Shihab, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, from al-Miqdad, who reported to him that he said: Messenger of Allah, if I met a man of the disbelievers and he fought me and struck one of my hands with the sword and cut it off, then sought refuge from me by a tree and said: I have submitted to Allah, may I kill him, Messenger of Allah, after he said it? He said: Do not kill him; if you kill him, he is in your station before you killed him, and you are in his station before he said the word he said. What is in this hadith requires uncovering, reflection, and seeking the intended meaning. The statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in answer to al-Miqdad, after the disbeliever cut his hand, not to kill him, and informing him that if he killed him he would be in his station before he killed him, meaning: by his Islam he returns to being a Muslim as you were a Muslim; and that you would be in his station before he said the word he said, meaning by that the word by which he became Muslim, meaning: you would return as a killer of one who has become Muslim, thus among the people of the Fire, as he before the word he said was a disbeliever among the people of the Fire. Success is from Allah."
"1063","Chapter 142 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement in the hadith of the leather strap to the brother of the slain mentioned in it: If you kill him, meaning the killer of his brother, you will be like him. 942 - Narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi, narrated to us by Abu Umayr ibn al-Nahhas, narrated to us by Damrah ibn Rabi'ah, from Ibn Shawdhab, from Thabit, from Anas who said: A man brought the killer of his kin to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said to him: Pardon. He refused. He said: Take blood-money. He refused. He said: Will you kill him, for you are like him? He said: So he freed him, and he was seen dragging his leather strap, going to his family."
"1064","943 - Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad, narrated to us by Abu Umar al-Hawdi, narrated to us by Jami' ibn Matar, from Alqamah ibn Wa'il ibn Hujr, from his father who said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came with a leather strap around his neck and said: Messenger of Allah, this man and my brother were in a well digging it, and he raised the pick and struck his companion's head with it and killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Pardon him. He refused, then said a second time: Messenger of Allah, this man and my brother were in a well digging it, and he raised the pick and struck his companion's head and killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Pardon him. He refused, then stood a third time and said: Messenger of Allah, this man and my brother were in a well digging it, and he raised the pick and struck his companion's head and killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pardon him. He refused. He said: Take him; if you kill him you will be like him. He took him away until he passed, then we called to him: Do you not hear what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says? He returned and said: Messenger of Allah, if I kill him I shall be like him? He said: Yes. So he pardoned him, and he went out dragging his strap until he was hidden from us. We examined these two hadiths and found in them what carries that the killing of the strap's owner of his companion accused of killing him was established before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by evidence he accepted against him; had it not been so, he would have rebuked his adversary for the strap with which he bound him until he brought him thus to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor would he have said to his companion: pardon him, nor said to him: take blood-money, when he refused to pardon him. In that is confirmation of what we said, and Allah knows best. In the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Anas to the adversary: pardon him, and when he refused he said to him: take blood-money, is proof that pardon by the guardian of the slain does not oblige blood-money for him upon the killer, as Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri, Zufar, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad say, contrary to what al-Awza'i and al-Shafi'i say, that blood-money is obligatory for him upon the killer. Then we examined the meaning of his statement: if you kill him you will be like him."
"1065","944 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Abu Kurayb and Ahmad ibn Harb, both saying: narrated to us by Abu Mu'awiyah, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: A man killed a man in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed him to the guardian of the slain. The killer said: No, by Allah, Messenger of Allah, I did not intend to kill him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he is truthful and then you kill him, you will enter the Fire. He said: So he freed him, and he was bound with a leather strap, and went out dragging his strap, so he was called the man of the strap. In this hadith is the statement of the accused killer: No, by Allah, Messenger of Allah, I did not intend to kill him. Its meaning, in our view, and Allah knows best, is that the evidence testifying against him for killing his adversary's brother testified to the outward act, which in its view was deliberate, with no doubt in it, while the accused knew himself and what he did better, so he claimed an inward matter in it by which no retaliation is due upon him for what he did. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then said to the guardian: If he is truthful and then you kill him, you will enter the Fire. By that we understood the meaning of his statement in the first two hadiths: if you kill him you will be like him, meaning: in outward appearance, in our view, he is among the people of the Fire because proof is established against him for killing whom he killed; if you kill him while he is truthful in what he said, you too will be among the people of the Fire, and Allah knows best. And we found the hadith of Wa'il ibn Hujr, from other than the route we narrated it, came with a meaning contradicting the meaning of his hadith which he narrated to us at the beginning of this chapter."
"1066","945 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, reported to us by Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Ullayah, narrated to us by Ishaq, meaning Ibn Yusuf, from Awf al-A'rabi, from Alqamah ibn Wa'il al-Hadrami, from his father who said: The killer who had killed was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the guardian of the slain brought him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Will you pardon? He said: No. He said: Will you take blood-money? He said: No. He said: Will you kill? He said: Yes. He said: Go. When he left, he called him and said: Will you pardon? He said: No. He said: Will you take blood-money? He said: No. He said: Will you kill? He said: Yes. He said: Go. When he left, he said: If you pardon him, he will bear your sin and the sin of your companion. So he pardoned him and released him. He said: I saw him dragging his strap."
"1067","946 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Awf ibn Abi Jamilah; narrated to me by Hamzah Abu Umar al-A'idhi; narrated to us by Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from Wa'il who said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the killer was brought, led by the guardian of the slain in a leather strap. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the guardian of the slain: Will you pardon, then he mentioned the first hadith exactly. Yahya ibn Sa'id added over Ishaq ibn Yusuf, in the chain of this hadith which both narrated from Awf, Hamzah al-A'idhi. Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb said to us: This Hamzah is a well-known man; Shu'bah narrated from him."
"1068","947 - Narrated to us by Ahmad, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us by Yahya, narrated to us by Jami' ibn Matar al-Habati, from Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Yahya said: It is better than it. What is in this hadith of Wa'il, in place of what we narrated from Wa'il and from Anas, 'if you kill him you will be like him; if you pardon him he will bear your sin and the sin of your companion,' its meaning, and Allah knows best, if it is authentic in the hadith of Wa'il, is that if you pardon him, he will bear the sin of your companion for whom you did not carry out punishment, and bear your sin for what entered your heart over his killing your companion for which no punishment was carried out."
"1069","Chapter 143 - Explanation of the problematic content in the answer of each of Abu Bakr, Umar, and Suhayl ibn Bayda to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he asked them what he would do with a man he found with his wife. 948 - Narrated to us by Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Shabbuwayh, narrated to us by al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, from Zayd ibn Yuthay', from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: If you found a man with Umm Ruman, what would you do with him? He said: I would do evil with him. He said: And you, Umar? He said: I would kill him. He said: And you, Suhayl ibn Bayda? He said: I would say, or saying: May Allah curse the far one, may Allah curse the far ones, and may Allah curse the first of the three; report this. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have interpreted the Quran, O son of Bayda: And those who accuse their wives, the verse. We examined this hadith and found what is in it of Abu Bakr's answer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his mentioned question clear in meaning. We found what is in it of Umar's answer to him about what he asked him needing reflection and grasping its meaning. We examined it and found in it Umar's report to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would kill whoever he found in that state, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving any rejection of that upon him, nor deterring him from it nor forbidding him it. In that is proof of his permitting it to him, that the law does not forbid him that. We know of no one among those whose fatwa is followed holding this view, though it is possible others among them hold it whom we have not encountered; for it is possible we may yet encounter it, or possible we may not. If it is among what no scholar holds, their abandoning it or turning from it to its opposite is proof of its abrogation, for we say only as he says by taking it from him and following what they were upon in it or in its like indicating that it must be said in it. Since they are trusted in what we mentioned as proof in it, they are likewise in abandoning its like and acting by its opposite. Like that is what Muhammad ibn Sirin said about mut'ah in Hajj."
"1070","As Yunus narrated to us, reported to us by Ibn Wahb; reported to me by Jarir ibn Hazim, from Ayyub who said: Muhammad ibn Sirin said: Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, forbade it, meaning the mut'ah of Hajj; they witnessed it and forbade it, so there is nothing in their view to be rejected nor in their counsel to be suspected. If it has holders, it is among what need not be abandoned nor is saying otherwise forbidden. We found in Suhayl's answer to him about what he asked him two points of jurisprudence. One: the permissibility of cursing the people of that disobedience, and that it is outside his prohibition (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his community being frequent cursers, and proof that what is intended by the prohibition of that in what is reported from him is other than what is permitted in this hadith. We shall mention what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prohibited cursing later in this book in a more fitting place, Allah willing. The other point: his silence about what he saw from his wife and about mentioning it to his imam until the mutual cursing Allah decreed for their likes occurs, by His statement: And those who accuse their wives and have no witnesses but themselves, the verse. For showing that, uncovering it, and reporting it would make him a slanderer of a chaste woman, and the threat for slandering a chaste woman would apparently attach to him before people, though in reality it is otherwise; but Allah undertook inner matters and referred people's judgments in this world to the outward which they perceive from one another. His silence about that was therefore praiseworthy. The li'an between him and his wife, had he shown that and his wife demanded her due from him in it, would lead only to her separation, while he can separate from her by divorcing her without anything attaching to him from that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised him and informed him of the source from which he took that, that it is the verse he recited to him. We ask Allah for success."
"1071","Chapter 144 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement: Delay by one able to pay permits his honor and punishment. 949 - Narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Abu Asim, narrated to us by Wabr ibn Abi Dulaylah; narrated to me by Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun; narrated to me by Amr ibn al-Sharid, who heard his father say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delay by one able to pay permits his honor and punishment."
"1072","950 - Narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Abu Asim, from Wabr ibn Abi Dulaylah or Dalilah; narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Maymun ibn Musaykah; narrated to me by Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delay by one able to pay permits his honor and punishment. A questioner asked about what is intended by this hadith. Our answer to him is that the delay intended therein is procrastination, from the saying of Dhu al-Rummah; it is the verbal noun of lawaytuhu, for you say: lawaytuhu layyan, as you say: tawaytuhu tayyan, and as you say: shawaytuhu shayyan, and as you say: ghawaytuhu ghayyan. And it is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the procrastination of one able to pay."
"1073","951 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, reported to us by Sufyan, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Procrastination by the rich is injustice."
"1074","952 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by Mu'alla ibn Mansur al-Razi, narrated to us by Sufyan, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1075","953 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by Mu'alla ibn Mansur, narrated to us by Abu Awanah, from Dawud ibn Abd Allah al-Awdi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1076","954 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, narrated to us by Mu'alla ibn Mansur, narrated to us by Hushaym, narrated to us by Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. When he deserves by his delay, if he is unjust, he deserves to be addressed by it and rebuked with it: one says to him, O unjust one, and it is said to him: you are unjust. This is what becomes permitted of his honor by his delay, and Allah knows best. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, in what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us from Abu Ubayd from him, said: It is demanding payment. The view with us on that is the first view, and Allah knows best, for demanding payment is the right of the creditor against the debtor before his delaying him with it; when he delays him with it, he deserves against him another meaning not due upon him before that, other than demanding payment. As for the punishment deserved upon him, some said: it is imprisonment for that debt. Muhammad, in the narration we mentioned, said: it is staying with him. Staying with him is confinement of the one stayed with from managing his affairs, so it resembles reasonable imprisonment, except that the better view with us on that, and Allah knows best, is that it is the judge's imprisonment of one deserving it in it; for in the creditor's staying with the debtor is his distraction from his own livelihood, and there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that when he asks the judge to imprison him for his debt, that is obligatory for him upon him. Punishment by imprisonment was therefore better than by staying."
"1077","Chapter 145 - Explanation of the problematic report from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding upper rooms, and what is reported from him permitting that. 955 - Narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, narrated to us by Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us by Hammad ibn Salamah, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab, from Abi al-Aliyah, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, that he built an upper room, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Throw it down. He said: I shall spend its price in the path of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated to him three times, and al-Abbas repeated to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three times; each time he said to him: Throw it down, and al-Abbas said: I shall spend its price in the path of Allah. In this hadith is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to al-Abbas to throw down the upper room he built. It may be from his dislike of upper rooms from which people's houses are overlooked because their houses are low, or because of dislike of building not needed, whether high or low. We examined what is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides this hadith on this meaning."
"1078","956 - We found Fahd narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus, narrated to us by Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah, narrated to us by Uthman ibn Hakim; narrated to me by Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Hatib al-Qurashi, from Abu Talhah al-Asadi, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and saw a lofty dome and said: What is this? His Companions said to him: It belongs to a man of the Ansar. He was silent and kept it in himself, until when its owner came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people, he turned away from him. He did that with him repeatedly until he recognized anger and turning away from him, and complained of that to his Companions, saying: By Allah, I find the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed toward me, and I do not know what happened to me or what I did. They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and saw your dome, and asked whose it was, and we told him. The man returned to his dome and demolished it until he leveled it with the ground. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and did not see it, and said: What happened to the dome that was here? They said: Your companion complained to us of your turning away from him, so we told him, and he demolished it. He said: Every building is a woe upon its owner on the Day of Resurrection, except what is indispensable. What is in this hadith proves that the reported dislike therein is of building itself, not for the two meanings for which we mentioned the first two hadiths may bear. In this hadith is: except what is indispensable, proving he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not intend by the second hadith all building, but only part of it. We examined what is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides that on this meaning."
"1079","957 - We found Yunus narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ibn Wahb; reported to me by Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever builds a building without injustice or transgression, or plants a planting without injustice or transgression, his reward continues so long as anyone of Allah's creation, Blessed and Most High, benefits from it. What is in this hadith proves the permissibility of building what anyone of Allah's creation benefits from, without injustice or transgression, and it is the exception from what is in the second hadith, and Allah knows best. We examined what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about taking upper rooms along with the building carrying them."
"1080","958 - We found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, both saying: narrated to us by Umar ibn Yunus ibn al-Qasim al-Yamami, narrated to us by Ikrimah ibn Ammar al-Ijli, from Abu Zumayl who said: narrated to me by Ibn Abbas who said: narrated to me by Umar ibn al-Khattab who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) secluded himself from his wives, I entered the mosque, and the people were striking pebbles and saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced his wives. I came to Hafsah and said to her: Where is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: He is in his storeroom in the upper room. I entered, and there was Rabah, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sitting on the threshold of the upper room, dangling his legs over a notched piece of wood, a trunk upon which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbed and descended. I called: Rabah, Rabah, ask permission for me to see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Rabah looked at the upper room, then looked at me, and said nothing to me. I said: Rabah, ask permission for me with you to see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He did the same and said nothing. I raised my voice and said: Rabah, ask permission for me to see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for I think the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thinks I came because of Hafsah. By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to strike her neck, I would strike her neck. I raised my voice, and he gestured to me with his hand to push him. I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was reclining on a mat, and he mentioned the story of zihar. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and I descended clinging to the trunk, while he descended as if walking on the ground."
"1081","959 - And we found Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh and Fahd narrated to us, both saying: narrated to us by Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi, narrated to us by Shu'bah, from al-Furat al-Qazzaz, from Abi al-Tufayl, from Abu Saraihah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked down upon us from an upper room and said: What do you mention and say? We said: Messenger of Allah, the Hour. He said: It shall not come until you see ten signs: a sinking in the east, a sinking in the west, a sinking in the Arabian Peninsula, Gog and Magog, the Beast, the Smoke, the Antichrist, the descent of Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him), the rising of the sun from its west, and a fire emerging from the bottom of Aden, resting with them when they rest at noon and going with them when they go."
"1082","960 - And we found al-Hasan ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by al-Firyabi, narrated to us by Sufyan, from Furat al-Qazzaz, from Abi al-Tufayl, from Hudhayfah ibn Asid who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked down upon us and said: The Hour shall not come until you see ten signs, then he related the signs in the hadith before this, except that he did not say in it: resting with them when they rest, to the end of the hadith."
"1083","961 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar al-Kufi, narrated to us by Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah, from Furat al-Qazzaz; narrated to me by Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah, from Hudhayfah ibn Asid Abu Saraihah, who was among the companions of the tree, who said: We were in the shade of a wall in an upper room belonging to Aishah, Mother of the Believers, discussing the Hour, then he mentioned the first hadith exactly. In it he said: driving the people, resting them; when they rest it drives them to a white land upon which no sin was ever done."
"1084","962 - And we found al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by al-Haytham ibn Jamil, saying: narrated to us by Sharik ibn Abd Allah, from Furat, from Abi al-Tufayl, from Hudhayfah, then he mentioned the same, except that he said: Until there are ten signs, the first of them the rising of the sun from its west. Then he mentioned the rest of the signs, except that he said: And a fire emerging from Yemen, from the bottom of Aden, driving to the gathering place. He did not mention what comes after that. Abu Ja'far said: The mashrubah is the upper room. What we mentioned proves there is no contradiction in anything we narrated in this chapter of the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we narrated from him in it, and that taking upper rooms and other lower parts, without injustice or transgression, of what is benefited from, is permitted, not forbidden. We ask Allah for success."
"1085","Chapter 146 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: The day the sky brings a clear smoke. 963 - Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath, narrated to us by my father, from al-A'mash who said: narrated to us by Muslim, who is Abu al-Duha, from Masruq who said: A man in the mosque narrated to me and mentioned: The day the sky brings a clear smoke, and said: When the Day of Resurrection comes, smoke will afflict people, seizing the hearing and sight of the hypocrites, and afflicting the believers like a cold. I entered upon Abd Allah and mentioned that to him while he was reclining, so he sat up angry and said: O people, whoever among you knows something, let him say it; whoever does not know, let him say: Allah knows best. For it is part of knowledge, when a man is asked about what he does not know, to say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knows best. He, Mighty and Majestic, said to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Say: I ask you no wage for it, nor am I of the pretenders. I shall tell you about that: Quraysh resisted and turned away, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed against them, and it was said to him: Watch for the day the sky brings a clear smoke. A year seized them, consuming everything, until they ate carrion and bones, until a man would see between him and the sky like smoke from hardship. They said: Our Lord, remove the punishment from us, indeed we believe. Then he recited: Indeed We shall remove the punishment a little; indeed you will return. It was removed from them, and they returned to their disbelief: The day We seize with the greatest seizure, indeed We shall take revenge. They returned to their disbelief, so Allah seized them on the day of Badr. Had it been the Day of Resurrection, it would not have been removed from them."
"1086","964 - Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi, narrated to us by Sufyan, narrated to us by al-A'mash and Mansur, from Abi al-Duha, from Masruq who said: While a man was narrating in Kindah, then he mentioned the same, except that he said in it: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon them and said: O Allah, help me against them with seven like the seven of Yusuf. In this hadith is that the smoke mentioned in the verse cited in it and in the hadith before it is among the signs that already passed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That is also reported from Ibn Mas'ud as his own statement in other than this hadith."
"1087","As narrated to us by Yazid ibn Sinan, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan, narrated to us by Fitr ibn Khalifah; narrated to me by Muslim ibn Subayh who said: I heard Masruq say: Abd Allah said: Five have passed: the smoke, the moon, the Romans, the greatest seizure, and the collar."
"1088","And as narrated to us by Ibn Abi Maryam, narrated to us by al-Firyabi, narrated to us by Fitr ibn Khalifah, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1089","And as narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Umar ibn Hafs, narrated to us by my father, narrated to us by al-A'mash, narrated to us by Muslim, from Masruq who said: Abd Allah said, then he mentioned the same, and added: Then it shall be a collar. An objector said: How do you accept this, when you reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, in the hadith of Hudhayfah ibn Asid, requiring that the smoke has not yet been and shall be before the Day of Resurrection; and what is reported from Abu Hurayrah confirming that."
"1090","965 - Narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb, narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Muhammad known as al-Baytari, narrated to us by Sulayman ibn Bilal, narrated to us by al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten to deeds six: the rising of the sun from its west, or the smoke, or the Antichrist, or the Beast, or the Hour. He did not mention to us in the hadith other than this. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that the smoke mentioned in the hadiths of Ibn Mas'ud is other than the smoke mentioned in the two hadiths of Hudhayfah and Abu Hurayrah. For Allah said in His Book in Surah al-Dukhan: Nay, they are in doubt, playing. Then He followed it with His statement: So watch for the day the sky brings a clear smoke, meaning: as punishment for them for their doubt and play. It is impossible that these two punishments be for others, or brought after their leaving this world and safety from that smoke. This objector said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said in this surah: So watch for the day the sky brings a clear smoke, while what Ibn Mas'ud mentioned in his hadith is not real smoke, but something Quraysh imagined as smoke though it is not smoke; and in it its coming is from the sky, while in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud there is not that, but only that they saw, from the hunger that befell them on earth, smoke between them and the sky. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that what is in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud was called smoke figuratively, because Quraysh imagined it as real smoke from the hardship upon them, though in reality it was not so, like what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the story of the Antichrist: that he commands the sky and it rains, and commands the earth and it grows, in the hadith of al-Nawwas ibn Sam'an unrestricted thus, and in the hadith of Jabir ibn Abd Allah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise, and in it: And with him are two rivers, I know them better than he; and in it: And he commands the sky and it rains in the people's view. That proves what is in the hadith of al-Nawwas is only of the Antichrist's sorcery, not of reality. We shall mention this later in this book about what is reported on the Antichrist from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah willing. So what Quraysh saw of what they saw as smoke may be called smoke figuratively, though in reality otherwise. As for the statement of Allah, Majestic and Mighty: The day the sky brings a clear smoke, it is what is reported in it from Ibn Mas'ud as cited in his hadiths we narrated from him, explained by that the ascription to the sky was, and Allah knows best, because things befalling people from their Lord, Mighty and Majestic, are ascribed to the sky; from that His statement: He directs the affair from the sky to the earth. He, Majestic and Mighty, reported that affairs in the earth are directed from the sky to it. Like that is what was of His direction, Majestic and Mighty, in the cause by which He punished Quraysh for its disbelief and insolence, until they saw from that punishment smoke, though not really so. As for what is in the two hadiths of Hudhayfah and Abu Hurayrah of smoke, it is real smoke of what shall be near the Hour. We ask Allah Most High for good outcomes in this world and the Hereafter, and Him we ask for success."
"1091","Chapter 147 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his seeking forgiveness in his prayer over a small deceased. 966 - Narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, both saying: narrated to us by Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, narrated to us by Hammam ibn Yahya, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, that he witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray over the deceased. He said: I heard him say: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our present and our absent, our young and our old, our male and our female. Yahya said: And Abu Salamah narrated to me these and added in it: Whom among us You keep alive, keep him alive upon faith; whom among us You take, take him upon Islam."
"1092","967 - Narrated to us by Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb ibn Nasib, narrated to us by Hammam ibn Yahya, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1093","968 - Narrated to us by Ibn Khuzaymah, narrated to us by Hajjaj ibn Minhal, narrated to us by Hammam, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1094","969 - Narrated to us by Yunus, narrated to us by Bishr ibn Bakr, narrated to us by al-Awza'i. And narrated to me by Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb, narrated to us by Bishr ibn Bakr, narrated to us by al-Awza'i; then both together said: He said: narrated to me by Yahya ibn Abi Kathir; narrated to me by Abu Ibrahim al-Ansari, a man of Banu Abd al-Ashhal; narrated to me by my father, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in prayer over the deceased, then he mentioned the same."
"1095","970 - And narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb, narrated to us by Isma'il ibn Mas'ud, narrated to us by Yazid, who is Ibn Zuray', narrated to us by Hisham, who is Ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abi Ibrahim al-Ansari, from his father, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, then mentioned the same, except that he did not mention of what we mentioned before it from Abu Salamah."
"1096","971 - Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Kathir, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abi Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in prayer over the deceased, then he mentioned the hadiths we mentioned before it."
"1097","972 - Narrated to us by Bakkar, Yazid ibn Sinan, and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, saying: narrated to us by Umar ibn Yunus, narrated to us by Ikrimah ibn Ammar, narrated to us by Yahya, from Abi Salamah who said: I asked Aishah: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over the deceased, then he mentioned exactly what is in the first hadiths."
"1098","973 - Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Buhlul, narrated to us by Abdah ibn Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Abi Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a funeral, then he mentioned what is in the first hadiths exactly."
"1099","974 - Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud, narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus, narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Thabit al-Thumali, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a funeral, then he mentioned what is in the first hadiths exactly. We examined what is in these hadiths of the seeking of forgiveness by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for young ones who have no sins, like his seeking forgiveness for elders with sins, since someone asked about uncovering that. We found for it a sound meaning: his asking (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) their Lord to forgive them the sins they will commit after leaving childhood for adulthood, so they are forgiven in advance and not written upon them, and they are not seized for them. Like the statement of Allah to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That Allah may forgive you your past and future sins. That was forgiveness from Him for what he had not yet done, so that in doing it it is forgiven, pardoned, not written upon him. Like the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umar in the story of Hatib: What tells you; perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you. We shall mention that and what is reported in it later in this book, Allah willing. Like that is the asking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, for forgiveness for the young: upon this meaning, forgiveness for what they will commit after puberty of sins, which, had this supplication from him not been, they would have been seized for and punished for; so by the supplication of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this supplication they return unseized and unpunished for them. We ask Allah for success."
"1100","Chapter 148 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his statement in prayer over the deceased mixed with supplication for him: And we know only good. 975 - Narrated to us by Abu Umayyah and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, both saying: narrated to us by Abu Umar al-Hawdi, narrated to us by Hammam ibn Yahya, narrated to us by Layth, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in prayer: O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, set right our relations, and join our hearts. O Allah, this is Your servant so-and-so son of so-and-so, and we know only good, and You know him better, so forgive us and him. I said, and I was the youngest of the people: If he did not know good? He said: Then he says only what he knows. What is in this hadith of his statement: and we know only good, is among what needs uncovering to grasp its meaning. We uncovered it until we grasped that, by al-Harith's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he asked him in it, and the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him with what he answered him in it. This al-Harith, in our view, and Allah knows best, is Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, who is al-Harith ibn Rib'i, and his son mentioned in it is Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah al-Ansari, from whom Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated the first hadiths we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. We know, had it not been for al-Harith's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he asked him in this hadith, and the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say in it: and we know only good, that he would not have said that while knowing other than good of him. Maymun ibn Mihran, in his prayer over one of whom he knew other than good, used to say in it what."
"1101","Narrated to us by Fahd, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi, narrated to us by Abu al-Malih, from al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Raqqi, from Maymun ibn Mihran who said: When you pray over one suspected among the people of innovated desires, it suffices you to say: Our Lord, You embrace all things in mercy and knowledge, so forgive those who repent and follow Your path, to the end of the verse. When you pray over one you love, strive in supplication. Abu Ja'far said: These people of desires are those who do not leave Islam by them, nor, though blameworthy for them, are they barred from prayer over them, as prayer is performed over other blameworthy Muslims, as was prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and by his command over one who stole from the spoils in the path of Allah, as we mentioned earlier in this book. As for one upon any desire that takes him out of Islam, no prayer is performed over him, for he is not of the people of the religions whose people are prayed over. Success is from Allah."
"1102","Chapter 149 - Explanation of the problematic hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his leaving the inheritance of his freedman who fell from a palm tree and died, ordering its payment to the people of his village. 976 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani, narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Mujahid ibn Wardan, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that a freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and he said: Is anyone here of the people of his village? And he gave it to him."
"1103","977 - Narrated to us by Ali ibn Shaybah, narrated to us by Yazid ibn Harun, reported to us by Sufyan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Mujahid, from Urwah, from Aishah, that a freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell from a palm tree and died. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: See whether he has an heir? They said: No. He said: Give it to some relative."
"1104","978 - Narrated to us by al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin, narrated to us by Asad ibn Musa, narrated to us by Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Mujahid ibn Wardan, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah who said: A freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell from a date-cluster of a palm tree and died, leaving something. That was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Did he leave a child or close kin? They said: No. He said: Look to the people of his village and pay it to them."
"1105","979 - Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb, narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Tamim, narrated to us by Hajjaj ibn Muhammad; narrated to me by Shu'bah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Mujahid ibn Wardan, a man of the people of Madinah, and he praised him well, from Urwah, from Aishah, the same. Other than Ibn al-Asbahani, Rabi'ah ibn Sayf al-Ma'afiri narrated from this Mujahid."
"1106","980 - As narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri, narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, narrated to us by Rabi'ah ibn Sayf, from Mujahid, from Urwah, from Aishah, that she was with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq when death came to him and recited verses. He said: Do not say this, my daughter, but say: And the agony of death comes in truth; that is what you were avoiding. Then he said: My daughter, in how many garments was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded? She said: In three garments. He said: Shroud me in these two garments of mine and buy a third with them; the living need new more than the dead; they are only for discharge, meaning pus. Thus say the people of hadith, while others among the people of language say: for discharge, with kasr of the mim. Al-Bukhari mentioned that this Mujahid is of the people of Madinah, and that among those who narrated from him is Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah. Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim mentioned from Malik ibn Anas that Kharijah ibn Zayd and Mujahid used to divide for people in Madinah without wage, and it was not known which Mujahid Malik meant of what we established, so we knew it is this Mujahid. By what we mentioned we intended it be known that he is other than Mujahid ibn Jabr, since Mujahid ibn Jabr was at times in Makkah and at times in Kufah, with no mention among the people of Madinah. An objector said: What was the meaning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving the inheritance of this deceased, he being his freedman whose patronage obliges the inheritance of the patron, and paying it to the people of his village who have nothing of his inheritance? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that Allah honored him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), raised his station, placed him in the highest ranks of this world and the Hereafter, and removed him from the morals of others among desirers of this world. Among what was revealed to him: Nay, you do not honor the orphan, nor urge feeding the poor, and you devour inheritance greedily, and you love wealth greatly. He described them with blameworthy morals, placing them in a low station. He removed him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from that to the highest stations, and made his ruling of what He removed him to the highest rulings: He did not make him of those who inherit from others, whether kin, patron, or marriage. He distinguished between him and the rest of his community in that, increase in his merit, honoring him, and raising his station in it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered in the inheritance of his freedman mentioned in this hadith, since he had no child nor close kin deserving his inheritance, that his inheritance be paid to the people of his village, as imams do with ownerless wealth, paying it to whom they see fit among people. If an objector says: Among the prophets of Allah, blessings upon them, were those who inherit and are inherited, from that what the Majestic and Mighty related in His Book from His prophet Zakariyya (peace be upon him), his asking Him to grant him from Himself a guardian to inherit him and inherit from the family of Ya'qub (peace be upon him), and to make him a prophet, and His answering him and granting him Yahya (peace be upon him) and mending his wife for him. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that what Zakariyya (peace be upon him) asked his Lord to grant him of one to inherit him was not wealth to inherit from him, and what wealth did he (peace be upon him) have; he was an ascetic carpenter working with his hand."
"1107","981 - As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi, narrated to us by Affan ibn Muslim, narrated to us by Hammad ibn Salamah, from Thabit al-Bunani; Abu Ja'far said: from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Zakariyya (peace be upon him) was a carpenter. Abu Ja'far said: Since he (peace be upon him) was a carpenter, not of the wealthy, we understood thereby that what he asked his Lord that his granted one inherit from him is other than wealth, namely prophethood, like what he asked to inherit from the family of Ya'qub (peace be upon him). Likewise all the prophets of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, blessings upon them: they did not bequeath dinar nor dirham, but bequeathed knowledge."
"1108","982 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Dawud al-Khuraybi, from Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywah, from Dawud ibn Jamil, from Kathir ibn Qays who said: I was sitting with Abu al-Darda in the mosque of Damascus when a man came to him and said: Abu al-Darda, I came to you from Madinah, the city of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for a hadith I heard you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Did you come for no need? He said: No. He said: Did you come for no trade? He said: No. He said: Did you come only for this hadith? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever travels a path seeking knowledge, Allah takes him by a path of the paths of Paradise; the angels lower their wings in pleasure for the seeker of knowledge; the merit of the scholar over the worshipper is like the merit of the moon on the night of full moon over all stars; the scholar is prayed for by those in the heavens and earth and everything, even whales in the water. The scholars are heirs of the prophets; the prophets, blessings upon them, did not bequeath dinar nor dirham, but bequeathed knowledge; whoever takes it takes an ample share. Abu Ja'far said: Zakariyya (peace be upon him) is among them, so he bequeathed nothing of wealth. If an objector says: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And Sulayman inherited Dawud. That, in our view, and Allah knows best, is what the prophets bequeath of what is besides wealth. If he says: Sulayman was already a prophet in the lifetime of Dawud, blessings upon them; what did he inherit from him? It is said to him: He inherited his wisdom and what is inherited from his like, added to the prophethood already with him before that. If he says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inherited his parents, inheriting from his father his house and his slave Umm Ayman and Shuqran, whom he freed as his two freedmen? It is said to him: That was before Allah gave him prophethood; when He gave it to him, He returned his rulings to the rulings upon which He took him: preventing inheritance from others, and preventing others' inheritance from him. People inherit only as they are inherited; since he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not inherited, he is not an inheritor. In what we mentioned is clarification of what we described. We ask Allah for success."
"1109","Chapter 150 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement: Whatever I left after the maintenance of my family and the provision of my worker is charity. 983 - Narrated to us by Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Wahb, that Malik narrated to him, saying: narrated to me by Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My heirs shall not divide a dinar; whatever I left after the maintenance of my family and the provision of my worker is charity."
"1110","984 - Narrated to us by al-Muzani, narrated to us by al-Shafi'i, narrated to us by Sufyan, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My heirs shall not divide a dinar; whatever I left after the maintenance of my family and the provision of my worker is charity; my heirs shall not divide a dinar. A questioner asked about the meaning of his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whatever I left after the maintenance of my family and the provision of my worker is charity. He said: The family intended here are his wives, and the marriage between him and them is cut from them by his death; what then is the meaning of maintenance upon them? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that his wives after his death are confined to him, forbidden to others, to be his wives in Paradise. Since they are so, all that was due to them upon him in his life by the marriage between him and them remains due to them upon him after his death as it was due to them upon him in his life. If he says: What is the meaning of his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My heirs shall not divide a dinar, affirming thereby that he has heirs, though he is not inherited, and whoever is not inherited has no heirs? It is said: That, in our view, and Allah knows best, is metaphorical, meaning: Those who would inherit me had I been inherited shall not divide a dinar; whatever I left is charity, for I am not inherited. Success is from Allah."
"1111","Chapter 151 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what is meant by the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, the verse. 985 - Narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Muhammad ibn al-Salt, narrated to us by Abu Kudaynah, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas who said: When this verse came down: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, the verse, the polytheists said: Isa (peace be upon him) is worshipped, and Uzayr (peace be upon him), and the sun and moon. So Allah sent down: Those for whom good has preceded from Us, those, meaning Isa and Uzayr, blessings upon them."
"1112","986 - Narrated to us by Ubayd ibn Rijal, narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani, narrated to us by Yahya ibn Adam, narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Asim, from Abi Razin, from Abi Yahya, from Ibn Abbas who said: A verse in the Book of Allah people do not ask me about, and I do not know whether they know it and so do not ask me, or are ignorant of it and so do not ask me? It was said: What is it? He said: A verse when it came down: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, you shall enter it, it distressed the people of Makkah, and they said: Muhammad has insulted our gods. Ibn al-Zib'ara stood and said: What is wrong with you? They said: Muhammad has insulted our gods. He said: What did he say? They said: He said: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, you shall enter it. He said: Call him to me. Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called, and Ibn al-Zib'ara said: Muhammad, is this for our gods خاص, or for all worshipped besides Allah? He said: For all worshipped besides Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He said: Then he said: We have defeated him, by the Lord of this building. Muhammad, do you not claim Isa is a righteous servant, Uzayr a righteous servant, and the angels righteous servants? He said: Yes. He said: These Christians worship Isa, these Jews worship Uzayr, and these Banu Malih worship the angels. He said: The people of Makkah clamored, and there came down: Those for whom good has preceded from Us, meaning Isa, Uzayr, and the angels, those are far removed from it. He said: And there came down: When the son of Maryam is cited as an example, your people laugh at it, and it is correct."
"1113","987 - Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari, narrated to us by Hisham ibn Ammar, narrated to us by al-Walid ibn Muslim, narrated to us by Shayban, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abi Razin, from Abi Yahya, freedman of Ibn Afra al-Ansari, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Quraysh: O company of Quraysh, there is no good with one worshipped besides Allah, Mighty and Majestic. They said: Do you not claim Isa (peace be upon him) was a prophet and righteous servant? If you are truthful, he is like their gods. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: When the son of Maryam is cited as an example, your people laugh at it, meaning they clamor, and he is a sign of the Hour. He said: It is the emergence of Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him) before the Day of Resurrection. Thus he said: a sign, with fath. Abu Ja'far said: This Abu Yahya was narrated from by both Makkans and Kufans."
"1114","988 - Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Dawud, narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah, narrated to us by Yazid ibn Abi Hakim, narrated to us by al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: Abd Allah ibn al-Zib'ara came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Muhammad, you claim Allah revealed to you this verse: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, you shall enter it. The sun, moon, angels, Uzayr, and Isa, blessings upon them, have been worshipped; are all these in the Fire with our gods? So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: Those for whom good has preceded from Us, those are far removed from it. And there came down: When the son of Maryam is cited as an example, your people laugh at it. Abu Ja'far said: An objector said: In these reports, upon the revelation of the first of these two verses in this hadith, the polytheists clamored and argued to the Muslims: Isa is worshipped, Uzayr is worshipped, and those mentioned with them in this hadith, though with their polytheism they are eloquent, not of those upon whose tongues solecism runs in speech. And (ma) is said only for other than humans, and in its place for humans is said (man), as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Whoever of them says: I am a god besides Him, and: Whoever does that shall meet punishment, in likes of that intending humans. And He said for other than humans: What the beast eats except what you slaughter, and what is slaughtered upon altars, for other than humans. In what you narrated attributing to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you mentioned in this hadith of this kind, and in one of the two verses you recited in it: You and what you worship besides Allah are fuel of Hell, you shall enter it, humans were intended. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that (man) and (ma) in most Arab speech occur as mentioned, but Arabs also use (ma) for humans as they use (man), though that is not frequent for them as (man) is for them. From that is the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: And chaste women except what your rights possess, in place of except whom your rights possess. And His statement: Whatever is in the heavens and earth glorifies Allah. And His statement: By the father and what he begot, meaning Adam (peace be upon him) and what he begot. In what we mentioned is proof of what we described. In what we narrated in these reports is proof that the view on the disputed reading of the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: When your people laugh at it, with kasr, and laugh, with damm, is as whoever read it with kasr read it; for whoever read it with damm intended turning away, and whoever read it with kasr intended clamor. Its revelation was at the clamor of the polytheists, as this first of the two verses mentioned in this hadith came down. This reading is also sounder in meaning with the people of language; had it been upon turning away it would have been: when your people turn away from him, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Those who disbelieve and bar from the path of Allah, and as He said: Those who disbelieve and barred from the path of Allah, He led their deeds astray, and as He said: And they are barred from the path, and as He said: And they barred you from the Sacred Mosque."
"1115","It is reported from Ibn Abbas his rejection of the reading: When your people laugh at it, with damm. As narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Zayd, narrated to us by Nu'aym ibn Hammad, narrated to us by Waki', from Abd Allah ibn Habib, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazza, from Sa'id ibn Ma'bad, who said: Ibn Abbas said to me: Your uncle Ubayd ibn Umayr, how does he solecize in this? He reads: When your people laugh at it, with damm, while it is: laugh, meaning they clamor? Ibn Abbas thus reported in this hadith the true reading of this word, and most Kufans read it so. An objector said: It is reported from Ali ibn Abi Talib that the revelation of: Those for whom good has preceded from Us, those are far removed from it, is in contrary to the meaning you reported from Ibn Abbas that its revelation was in."
"1116","And he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, both saying: narrated to us by Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Abi Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Sa'd, from Muhammad ibn Hatib who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, preaching, reciting this verse: Those for whom good has preceded from Us, the verse. He said: It came down about Uthman and his companions, or he said: Uthman is among them. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that Ali may have meant by what is reported from him in this hadith that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, is among those for whom good preceded, mentioned in this verse; for the verse came down about whoever good preceded for him from Allah, Mighty and Majestic: among them Isa, among them Uzayr, among them the angels, and among them others for whom good preceded from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, among them Uthman and his companions. It became clear, by Allah's praise and favor, that all we narrated in this chapter contradicts nothing of it."
"1117","Chapter 152 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement: Mudar shall not leave a servant of Allah but they tempt or kill him. 989 - Narrated to us by Abu Umayyah, narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Numayr, from al-A'mash, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, from Amr ibn Hanzalah who said: Hudhayfah said: Mudar shall not leave a believing servant of Allah but they tempt him or kill him, or Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the angels and believers strike them until they cannot protect the tail of a mountain path. A man said to him: Abu Abd Allah, do you say this though you are a man of Mudar? He said: Shall I not say what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said?"
"1118","990 - Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud, narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Ziyad Sabalan, narrated to us by Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi, narrated to us by Mujalid, from Abi al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mudar shall strike the servants of Allah until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is not worshipped, or the believers shall strike them until they cannot protect the tail of a mountain path. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is mention of Mudar as mentioned in it, and intended thereby, and Allah knows best, is the blameworthy among them, apart from others who do not do their deed mentioned about them in this hadith. This hadith is also narrated from another route, directing what is mentioned in it to the unjust of Mudar, apart from others of Mudar."
"1119","991 - As narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, narrated to us by Wahb ibn Jarir, narrated to us by my father who said: I heard al-A'mash narrating, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil who said: We came to Hudhayfah when Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, and Rabi'ah, Mudar, and Yemen crowded over his room and house. He said: The injustice of Mudar shall not cease with every believing servant, tempting and killing him, or Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the believers strike them until they cannot protect the tail of a mountain path. A man said to him: Do you say this though you are of Mudar? He turned to him and said: Shall I not say what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? Abu Ja'far said: A questioner asked about the generality of Mudar in what it was generalized with in these reports we narrated. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that that speech, though unrestricted about Mudar, intended only those of them from whom the cause for which the statement was said came, apart from others of them. Arabs do that with broad things, ascribing what was from some of its people to all its people, intending only those of its people from whom the thing came, apart from others from whom it did not. From it is the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And your people denied it though it is truth, intending only those of his people who denied it, apart from others of them. From that is what was of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his qunut in Fajr prayer: O Allah, harden Your pressure upon Mudar, and make them years like the years of Yusuf (peace be upon him). We mentioned that with chains earlier in this book, and he did not intend thereby all Mudar. How could he intend thereby all Mudar, when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is of Mudar, and those behind him in that prayer are its best of Mudar? He intended thereby of Mudar whoever is contrary to what he is upon and contrary to those of them in that prayer with him. Like that is his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Mudar shall not leave a believing servant of Allah but they tempt him: upon this meaning, intended thereby is whoever of them does that deed, not others of them. We ask Allah for success."
"1120","Chapter 153 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the name of the prayer following Maghrib of the five prayers. 992 - Narrated to us by Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, both saying: narrated to us by Abu Amir, saying: narrated to us by Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Labid, from Abi Salamah, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let not the Bedouins overcome you for the name of your prayer; it is Isha, but they darken from their camels. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the report of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the name of that prayer is Isha, not Atamah, and that those who call it Atamah are the Bedouins. Then we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his calling it Atamah."
"1121","993 - As narrated to us by Fahd ibn Sulayman who said: narrated to us by Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi who said: narrated to us by Hariz ibn Uthman who said: narrated to me by Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Asim ibn Humayd al-Sukuni, companion of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal who said: We waited for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the Atamah prayer one night, and he delayed it until one thought he had prayed or would not come out. Then he came out, and one said to him: We thought you had prayed, or would not come out. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delay this prayer; you have been favored by it over all nations; no nation before you prayed it. An objector said: In this hadith is the naming by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it as Atamah. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that in this hadith is no naming by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it as Atamah; what is in it is his ordering them to darken with it, meaning to delay it, though its name is Isha, not Atamah. As you say: I eveninged with the Asr prayer, not because evening is its name, but reporting that you eveninged with it, its name not derived from eveninging with it. Another objector also said: Other than this hadith is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in which he confirmed its name as Atamah."
"1122","994 - As narrated to us by Yunus who said: narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, that Malik narrated to him, from Sumayy, freedman of Abu Bakr, from Abi Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had people known what is in the call and first row, then found only casting lots for it, they would cast lots; had they known what is in early going, they would race to it; had they known what is in Atamah and Subh, they would come to them even crawling. Our answer to him also, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that this hadith was narrated by Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as mentioned, while Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to that."
"1123","995 - As narrated to us by Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi who said: narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Amr al-Ash'athi who said: narrated to us by Abthar ibn al-Qasim Abu Zubayd, from al-A'mash, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer is heavier upon the hypocrites than Isha and Fajr prayers; had they known the merit in them, they would come to them even crawling. This Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that its name is Isha, in place of what Abu Hurayrah narrated from him that its name is Atamah. The correction of these two hadiths is that what Arabs knew for this prayer's name was Atamah, not Isha, and the cause of their naming it that name is what is mentioned in the hadith of Abu Salamah from Ibn Umar which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed to His Messenger: O you who believe, let those your rights possess and those not reaching puberty among you ask permission three times: before Fajr prayer, when you lay your garments at noon, and after Isha prayer; three privacies for you. So they moved to its name which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, named it in this verse. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what he said in the hadith of Ibn Umar which we narrated. We thereby understood that what Ibn Mas'ud related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of its name, which he mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah as Atamah, and Allah knows best. An objector said: What is the meaning of this name, meaning Isha? Our answer to him, and Allah knows best, is that it is taken from the darkness that dims sight, and this prayer's name is referred to like the names of the other five prayers; for Subh is named Subh because it is prayed at morning, Fajr prayer is named Fajr because it is prayed near dawn, Zuhr prayer is named Zuhr because it is prayed at midday, Asr prayer is named Asr because it is prayed after delay, which is lateness. Likewise is reported from Abu Qilabah."
"1124","As narrated to us by Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari who said: narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Mansur who said: narrated to us by Hushaym who said: reported to us by Khalid, from Abi Qilabah: It was named Asr to be delayed. Abu Ja'far said: From it is the Arabs' saying: So-and-so delayed my right, when he postponed it past its time to him; and from it the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Fadalal al-Laythi."
"1125","996 - As narrated to us by Yazid ibn Sinan who said: narrated to us by al-Hasan ibn Ali, meaning al-Wasiti, who said: narrated to us by Khalid ibn Abd Allah. And as narrated to us by Fahd ibn Sulayman who said: narrated to us by Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti who said: narrated to us by Khalid. Then both together, each saying: from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abi Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Abd Allah ibn Fadalah, from his father who said: I said: Messenger of Allah, teach me something by which Allah benefits me. He said: Guard the five prayers. I said: Messenger of Allah, these are hours in which I am busy; order me with a comprehensive matter which if I do suffices me. He said: Guard the two Asrs. I said: What are the two Asrs? They were not of our language. He said: A prayer before sunset, and a prayer before sunrise."
"1126","997 - As narrated to us by Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman who said: narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Mansur who said: narrated to us by Hushaym, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind who said: narrated to me by Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Fadalal al-Laythi. Thus he said, then mentioned this hadith, and did not mention in it his statement: They were not of our language. Abu Ja'far said: From it is the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith reported from him on this meaning also."
"1127","998 - It is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Hudbah ibn Khalid who said: And narrated to us by Hammam who said: narrated to us by Abu Jamrah al-Duba'i, from Abu Bakr, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever prays the two Asrs shall enter Paradise. Maghrib prayer was named Maghrib because it is prayed right after sunset; likewise the prayer following it was named Isha because it is prayed after sight is dimmed by the arriving darkness. The names of the five prayers thus agree as for their times in which they are prayed. It became clear, by Allah's praise and favor, that there is no contradiction in anything we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any of their names. We ask Allah for success."
"1128","Chapter 154 - Explanation of the problematic hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend; your companion is the close friend of Allah. 999 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq who said: narrated to us by Wahb ibn Jarir who said: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend."
"1129","1000 - Narrated to us by Fahd ibn Sulayman who said: narrated to us by Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi, who said: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, adding: But my brother and companion."
"1130","1001 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq who said: narrated to us by Wahb ibn Jarir who said: narrated to us by my father who said: I heard Ya'la ibn Hakim narrating, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out in his illness in which he died, binding his head with a cloth, sat upon the pulpit, praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and extolled Him, then said: No one of people is more beneficent to me with himself and wealth than Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah; had I taken among people a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend, but the brotherhood of Islam is better. Close every small door in the mosque except the small door of Abu Bakr."
"1131","1002 - Narrated to us by Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la who said: narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Wahb who said: narrated to me by Malik ibn Anas, from Abi al-Nadr, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, who said: Yunus, I think, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend."
"1132","1003 - Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb who said: narrated to me by my uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb who said: reported to me by Malik, from Abi al-Nadr, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated of this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his informing people that had he taken a close friend he would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend; in that is repulsion that anyone of people besides him be his close friend. Some used to reject against whoever narrated from his Companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, their saying: I heard my close friend, and: My close friend said. Among those reported to reject that against whoever said it of them is Amir al-Sha'bi."
"1133","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Ali ibn Abd al-A'la al-Baghdadi, known as Juhaysh, who said: narrated to us by Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti who said: narrated to us by Isma'il ibn Zakariyya who said: narrated to us by Asim who said: I said to al-Sha'bi: Hafsah used to narrate to us from Umm Atiyyah, saying: My close friend narrated to me, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Sha'bi said: This is of women's minds. Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say before his death: Whoever had close friendship between me and him, I have returned it to him; had I taken a close friend of this community, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend? Abu Ja'far said: Then we uncovered what al-Khalil in this is, since al-Khalil in Arab speech may be from khullah, which is friendship, and may be from neediness of states."
"1134","1004 - We found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus who said: narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I disavow every close friend of his close friendship; had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr."
"1135","1005 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Yahya ibn Hammad who said: narrated to us by Abu Awanah, from Sulayman, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abi al-Ahwas who said: Abd Allah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I disavow every close friend of his close friendship; had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend; your companion is the close friend of Allah. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated of this is proof that the close friend mentioned in these reports is the friend, not the poor, and that the meaning by which he was called close friend in them is friendship and affection, not otherwise. We found this uncovered."
"1136","1006 - As narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud who said: narrated to us by Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi who said: narrated to us by Abu Awanah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ibn Abi al-Mu'alla, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken the son of Abu Quhafah; but love of faith, twice; your companion is the close friend of Allah."
"1137","1007 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from one of Banu Abi al-Mu'alla, a man of the Ansar, from his father, he being a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I taken among you a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend; but love and brotherhood of faith; your companion is the close friend of Allah. Abu Ja'far said: What is in these reports is proof of what we mentioned. These reports are also narrated with an added meaning over the meanings we mentioned in them in this chapter."
"1138","1008 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Ubayd Allah ibn Musa who said: narrated to us by Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend; your companion is the close friend of Allah."
"1139","And as narrated to us by Bakkar ibn Qutaybah who said: narrated to us by Abu Dawud who said: narrated to us by al-Mas'udi, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abi Wa'il, from Abd Allah, about the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: And Allah took Ibrahim as close friend: Indeed your companion is the close friend of Allah, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Resurrection is the noblest of creatures to Allah. Abd Allah recited: Perhaps your Lord shall raise you to a praised station. Abu Ja'far said: We needed to grasp the meaning of what is ascribed of that to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. We found one saying: Intended by close friend of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in this is the poor of Allah who placed his poverty and need only with Him, not with any creature. We found another saying: He is the lover with no breach in his love. We found another saying: He is the one خاص with love apart from other people. All these interpretations may bear what they were interpreted upon. Others said: It is alliance, as if they hold that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made him His ally, an alliance with none above it nor like it, so he deserved thereby the إطلاق of the name close friend from friendship. They adduced proof for what they said."
"1140","1009 - As narrated to us by Bakkar ibn Qutaybah who said: narrated to us by Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr who said: narrated to us by Sufyan, from his father, from Abi al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every prophet has allies among prophets; my ally among them is my father, the close friend of my Lord, Mighty and Majestic. Then he recited: The most worthy of Ibrahim are those who followed him, and this Prophet, and those who believe, the verse. They said: Since Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is his close friend, it can only be of the friendship which is utmost love; since the meaning of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, being his close friend is this meaning, the meaning by which he is close friend to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is that meaning too; Allah knows best His intent in that. Abu Ja'far said: Among what is adduced for the equality of alliance from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, among His creation, and the meaning of whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, allies among His creation, is that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Your ally is only Allah, His Messenger, and those who believe, the verse. And He said: My ally is Allah who sent down the Book, and He allies the righteous. And He said: You are my ally in this world and the Hereafter; take me as Muslim and join me with the righteous. And He said: Indeed the allies of Allah, no fear upon them nor shall they grieve, in likes of that He, Mighty and Majestic, mentioned in His Book. Alliance therein from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, toward whom He allies among His servants is like the alliance by which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, allies whom He allies, nothing else. Since alliance in what we mentioned is so, friendship in what we described is so. We ask Allah for success. A questioner asked about the reason the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not take Abu Bakr as close friend. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in the hadith of Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, which we narrated in this chapter, that it is better than it, namely the brotherhood of Islam. And for what he reported in the hadith of Abi al-Mu'alla of love of faith. Close friendship takes its lineage by affection which may exist without Islam with it, while what exists only by Islam or faith is better than that. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned Abu Bakr's station with him to that meaning, making it above close friend. We ask Allah for success."
"1141","Chapter 155 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement: Do not prefer me over Musa (peace be upon him), for the reason he mentioned in the hadith in which that is reported from him. 1010 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq who said: narrated to us by Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim who said: narrated to us by my father who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prefer me over Musa; people shall swoon on the Day of Resurrection and I shall be the first to recover, and Musa shall be holding the side of the Throne, and I shall not know whether he swooned among those who swooned and recovered before me, or was among those Allah, Mighty and Majestic, excepted. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning thereby what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, excepted by His statement: All in the heavens and earth shall swoon except whom Allah wills. He said: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade preferring him over Musa for the reason he mentioned in it. It may be that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before the things Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gave him and favored him by over all other people, which we shall mention after this chapter in a more fitting place in this book, Allah willing. It may be that is not included in them; for when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recovered from his swoon he found Musa (peace be upon him) in the state he found him, so he considered thereby that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, may have excepted him among those He excepted in the verse we recited, preferring him thereby over others. It may be he was among those who swooned, not entering the mentioned exception, thus not preferring thereby the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to stop at that ambiguity from preferring either of him and Musa over the other; Allah knows best its true reality. Him we ask for success."
"1142","Chapter 156 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his forbidding saying: He is better than Yunus ibn Matta. 1011 - Narrated to us by Bakkar ibn Qutaybah who said: narrated to us by Wahb ibn Jarir who said: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Abi al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: It is not fitting for anyone to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta."
"1143","1012 - Narrated to us by Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani who said: narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad who said: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim who said: I heard Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: It is not fitting for a servant of Mine to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta. Abu Ja'far said: We needed to grasp the reason for which what is said in this hadith was said, so we sought that."
"1144","1013 - We found al-Kaysani narrated to us, saying: narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad who said: narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah who said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Salamah narrating from Ali (peace be upon him), as if from Allah, then he mentioned the same. Adding: He glorified Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in darknesses. In this hadith is the reason Yunus was خاص with the meaning for which what is said of his matter was said, of what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. That statement may have been from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, favored him over all His creation, of what we shall mention reported in it later in this book, Allah willing. We ask Allah for success."
"1145","Chapter 157 - Explanation of the problematic answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to one who said to him: O best of creation, by his saying: That is Ibrahim (peace be upon him). 1014 - Narrated to us by Bakkar ibn Qutaybah who said: narrated to us by Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi who said: narrated to us by Sufyan, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful who said: I heard Anas say: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O best of creation. He said: That is my father Ibrahim (peace be upon him)."
"1146","1015 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basriyyan together; narrated to us by Abu Hudhayfah who said: narrated to us by Sufyan, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1147","1016 - Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah who said: narrated to us by Musaddad ibn Musarhad who said: narrated to us by Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sufyan, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1148","1017 - Narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq who said: narrated to us by Affan ibn Muslim who said: narrated to us by Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Ja'far said: What is in this hadith, in our view, and Allah knows best, may be that this statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was before Allah took him as close friend, when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had no close friend besides Ibrahim (peace be upon him); Ibrahim then excelled him by close friendship, close friendship being love with no love above it. When that man said to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O best of creation, and it being impossible that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, خاص for His love one among His servants above whom is another, he said to him: That is my father Ibrahim (peace be upon him). When Allah made him His close friend, he returned by close friendship from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to the meaning by which Ibrahim deserved in the hadith we narrated what his deserving is mentioned in. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became close friend to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, as Ibrahim was close friend to Him, so both became equal in close friendship from Him. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, خاص His Prophet, apart from Ibrahim, by mentioning him where Ibrahim is not mentioned, in the call to prayer and its establishments, by making him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned therein right after His mention, Mighty and Majestic; this was a station by which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was favored over all prophets, blessings upon them, in this world, and He gave him in the Hereafter the praised station He gave none other."
"1149","1018 - As narrated to us by Fahd ibn Sulayman who said: narrated to us by Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi al-Jurjusi who said: narrated to us by Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid who said: narrated to us by al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from Ka'b ibn Malik, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People shall be gathered on the Day of Resurrection, and I and my community shall be upon a hill; my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, shall clothe me in a green robe. Then I shall be permitted and say what Allah wills me to say; that is the praised station."
"1150","1019 - And as narrated to us by Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari who said: narrated to us by Amr ibn Uthman and Muhammad ibn al-Musaffa al-Himsiyyani, both saying: narrated to us by Baqiyyah, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"1151","1020 - And as narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq who said: narrated to us by Makki ibn Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say about the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Perhaps your Lord shall raise you to a praised station. He said: It is the station in which I intercede for my community."
"1152","1021 - And as narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud who said: narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus who said: narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, took Ibrahim (peace be upon him) as close friend, and your companion is the close friend of Allah. Then he recited: Perhaps your Lord shall raise you to a praised station. Abu Ja'far said: That praised station is of what Allah خاص him with in the Hereafter, giving it to none other of His prophets, blessings upon them, until the first and last envied him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for it."
"1153","1022 - As narrated to us by Harun ibn Kamil who said: narrated to us by Abd Allah ibn Salih who said: narrated to me by al-Layth ibn Sa'd who said: narrated to me by Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far who said: I heard Hamzah ibn Abd Allah say: I heard Abd Allah ibn Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man keeps asking people until he comes on the Day of Resurrection with no morsel of flesh on his face. And he said: The sun draws near until sweat reaches half the ear. While they are so, they seek help from Adam (peace be upon him), who says: I am not its man; then from Musa (peace be upon him), who says that; then from Muhammad, blessings upon him and upon them all, and he intercedes for judgment to be passed among creation. He walks until he takes the ring of Paradise; that day Allah raises him to a praised station, praised by all the gathering. Abu Ja'far said: Among what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, خاص him with besides that."
"1154","1023 - As narrated to us by al-Muzani who said: narrated to us by al-Shafi'i who said: narrated to us by Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given five none before me was given: the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, I was aided by awe, spoils were made lawful for me, I was sent to the red and white, and I was given intercession. Al-Muzani said to us: Al-Shafi'i said: Then I sat with Sufyan, and he mentioned this hadith, saying: al-Zuhri from Abu Salamah and Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned it."
"1155","1024 - And as narrated to us by Fahd who said: narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani who said: narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from Abi Malik al-Ashja'i, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We were favored over people by three: our rows were made like the rows of angels, all the earth was made for us a mosque, and its soil was made for us purification when we find no water. And I was given these verses from a treasure under the Throne: the endings of Surah al-Baqarah; none before me was given them, nor shall any after me be given them. Abu Ja'far said: In what we mentioned of this is confirmation of what we narrated in the chapter explaining the problematic: Had I taken a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as close friend. In what we narrated in it is the statement of Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, which he said only by divine instruction, for its like is said only by instruction, that Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the noblest of creatures to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. In what we mentioned of this chapter is proof that the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in answer to one who said to him: O best of creation: That is my father Ibrahim (peace be upon him), and what we narrated in the chapter after it of his statement: Do not prefer me over Musa (peace be upon him), and what we mentioned in the other chapter of his statement: It is not fitting for a servant to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta, was only before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gave him what we mentioned of His giving him in this chapter, the gifts by which He favored him over all His creation, until thereby he became superior to their first and last (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We ask Allah for success."
"1156","1025 - And narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Yazid who said: narrated to us by al-Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim who said: narrated to us by Isma'il ibn Ja'far, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was favored over prophets by six: I was given comprehensive speech, aided by awe, spoils made lawful for me, the earth made for me purification and mosque, sent to all creation, and prophets sealed by me. Abu Ja'far said: In this is mention of his favor (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over prophets, among them Ibrahim, blessings upon him and upon them all."
"1157","Chapter 158 - Explanation of the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his statement: Do not prefer among the prophets of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, blessings upon them all. 1026 - Narrated to us by Husayn ibn Nasr who said: narrated to us by Abu Nu'aym who said: narrated to us by Sufyan, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prefer among the prophets of Allah."
"1158","1027 - Narrated to us by Fahd ibn Sulayman who said: narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani who said: reported to us by Waki', from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1159","1028 - Narrated to us by Yunus who said: narrated to us by Nu'aym ibn Hammad who said: narrated to us by Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1160","1029 - Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Dawud who said: narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi who said: narrated to us by Abd al-Aziz al-Majishun, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl who said: narrated to me by al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, in a long hadith in it: Do not prefer among the prophets of Allah. Abu Ja'far said: This, in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon preferring among them and choosing among them by our opinions and what He did not instruct us upon nor clarify to us. As for what He clarified to us and informed us, He permitted it to us. What he forbade in this chapter returns to other than that of what He did not clarify to us nor permit us speech in, of what He, Mighty and Majestic, undertook and barred us from. We ask Allah for success."
"1161","Chapter 159: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about naming the newborn on its seventh day, and about his naming some newborns before that. 1030 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Quraysh ibn Anas narrated to us, he said: Ash'ath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The boy is held in pledge by his 'aqiqa - or he said: by an 'aqiqa - which is slaughtered on the seventh day, his head is shaved, and he is named. Quraysh said: Habib ibn al-Shahid informed us that Ibn Sirin told him to ask al-Hasan from whom he heard his hadith about the 'aqiqa; so he asked him and he said: I heard it from Samura. Abu Ja'far said: Some people went to the view that this whole hadith goes back entirely to Samura. We examined that and found it open to other than what they said, because Ibn Sirin only told Habib to ask al-Hasan from whom he heard his hadith about the 'aqiqa, so that was aimed at the 'aqiqa, not at the rest of what is in this hadith of Quraysh. So we sought that in other than this hadith, to establish his source from Samura: does it contain naming the newborn on its seventh day, so that would be his instruction to the people about that, or not?"
"1162","1031 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Qatada narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samura, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A boy is held in pledge by an 'aqiqa which is slaughtered for him on his seventh day, his head is shaved, and he is named. Abu Ja'far said: There was no mention in this hadith of the time of naming the newborn. Then we examined whether we find it in other than it from what was reported from Samura."
"1163","1032 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubada narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Aruba narrated to us, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Samura ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Every boy is held in pledge by his 'aqiqa, so it is slaughtered for him, he is named, and his head is shaved on the seventh day. Abu Ja'far said: In this was his naming on the seventh day, except that it is not strong in our hearts, because the one who reported it from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Aruba is Rawh ibn 'Ubada, and the hearing of Rawh from Sa'id was only after his confusion (ikhtilat). So we sought it from the narration of others whose hearing from him was before his confusion."
"1164","1033 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Ali and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, they said: Yazid - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb said: he is Ibn Zuray' - narrated to us, from Sa'id, he said: Qatada informed us, from al-Hasan, from Samura ibn Jundub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every boy is held in pledge by his 'aqiqa, slaughtered for him on his seventh day, his head is shaved, and he is named. Abu Ja'far said: By that we understood that all that is in the hadith of Bakkar, from Quraysh, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, has gone back entirely to Samura, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), through the narration of one against whose narration there is no criticism of hearing in a state of confusion nor anything else. Then we looked: was there reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that?"
"1165","1034 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tonight a boy was born to me, and I named him after my father Ibrahim."
"1166","1035 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salama Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, from Thabit, he said: Anas said: When Umm Sulaym gave birth to 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talha, Abu Talha said to me: O Anas, let him not taste anything until you take him in the morning to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he spent the night crying. In the morning I took him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him he said: Has Umm Sulaym given birth? I said: Yes. He sat down, and I came until I placed him in his lap. He called for an 'ajwa date from the 'ajwa of Medina, chewed it in his mouth until it dissolved, then spat it into his mouth, and the baby began to smack his lips. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Look at the love of the Ansar for dates! And he wiped his face and named him 'Abdullah."
"1167","1036 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: I took 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talha to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day he was born, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a cloak smearing tar on one of his camels. He said: Do you have any dates with you? I said: Yes. Then he fed them to him, and the baby smacked his lips. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The love of the Ansar is dates! And he named him 'Abdullah."
"1168","1037 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, that Umm Sulaym gave birth to her son 'Abdullah at night, and I disliked tahnik (chewing a date for him) until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do his tahnik. So in the morning I went with some 'ajwa dates and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was smearing tar on camels of his or branding them. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Umm Sulaym has given birth, and I disliked doing his tahnik until you do his tahnik. He said: Do you have anything with you? I said: 'Ajwa dates. He took some of those dates, chewed them and mixed them with his saliva, and fed them to him. The baby smacked his lips, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The love of the Ansar for dates. I said: Name him, O Messenger of Allah! He said: He is 'Abdullah. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported is the naming by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his son Ibrahim and of 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talha by these two names of theirs before their seventh day. So we examined that to know which is preferable of the two reports we mentioned in this chapter: naming the newborn on its seventh day, or doing that before its seventh."
"1169","Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn Burayda, from his father, who said: In the Jahiliyya, when a boy was born to us we slaughtered a sheep for him and smeared his head with its blood. Then in Islam, when a boy was born to us we slaughtered a sheep for him and smeared his head with saffron. Abu Ja'far said: By that we understood that what they used to do at the beginning of Islam on the seventh day of the newborn was like what they used to do in it in the Jahiliyya, and that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with his son Ibrahim and with 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talha, in naming them both before their seventh day and before slaughtering an 'aqiqa for each of them, was before the ruling that it be on its seventh day came later upon that and abrogated it. So it (the later) was preferable to what was before it that contradicts it from what we mentioned in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"1170","Chapter 160: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is slaughtered for the male newborn on its seventh day: is it one sheep or two sheep? 1038 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us that Qatada narrated to him, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the 'aqiqa for Hasan and Husayn with two rams."
"1171","1039 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrima, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the 'aqiqa for al-Hasan with one ram and for al-Husayn with one ram. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported is what indicates that what is slaughtered for the male newborn on its seventh day is one sheep, just as is slaughtered for the female. And there was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that: that two sheep are slaughtered for the male and one sheep for the girl."
"1172","1040 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, from his father, from Siba' ibn Thabit, who heard it from Umm Kurz al-Ka'biyya, who reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep. It does not harm you whether they are males or females."
"1173","1041 - And as Yunus and 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr, from 'Ata', from Habiba bint Maysara, from Umm Kurz al-Khuza'iyya, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"1174","1042 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn Ward al-Makki narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka saying: A boy was born to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and it was said to 'A'isha: O Mother of the Believers, do the 'aqiqa for him with a camel! She said: Allah forbid! Rather what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two equal sheep."
"1175","1043 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Siba' ibn Thabit, from Umm Kurz, that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying about the 'aqiqa: For the boy two sheep, and for the girl one sheep. Abu Ja'far said: And he did not mention in it Abu 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid."
"1176","1044 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Hafsa bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'isha, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us that the 'aqiqa for the boy be two sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"1177","1045 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from 'Ata' and Tawus and Mujahid, from Umm Kurz, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy two equal sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"1178","1046 - And as 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' narrated to me, from Umm 'Uthman bint Khuthaym, from Umm Kurz, that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being asked about the 'aqiqa, and he said: For the boy two equal sheep, and for the girl one sheep."
"1179","1047 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Yazid al-Kahili al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy two 'aqiqas, and for the girl one 'aqiqa. In what we reported in this second section is the difference between what is slaughtered for the male on its seventh day and what is slaughtered for the female on her seventh day: that two sheep are slaughtered for the male and one sheep for the female. Had we been left to our own opinions in that, there would be no difference between what is slaughtered for the boy and what is slaughtered for the girl in that, just as there is no difference between what is slaughtered for each of them in sacrifices, just as there is no difference between what is slaughtered for each of them in tamattu' and qiran, and in what is due from each of them in blood penalties for what befalls them in their ihram. But we were not left to that; we were referred to what we established from what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so that was preferable for us. And what we reported in the second section of this chapter was the worthiest thing to act upon, because it contains the addition over what we reported in the first section of it. So what we were commanded of the addition over what we were commanded in the first section is acted upon, and it remains upon what we were commanded in the first section, and we do not make what is in the second section of addition abrogated by what is in the first section until we establish that it is so in reality. And Allah we ask for success."
"1180","Chapter 161: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: 'And remove the harm from him' - meaning what is done with the newborn on its seventh day. 1048 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Qatada and Ayyub and Yunus and Hisham and Habib informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salman ibn 'Amir al-Dabbi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy there is an 'aqiqa, so shed blood for him, and remove the harm from him."
"1181","1049 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Salman ibn 'Amir al-Dabbi narrated to us, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: With the boy there is an 'aqiqa, so shed blood for him, and remove the harm from him. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported was the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the harm be removed from the newborn, and that is from what was difficult for those before us, among them Muhammad ibn Sirin: what is it? Until there was reported from him about that:"
"1182","1050 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Salman ibn 'Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the boy there is an 'aqiqa, so shed blood for him, and remove the harm from him. Muhammad said: I was keen to know what 'remove from him' is, but I found no one to inform me. Abu Ja'far said: Then we ourselves examined that harm which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding the newborn, to know what it is. We found in a hadith reported from 'A'isha on this meaning."
"1183","1051 - And it is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Yafi'i narrated to me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Amra, from 'A'isha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the 'aqiqa for Hasan and Husayn on the seventh day, named them both, and commanded that the harm be removed from his head - meaning from their two heads. I say that - and Allah knows best. Abu Ja'far said: By that we understood that the removal which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended is the removal from the head of the boy slaughtered for on that day of what is slaughtered for him in it. And we found in the hadith of Burayda reported from him what added in indication of what the intended removal in that is, as:"
"1184","Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Burayda, from his father, who said: In the Jahiliyya, when a boy was born to us we slaughtered a sheep for him and smeared his head with its blood. Then in Islam, when a boy was born to us we slaughtered a sheep for him and smeared his head with saffron. By that we understood that the harm commanded to be removed from the head of the newborn is the blood with which his head was smeared in the Jahiliyya - and Allah knows best."
"1185","1052 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Yazid ibn 'Abd al-Muzani, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The 'aqiqa is done for the boy, and his head is not to be touched with blood. In this hadith was an addition in uncovering what it is that is removed from the head of the newborn on its seventh day. Abu Ja'far said: And it is possible that the harm removed from his head is the shaving of the hair upon it, like what is intended in the saying of Allah Almighty: Whoever among you is sick or has harm of his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. He means by that those prevented from the House in the 'umra for which they set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in what we mentioned. And Him we ask for success."
"1186","Chapter 162: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'aqiqa: is it obligatory or optional? Abu Ja'far said: We reported in what preceded from us in these chapters on sacrifices that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The newborn is held in pledge by its 'aqiqa. And in that is what indicates the obligation of slaughtering it. And there was reported what confirms that. 1053 - What al-Hasan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Muthanna ibn Anas narrated to us, from Thumama ibn Anas, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did the 'aqiqa for himself after prophethood came to him."
"1187","1054 - And what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Muthanna ibn Anas ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: A man from the family of Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported of this was confirmation of its obligation. Then we looked: was there reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that or not?"
"1188","1055 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman, meaning al-Rahawi, narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the 'aqiqa, and he said: I do not like 'uquq (disobedience)! As if he disliked the name. He said: O Messenger of Allah, we only ask you about one of us to whom a child is born! He said: Whoever loves to offer a sacrifice for his child, let him offer it for him: for the boy two equal sheep, and for the girl one sheep. Dawud said: I asked Zayd ibn Aslam about the two equal ones; he said: Two similar sheep slaughtered together."
"1189","1056 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man of Banu Damra, from a man of his people who asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage, saying: What do you think about the 'aqiqa? He said: I do not like 'uquq! Whoever has a child born to him and loves to offer a sacrifice for him, let him do so."
"1190","1057 - 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man of Banu Damra reporting from his father or from his uncle: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the 'aqiqa, and he said: I do not like 'uquq! But whoever loves to offer a sacrifice for his child, let him do so. Abu Ja'far said: What is in these two hadiths indicates that its matter was referred to choice, due to his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever has a newborn born to him and wants or loves to offer a sacrifice for him, let him do so. And what we reported before that in confirming its matter was according to what it was upon in the Jahiliyya; then Islam came and it was retained upon what it was upon in the Jahiliyya. By that we understood that what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that contradicted that came later upon it and abrogated it. And Allah we ask for success."
"1191","Chapter 163: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the 'atira, and is it the Rajabiyya or not? Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in his dictation to them, he said: And a slaughter that was in the Jahiliyya: they used to slaughter in Rajab a sheep, and it is the Rajabiyya. The people of the house would slaughter it, then eat, cook, and feed. And the 'atira was when a she-camel or sheep of a man gave birth, he would slaughter the first offspring born to him, then eat and feed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the 'atira and said: That he leave it until it becomes a shughzub is better for him than slaughtering it with its flesh sticking to its fur, and you overturn your vessel and give your she-camel to it. And I heard al-Muzani saying: al-Shafi'i said: The 'atira is the Rajabiyya, a sacrifice by which the people of the Jahiliyya sought blessing, slaughtering it in Rajab. In what we reported from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, the 'atira is other than the Rajabiyya, and in what we reported from al-Shafi'i, the 'atira is the Rajabiyya. Since they differed in that, we sought its reality in the reports transmitted about it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to establish thereby the correct of these two sayings said about it."
"1192","1058 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-'Anbari narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Awn, he said: Abu Ramla narrated to me, from Mikhnaf ibn Sulaym, who said: While we were standing with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at 'Arafa, he said: O people, upon the people of every house in every year is an adhha (sacrifice) and an 'atira. Do you know what the 'atira is? He said: I do not know what their reply to him was. He said: It is what the people call the Rajabiyya."
"1193","1059 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Abu Ramla al-Kindi, from Mikhnaf ibn Sulaym, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and we came to him in the delegation of Ghamid - and he said: Upon every people of a house in every year is an adhha and an 'atira. He said: We said: What is the 'atira? He said: The Rajabiyya. Abu Ja'far said: By this hadith we understood that the 'atira is the Rajabiyya, and we found in this hadith what indicates its obligation like the obligation of the sacrifice. So we needed to establish what was reported in other than this hadith, and the application of any of the scholars of it."
"1194","1060 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awana narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Waki' ibn 'Udus, from his uncle Abu Razin, and he is Laqit ibn 'Amir, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: We used to slaughter sacrifices in Rajab and feed whoever came to us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No harm. Waki' said: I will never leave it."
"1195","'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, that Muhammad ibn Sirin used to do the 'atira. Mu'adh said: And Ibn 'Awn used to do the 'atira. Mu'adh said: The 'atira is a sheep slaughtered in Rajab. Abu Ja'far said: Then we looked: was there reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what abrogates that or not?"
"1196","1061 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No fara'a, and no 'atira. Sufyan said, meaning: in Islam. Then al-Zuhri said to us: The fara'a is the first offspring, and the 'atira is a sheep they used to slaughter in Rajab."
"1197","1062 - Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to me, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no 'atira in Islam, and no fara'. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the negation of the 'atira, and its negation mentioned in it may mean negation of obligation, and that does not prevent doing an act in which there is no disobedience. And there is no contradiction to what is in this hadith, and it may bear the opposite of that. So we examined that."
"1198","1063 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid narrating, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu al-Malih, from Nubaysha, who said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, we used to do an 'atira in Rajab, so what do you command us? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter for Allah Almighty in whatever month it may be, and do good to Allah and feed. I heard al-Muzani saying: 'And do good to Allah' or 'prefer Allah' - the doubt is from al-Muzani."
"1199","1064 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Nubaysha al-Hudhali, who said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, we used to do an 'atira of ours in the Jahiliyya, so what do you command us? He said: Slaughter for Allah Almighty in whatever month it may be, and do good to Allah Almighty and feed. He said: And I said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to do a fara' of ours in the Jahiliyya, so what do you command us? He said: In every grazing herd there is a fara' which your livestock rears; when it is complete you slaughter it and give its meat in charity. He said: I think he said: upon the wayfarer; for that is better. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is what we understood thereby that the matter of the 'atira was referred to choice and negation of obligation, and that it is piety: whoever takes it has done well, and whoever dislikes it is not harmed."
"1200","1065 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Utba ibn 'Abd al-Malik al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Zurara ibn Karim ibn al-Harith ibn 'Amr al-Sahmi narrated to me, from his grandfather, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Mina and 'Arafat, with people crowded around him. A man asked him about the 'atira, and he said: Whoever wishes may do the 'atira, and whoever wishes may not do the 'atira; and whoever wishes may do the fara', and whoever wishes may not do the fara'. And he said: For sheep, its sacrifice. And he gestured with his index finger, bending its tip slightly."
